new const g_FamilyLineDefs[1570][E_FAMILY_LINE_DEF] = {
	{0, "Look at the low strip, the rye's standing thin. The rain sat on it all spring."},
	{2, "Then we'll have less bread and more pottage. We've done it before."},
	{1, "When I was a girl thin rye meant thin soup till Easter, and we lived."},
	{3, "Matej threw a stone at the potter's cat again."},
	{5, "The cat looked at me first. It did!"},
	{2, "Looked at you! Matej, the potter's boy came to our door about this."},
	{4, "He hit the cat and he missed. He's bad at both."},
	{1, "We had three cows once, and nobody went hungry."},
	{0, "In your day the count wasn't counting them, mother."},
	{1, "Counting cows, what an idea. A cow is a cow."},
	{2, "Eat your pottage, Ludmila. The cows are gone, God rest them."},
	{2, "Vojta, you'll take the seed sacks to the strip after dinner."},
	{3, "They're heavy, Mother. Can Bara help?"},
	{4, "I'm carrying the hen. She'd be lonely."},
	{0, "The hen stays home. We've seed to get in."},
	{3, "I saw a wolf at the edge of the wood. A big grey one."},
	{0, "You saw a dog."},
	{5, "It had a long tail and eyes like candles!"},
	{1, "Wolves come down when the leaves fall. Bring the hens in."},
	{0, "If the {neighbour} lends us their ox we'll have the strip turned by Friday."},
	{2, "They'll want something for it. They always do."},
	{0, "A day's labour on their hay. That's fair."},
	{1, "Neighbours lent oxen for a smile when I was young."},
	{4, "Can I keep the hen that won't lay? She follows me everywhere."},
	{2, "A hen that won't lay goes in the pot, Bara."},
	{4, "She'd taste bad. She's sad."},
	{0, "Hens aren't sad. Hens are stupid."},
	{0, "The reeve's clerk counted our sheaves twice. He trusts nobody."},
	{2, "He counts for the count. It's his work."},
	{0, "It's an insult. As if a man like me would hide one sheaf."},
	{1, "Everybody hides one sheaf, Jakub. Even the priest."},
	{2, "Last candle end. Eat quickly, before it's gone."},
	{5, "I'm not tired. I want to stay up."},
	{1, "Say your prayers before you sleep. The nights are long."},
	{0, "When the candle's out we go to bed. We can't buy light."},
	{0, "Last log tonight. The woodpile's down to twigs."},
	{2, "The children's feet were like ice today."},
	{3, "I'll go to the forest tomorrow, Father."},
	{0, "Not you. The forester would run you off. My back is bad, but I'll go."},
	{2, "The goose is fat enough. We'll keep it for the feast."},
	{5, "Can I name her? Her name is Margaret."},
	{1, "Never name what you eat, boy."},
	{3, "He named the last one too. He cried at dinner."},
	{1, "My husband died the winter the river froze to the bottom."},
	{2, "We know, Ludmila. You tell it every year."},
	{1, "And I'll tell it till I join him. He was a good man."},
	{0, "He was. He taught me to sharpen a scythe."},
	{6, "The warp snapped twice today. This damp is in the wool."},
	{7, "Then hang it by the fire tonight."},
	{6, "And risk a spark? One fleck and the whole loom goes up."},
	{8, "I'll sit by it with a bucket, Father."},
	{8, "I spun four skeins. Mother says it's lumpy, but it's done."},
	{7, "It's lumpy, Johanka. Spin it again tomorrow."},
	{8, "But it's strong! Try to break it."},
	{6, "It's strong enough. Strong and ugly. We'll sell it for sacking."},
	{6, "The merchant wants the blue cloth by the end of the season. Blue!"},
	{7, "The dyer sisters in the other village have woad. Ask them."},
	{6, "They'll want a bolt for a pot of woad. Robbery."},
	{7, "Then we pay in cloth and he pays in coin. That's trade."},
	{6, "A good bolt of cloth is worth more than a cow. People forget that."},
	{7, "Nobody forgets, Pavel. They just don't have the coin."},
	{8, "A cow gives milk, though."},
	{6, "A cow gives milk and dies. Cloth gives warmth for twenty years."},
	{8, "The crofter's boy keeps staring at me at mass."},
	{7, "Then sit on the other side of the church."},
	{8, "I like him a little, Mother. Not much."},
	{6, "A little is too much. He has no coin and no roof."},
	{6, "Pass the cheese. My hands ache from the shuttle."},
	{7, "Soak them in warm water. I'll fetch the basin."},
	{6, "Forty years of throwing a shuttle. They've earned their ache."},
	{8, "I'll thread tomorrow, Father. You rest."},
	{6, "The shearers come soon. I hope the fleece isn't full of burrs."},
	{7, "Last year it was full of burrs and twigs."},
	{8, "I picked them out for three days. My fingers bled."},
	{6, "That's why it's called wool work, girl."},
	{7, "The loom's creaking louder every night."},
	{6, "It needs grease. I've none."},
	{8, "I'll rub it with a candle end."},
	{6, "Wax is dear. Use the mutton fat in the crock."},
	{7, "The tailor's wife wants her skein by Sunday."},
	{8, "I'll bring it. She's kind to me."},
	{6, "She pays on time. That's kind enough."},
	{7, "And she gave you a honey cake once."},
	{6, "Three bolts to the market, and one is for the count's steward."},
	{7, "Don't sell the steward's bolt cheap because he asks nicely."},
	{6, "He never asks nicely."},
	{8, "Can I come? I'd like to see the fair."},
	{9, "The count's tax rolls don't add up. Somebody's been hiding sheaves."},
	{10, "Not at the table, Mikulas."},
	{9, "Then where? They're due before the season ends."},
	{11, "Maybe somebody counted wrong, Father."},
	{9, "The tailor has my new coat. Black, lined."},
	{10, "You'll pay him promptly this time, Mikulas."},
	{9, "I'll pay him when the count pays me."},
	{12, "You'll look like the priest, Father."},
	{12, "The merchant's son brought me a ribbon from the fair."},
	{10, "Give it back. We don't owe that family anything."},
	{12, "It's only a ribbon. It's red."},
	{11, "Red is a bold colour, Marketa."},
	{9, "Grace was short today, Hedvika. The Lord likes it longer at a table like this."},
	{10, "It was as long as always."},
	{11, "I counted. It was eleven words."},
	{9, "Eleven. A beggar's blessing."},
	{11, "Father, may I go with the guards on the night watch?"},
	{9, "You'll learn your letters first. Guards can't read the warrants they carry."},
	{11, "I can read already."},
	{10, "You can read. You can't stand still for an hour."},
	{9, "The ledger can wait till morning. My eyes are tired."},
	{10, "It's a short candle. We'll go up soon."},
	{12, "Father, you've counted that sack three times."},
	{9, "Because it came out different each time."},
	{11, "The smith says the new tax is robbery."},
	{9, "The smith says a lot of things. Write them down and he'd hang."},
	{10, "Mikulas, the boy hears you."},
	{9, "Then he learns how a village works."},
	{9, "A sheet of parchment costs more than a hen."},
	{10, "Then use less."},
	{9, "Less? I've three villages to write."},
	{11, "I could scrape the old ones, Father, and write again."},
	{12, "The fair is in three weeks. May I go?"},
	{10, "With your brother, and home before dark."},
	{11, "I don't want to carry her ribbons."},
	{9, "You'll carry them and be grateful."},
	{10, "A quiet evening. I could almost enjoy it."},
	{9, "Don't say it so loud. You'll scare it off."},
	{11, "Can I read by the candle?"},
	{10, "One page, Ota. Then bed."},
	{9, "The count's cellar owes the merchant for the wine from the feast."},
	{10, "And we owe him a favour for the ribbon business."},
	{9, "Let the count pay first, then favours."},
	{12, "Nobody pays anybody in this village."},
	{13, "Up before Prime again tomorrow. The oven won't light itself."},
	{14, "Then go to bed, Stepan."},
	{15, "I'll wake you, master."},
	{13, "You'll wake me and I'll curse you. Fair enough."},
	{15, "I burnt the second batch. The rings went black on the bottom."},
	{13, "Then you'll eat the black ones."},
	{15, "They taste like charcoal, master."},
	{14, "They taste like a lesson, Kuba."},
	{14, "Half the village owes us for bread. Write it down, Stepan."},
	{13, "I can't write, woman. That's why I remember."},
	{14, "You remember what suits you."},
	{15, "I could learn letters from the priest."},
	{15, "The miller's flour has grit in it again."},
	{13, "The miller's flour always has grit in it. Sift it twice."},
	{14, "The customers complain of sore teeth."},
	{13, "Then they chew carefully."},
	{14, "Eat while it's warm. Nothing's worse than cold bread at your own table."},
	{13, "Nothing's worse than burnt bread at your own table."},
	{15, "I said I was sorry."},
	{14, "Eat, Kuba. Nobody's shouting."},
	{13, "Lent's coming. Fewer loaves, plainer dough."},
	{14, "And the priest will bless them."},
	{15, "Can I make the little crosses on the top?"},
	{13, "Cut them straight or I'll cut you."},
	{13, "Four bundles tomorrow. The oven eats firewood like a horse eats oats."},
	{14, "We've three bundles left."},
	{15, "I can fetch some at dawn."},
	{13, "You'll fall asleep on the path."},
	{14, "The crofter's wife sent cheese for the bread we gave them. Good people."},
	{13, "Their little one was ill. We'd give bread again."},
	{15, "The cheese is lovely. A bit sharp, though."},
	{14, "Sharp is how cheese should be."},
	{15, "The dough didn't rise today."},
	{13, "You forgot to keep it warm."},
	{14, "Or it was the cold draft from the door."},
	{13, "Or the devil. It's always the devil, the priest says."},
	{14, "We're out of salt for the dough."},
	{13, "Then the bread will taste of nothing."},
	{15, "I can ask the merchant, but he's dear."},
	{14, "Dear is better than flat."},
	{16, "No woman at this table since your mother died, and it shows. Who cooked this?"},
	{18, "I did. It's stew."},
	{17, "It's something."},
	{16, "It's hot. That's all a stew needs to be."},
	{16, "The mason wants new scaffold poles before the season is out."},
	{17, "We've no seasoned oak left. Green wood will warp."},
	{16, "Then we buy from the woodcutter. He charges double."},
	{18, "Ask the forester. He owes us for the gate."},
	{18, "The innkeeper's table has a wobble. He asked if we'd fix it for ale."},
	{16, "Fix it for coin. Ale doesn't feed anyone."},
	{17, "Ale feeds Father plenty."},
	{16, "Watch your mouth, Lukas."},
	{17, "Father, I want to take the cooper's daughter to the fair."},
	{16, "The cooper has no daughter. He has a son and a father."},
	{17, "The other cooper. The one in the next village."},
	{18, "He means the pretty one."},
	{18, "The house is quiet since Mother died."},
	{16, "It is. Too quiet."},
	{17, "I still set her stool by the hearth."},
	{16, "Leave it. It does no harm."},
	{16, "The beam for the chapel roof has a split."},
	{17, "It dried too fast in the sun."},
	{16, "I told you to stack it in the shade."},
	{18, "He stacked it by the wall, Father. The wall was sunny."},
	{18, "There's sawdust in the soup again."},
	{17, "That's from your hair, Vit."},
	{16, "Both of you shake out your clothes before you sit."},
	{18, "It's flavour, Father."},
	{16, "My fingers can't hold a chisel in this cold."},
	{17, "We can work indoors on the chests."},
	{16, "Chests need nails. We're nearly out."},
	{18, "I'll count them. Four. Maybe three."},
	{17, "I lost a pfennig at the inn."},
	{16, "How many times have I said?"},
	{17, "Only the one."},
	{18, "He lost four, Father. I saw."},
	{16, "The old oak at the crossroads fell in the storm."},
	{18, "It was the oldest tree in the parish."},
	{17, "Think of the planks!"},
	{16, "Think of the count's men, who'll say it's theirs."},
	{19, "Two travellers left without paying last night. Two!"},
	{20, "You were asleep at the counter, Bohus."},
	{19, "I was resting my eyes."},
	{21, "You snored. I heard it from the loft."},
	{22, "The brewer's ale is sour this week. People are complaining."},
	{19, "Then we tell them it's a new kind."},
	{20, "Bohus, you'll not cheat our own guests."},
	{19, "A new kind of sour. Nobody's had it before."},
	{21, "A guard asked me who stays in the room upstairs. I didn't tell him."},
	{20, "Good girl. What happens in our rooms stays in our rooms."},
	{22, "Who is it, anyway?"},
	{19, "A man who pays in advance. That's all you need to know."},
	{19, "The dice players are good for business. They drink while they lose."},
	{22, "The old regular cheats at dice. Everyone knows it."},
	{19, "Everyone knows it and everyone still plays him."},
	{20, "Because he buys a round when he wins."},
	{19, "The taproom's cold as a tomb tonight."},
	{20, "Light the second fire, Bohus."},
	{19, "Firewood costs coin."},
	{20, "Customers cost more when they leave."},
	{19, "Tomorrow's a fast day. We'll need fish."},
	{20, "Two fish, from the river. No more."},
	{22, "I'll cook them with leeks."},
	{19, "Not with the leeks. Plain, with salt."},
	{22, "The old regular fell asleep in the soup."},
	{19, "He does that every harvest."},
	{21, "I wiped his face. He didn't wake."},
	{20, "He's lonely. Be kind to him."},
	{19, "The merchant brought three casks of southern wine."},
	{20, "And a bill for each."},
	{22, "Can I taste it?"},
	{19, "When you're married, boy."},
	{20, "We burn through candles in the taproom."},
	{19, "Nobody wants to drink in a dark taproom."},
	{21, "I can make rushlights from grease."},
	{20, "They stink. People would leave."},
	{19, "The toll bar on the old road is costing me guests."},
	{20, "Fewer travellers, fewer beds."},
	{22, "But more rumours. The merchants talk of it."},
	{19, "Rumours don't pay for ale."},
	{23, "Half the pots cracked in the kiln. I put it down to wet clay and the cold night."},
	{24, "Mother would have known what went wrong."},
	{23, "Your mother knew a lot of things. Eat your pottage."},
	{24, "I only said."},
	{24, "The innkeeper wants twenty mugs. Twenty!"},
	{23, "Then we'll have coin for the season for once."},
	{24, "Can I make the handles?"},
	{23, "You can make them straight. That would be a start."},
	{24, "The clay pit by the stream is nearly dug out."},
	{23, "There's more past the bend. Your grandfather found it."},
	{24, "It's wet there. I sank to my knees."},
	{23, "Clay is wet. That's how you know it's clay."},
	{23, "Leave the wheel alone tonight. A tired hand throws a crooked pot."},
	{24, "I'm not tired, Father."},
	{23, "You've yawned six times."},
	{24, "Four. And two were hunger."},
	{24, "Mother put ash in the glaze, didn't she?"},
	{23, "Not ash. Powdered lead, from a trader."},
	{24, "Where is the trader?"},
	{23, "Gone to the south. I haven't seen him in two years."},
	{23, "The kiln wants charcoal. Two baskets."},
	{24, "The burner's late with the delivery."},
	{23, "The burner is always late."},
	{24, "He sleeps by the clamp, they say."},
	{24, "The cat had kittens in the straw by the kiln."},
	{23, "Five?"},
	{24, "Four. One's the grey one."},
	{23, "Keep the grey one. Drown the rest."},
	{23, "Pottage again."},
	{24, "It's what there is, Father."},
	{23, "A potter eats from his own pots, and it's still pottage."},
	{24, "With a bit of leek today."},
	{23, "This wheel was my father's."},
	{24, "It wobbles, Father."},
	{23, "It wobbles because it's old. Like me."},
	{24, "Can we make a new one?"},
	{24, "There's a winter fair at the market."},
	{23, "We'll take a dozen pots."},
	{24, "Can I mind the stall?"},
	{23, "You'll mind it and not sell cheap."},
	{25, "The brown cow won't eat. She stands in the byre and stares at the wall."},
	{26, "Give her warm mash. She did this last winter too."},
	{25, "Last winter it was the cold. Now?"},
	{28, "Maybe she's sad."},
	{27, "I saw a wolf at the edge of the wood. A big grey one."},
	{25, "You saw a dog. But we'll pen the sheep tonight all the same."},
	{28, "What if it comes for the calf?"},
	{26, "Then your father will meet it with the staff."},
	{28, "The calf licked my face. It's so rough!"},
	{26, "Wash before you eat, then."},
	{28, "It tickled! I laughed and laughed."},
	{27, "She smells of byre, Mother."},
	{25, "Milk's good this week. We'll take cheese to the market."},
	{26, "If the count's men leave us any."},
	{27, "They took a whole wheel last time."},
	{25, "They took two. I counted."},
	{26, "The hay won't last till Easter."},
	{25, "We'll cut the straw thin."},
	{27, "The cows will starve."},
	{25, "They won't. I'll find oats."},
	{26, "Take milk to the old couple by the hamlet tomorrow."},
	{25, "They can't walk to us any more."},
	{27, "Can I go?"},
	{26, "You'll go, and you won't stay to gossip."},
	{27, "The goat ate the thatch above the door."},
	{25, "Again?"},
	{28, "She says it tastes good."},
	{26, "She says nothing. She's a goat."},
	{25, "I'll sleep in the fold tonight. The ewes are close."},
	{26, "Take the blanket. The dew is cold."},
	{27, "Can I come?"},
	{25, "You'll keep your mother company."},
	{26, "The priest blessed the flock at Easter."},
	{25, "He blessed the sheep. He forgot the cows."},
	{28, "Does God mind?"},
	{26, "God doesn't forget anyone, Veronika."},
	{25, "The count's men took three lambs for the feast."},
	{26, "And a promise of coin."},
	{25, "A promise doesn't feed children."},
	{27, "When will they pay us?"},
	{29, "The bees swarmed again. Third time this summer."},
	{30, "They don't like the smoke from the smith's yard."},
	{29, "Nobody likes the smoke from the smith's yard."},
	{30, "I'll bang the pan and bring them down."},
	{30, "The apples on the south tree have the worm."},
	{29, "Then that tree goes into the cider."},
	{30, "The worm goes into the cider too."},
	{29, "A bit of worm never harmed a man."},
	{30, "Forty years we've sat at this table, Vaclav."},
	{29, "And the table's held up better than the both of us."},
	{30, "I remember the day you carved it."},
	{29, "I carved it badly. Look at the leg."},
	{30, "Eat your egg. The hens worked hard for it."},
	{29, "The brown one works. The speckled one sulks."},
	{30, "She'll lay when the sun returns."},
	{29, "She'll lay when she likes."},
	{29, "The jars need sealing with wax before the frost."},
	{30, "We've scraped the old wax to the last."},
	{29, "A cake of wax would do."},
	{30, "Ask the beekeeper. Oh, that's you."},
	{30, "A frost last night. The blossom's brown."},
	{29, "Half of it. The north trees held."},
	{30, "Half is half."},
	{29, "We'll have fewer apples and sweeter."},
	{30, "The smith's chimney smokes in our windows."},
	{29, "Then he'll hear of it."},
	{30, "You'll be polite. He's a big man."},
	{29, "I'm a small one. I'm always polite."},
	{29, "My knees ache. It'll snow tomorrow."},
	{30, "You say that every night."},
	{29, "And every year I'm right once."},
	{30, "Go to bed. I'll bank the fire."},
	{30, "The brewer wants our apples for his cider."},
	{29, "He'll pay a jug. A jug!"},
	{30, "A jug is better than nothing."},
	{29, "A jug is better than being cheated."},
	{30, "The cat is sleeping by the hearth again."},
	{29, "She's earned it. Fourteen winters."},
	{30, "She caught a mouse yesterday. Her last, I think."},
	{29, "Don't say that."},
	{31, "The castle wall has a crack you could put your fist in."},
	{33, "The count's steward says it's been there since his grandfather."},
	{31, "Then it's been getting worse since his grandfather."},
	{32, "Will you tell him so, Dietrich?"},
	{32, "Will they pay this time? They didn't pay for the chapel steps."},
	{31, "They paid in promises. I can't build with promises."},
	{33, "The steward says the harvest will fill the coffers."},
	{31, "He said that last harvest."},
	{33, "I'll have my own mark soon. My own templates."},
	{31, "When you can cut a clean arch, Ulrich. Not before."},
	{33, "I cut one last week."},
	{31, "You cut a lopsided one. The priest laughed."},
	{32, "There's stone dust in the bread again. Shake your clothes outside."},
	{31, "A bit of stone does a man good."},
	{32, "It breaks the teeth."},
	{33, "I've only two left, mistress."},
	{31, "The quarry's low on good stone."},
	{33, "The quarrymen took the best for the count."},
	{31, "The count's best is our worst."},
	{32, "Eat, you two, and stop grumbling."},
	{31, "I need four cut blocks for the castle job."},
	{32, "And a few coin for the roof."},
	{33, "I can cut them. It'll take a week."},
	{31, "A week we haven't got."},
	{32, "The chapel steps are worn through."},
	{31, "We built them, remember? And weren't paid."},
	{33, "Shall I mend them on my own?"},
	{31, "You'll mend them and I'll not take a pfennig."},
	{31, "My back's like a board tonight."},
	{32, "Sit by the fire. I'll rub it."},
	{33, "I'll carry the tools tomorrow."},
	{31, "You'll carry nothing. You'll drop it."},
	{33, "The master has ears like a hare."},
	{31, "I hear every whisper."},
	{32, "You hear the dust and the gossip."},
	{31, "Both come in handy."},
	{31, "The new arch over the gate needs a keystone."},
	{33, "I'll cut it. I've the mark."},
	{31, "You've no mark. You've a hammer."},
	{32, "He's a good lad, Dietrich."},
	{34, "Someone's been felling oak in the count's wood. Fresh stumps by the stream."},
	{35, "Wasn't me. I burn pine."},
	{34, "I know it wasn't you. You can't swing an axe straight."},
	{35, "I swing it fine. The tree moves."},
	{35, "The clamp ran hot last night. Half of it's ash now."},
	{34, "You fell asleep again."},
	{35, "A man has to sleep sometime."},
	{34, "Not when the clamp is burning."},
	{34, "Found big boar tracks by the south clearing."},
	{35, "Stay out of there at dusk."},
	{34, "I mean it. A boar will gut a man."},
	{35, "I've the scar to prove it."},
	{35, "Pass the sausage before it's all gone, brother."},
	{34, "There's only one left."},
	{35, "Then I'll take the half."},
	{34, "You'll take the end and be glad."},
	{34, "My rope's frayed through. I nearly dropped a log on my foot."},
	{35, "I told you."},
	{34, "You told me nothing."},
	{35, "I told the rope."},
	{34, "The shed roof is leaking."},
	{35, "I'll sleep under the logs."},
	{34, "You'll sleep under the roof. We'll mend it."},
	{35, "With what? Spit?"},
	{34, "The count says no man cuts without his leave."},
	{35, "And we cut with his leave."},
	{34, "With the steward's leave, which isn't the same."},
	{35, "It's near enough."},
	{35, "The clamp needs kindling to start. Six bundles."},
	{34, "Gather it yourself."},
	{35, "My back won't bend."},
	{34, "It bends fine for the sausage."},
	{35, "I heard a wolf by the clamp last night."},
	{34, "A wolf or an owl."},
	{35, "A wolf. I know the difference."},
	{34, "You've never been right yet."},
	{35, "You snore like a bear."},
	{34, "You smell like a clamp."},
	{35, "That's honest work."},
	{34, "So is snoring."},
	{36, "The reeve's clerk wants a new black coat, lined, by Sunday."},
	{37, "He'll pay late, like last time."},
	{36, "He'll pay late, but he'll pay."},
	{37, "Late is a long way from paid."},
	{37, "My hands are blue to the wrist from the dye pot."},
	{36, "Blue sells. Nobody pays extra for brown."},
	{37, "I'd take brown over blue hands."},
	{36, "Scrub with ash. It helps."},
	{37, "The weaver's daughter brought the wrong skein again."},
	{36, "Be kind to her. Her father works her hard."},
	{37, "It was green. I asked for red."},
	{36, "Green's a good colour too."},
	{37, "Pass the bread, Matous. And keep your needles off the table."},
	{36, "I only had one."},
	{37, "It's in the loaf."},
	{36, "Then somebody's lucky."},
	{36, "I need linen for the clerk's coat lining. Two lengths."},
	{37, "The weaver has some."},
	{36, "The weaver has what the weaver chooses."},
	{37, "Then pay him with a coat."},
	{37, "Three gowns for the fair, and no cloth."},
	{36, "The merchant has cloth. At a price."},
	{37, "A price is a price."},
	{36, "Then we make two gowns and call it fashion."},
	{36, "The priest wants a new cassock."},
	{37, "He wants it cheap."},
	{36, "He wants it blessed. I'll bless it with stitches."},
	{37, "Don't blaspheme at table."},
	{36, "My fingers are stiff tonight."},
	{37, "Warm them at the pot."},
	{36, "I can't thread the needle."},
	{37, "Give it here. I'll thread it."},
	{37, "The smith's apprentice tore his shirt on the anvil."},
	{36, "Again?"},
	{37, "He's careless. But he's a good boy."},
	{36, "Mend it, charge a pfennig."},
	{36, "The merchant's wife haggled me down to nothing."},
	{37, "She haggles over buttons."},
	{36, "She haggled over thread."},
	{37, "She's careful with money. I'll give her that."},
	{38, "Salt's up a third since the toll bar went up on the old road."},
	{39, "Then we sell it up a third. That's how it works, Konrad."},
	{38, "And the customers go to the next town."},
	{39, "Then they pay the toll too."},
	{40, "A trader from the south has spices. Real pepper."},
	{38, "Real pepper and fake weights, I'll wager."},
	{40, "He let me taste it. It burned like fire."},
	{39, "Then it's real. Fake pepper only warms."},
	{41, "The reeve's clerk's wife sent a note. She's angry about something."},
	{39, "She's always angry about something."},
	{38, "It's about the ribbon, I'd say."},
	{40, "I only gave her daughter a ribbon, Father!"},
	{38, "The count owes us for the wine at the feast. Again."},
	{39, "Six casks, and no coin."},
	{38, "Next year we'll send only vinegar."},
	{41, "He'd never know, Father."},
	{40, "Father, let me take the cart to the fair this spring."},
	{38, "When you can tell good cloth from bad by touch alone."},
	{40, "I can already."},
	{38, "Close your eyes. Which is wool, which is linen?"},
	{38, "The ledger doesn't balance. A sack of wheat is gone."},
	{39, "You sold it to the baker on Thursday."},
	{38, "I did? Then where is the coin?"},
	{39, "In the box, under the bed."},
	{41, "Can I sell ribbons at the fair?"},
	{39, "A girl at a stall invites trouble."},
	{41, "I'll shout 'Fine ribbons!' and smile."},
	{38, "She'd sell twice as many as Albrecht."},
	{39, "Elbows off the table, Albrecht."},
	{40, "Father puts his on."},
	{38, "I'm the head of the house."},
	{39, "You're the head of nothing, Konrad. Elbows."},
	{38, "A cold wind and a warm hearth. What more could a man want?"},
	{39, "A bit less cold, a bit more warm."},
	{41, "I'd like a fur collar."},
	{38, "I'd like the count's payment."},
	{38, "A cart came from the south, with spice and pepper."},
	{40, "And rumours of bandits near the ford."},
	{39, "Konrad, we'll not send the cart this month."},
	{38, "Gertruda, bandits are always rumours."},
	{42, "In my day a barrel held for twenty years."},
	{43, "In your day you didn't fill them with the brewer's sour ale."},
	{42, "Ale doesn't rot a good barrel."},
	{43, "Sour ale does."},
	{42, "Your hoops are too loose. I can see the light through them."},
	{43, "That's the window, father."},
	{42, "Don't sass me at my own table."},
	{43, "It's my table too."},
	{43, "The brewer wants six barrels before the harvest."},
	{42, "Then stop eating and start working."},
	{43, "I'm eating because I'm working."},
	{42, "We ate afterwards, boy."},
	{43, "Your cough's worse this winter. I'll fetch the herb widow."},
	{42, "I need no widow."},
	{43, "You need rest and herbs."},
	{42, "I need a cooper who listens."},
	{43, "The brewer swears his ale isn't sour."},
	{42, "It's sour as vinegar."},
	{43, "It's my barrels he blames."},
	{42, "Then your barrels are to blame."},
	{42, "Leave my chair alone. It fits me."},
	{43, "It's broken, father."},
	{42, "It's not broken. It leans."},
	{43, "It leans into the fire."},
	{43, "The merchant wants twelve small casks for wine."},
	{42, "Small casks. A fool's job."},
	{43, "Small casks pay as well as big ones."},
	{42, "We made them big."},
	{42, "I made a barrel for the old count once."},
	{43, "You've told us, father."},
	{42, "He gave me a silver pfennig and a pat on the head."},
	{43, "He gave you a pat and kept the barrel."},
	{42, "My chest hurts in the cold."},
	{43, "I'll gather herbs."},
	{42, "You've never gathered herbs in your life."},
	{43, "I'll learn."},
	{43, "A barrel's just wood and patience."},
	{42, "And a good eye."},
	{43, "And a good father who shouts."},
	{42, "I don't shout. I instruct."},
	{44, "The innkeeper says the ale's sour. The innkeeper says a lot of things."},
	{45, "It was sour, Jiri. You left the mash too long."},
	{44, "Long mash makes strong ale."},
	{45, "Strong and sour."},
	{46, "Jana drank from the barrel when you weren't looking."},
	{47, "I did not!"},
	{46, "You did. You hiccuped all morning."},
	{45, "Both of you, eat. No more ale till you're married."},
	{44, "We need barley. The miller's holding it back for a better price."},
	{45, "Then go round him to the farmers. They'll sell for less."},
	{44, "The farmers sell to the miller."},
	{45, "Then find the ones who don't."},
	{44, "To the next barrel, then. May it be sweet."},
	{45, "May you not leave it too long."},
	{46, "Can I stir it?"},
	{44, "When you've grown an arm like a post."},
	{45, "Hops! I'm out of hops."},
	{44, "Then the ale will taste like bread."},
	{45, "Bread ale. A fine thing for the children."},
	{44, "Don't joke. Hops keep it from souring."},
	{44, "The innkeeper's daughter will marry in the autumn."},
	{45, "We must brew a hundred jugs."},
	{44, "A hundred? We've never made so many."},
	{45, "Then we'll make a hundred and one."},
	{44, "The orchard keeper's wife sent apples."},
	{45, "Apples for a jug of cider. A fair swap."},
	{44, "Her cider's better than mine."},
	{45, "Don't say it where the innkeeper can hear."},
	{44, "The mash won't warm in this cold."},
	{45, "Then bring it by the fire."},
	{44, "The fire's low. We've no wood."},
	{45, "Then we'll drink the ale warm."},
	{44, "One barrel is over-full. We can't ship it."},
	{45, "Then we drink it."},
	{44, "I'd drink it gladly, wife."},
	{45, "I'd drink half. You'd drink the rest."},
	{47, "We swapped places and nobody noticed."},
	{46, "Father thought I was Jana."},
	{44, "I did not."},
	{45, "He called you Hana twice."},
	{48, "The count's men brought in twelve spears to mend. Twelve. And they'll pay in thanks."},
	{49, "Thanks don't buy coal, Vojtech."},
	{50, "I can straighten the shafts, master."},
	{48, "You can straighten your face first."},
	{50, "I quenched the axe head too early. It cracked."},
	{48, "Then you'll forge another and I'll watch you do it."},
	{50, "Can I use the good iron?"},
	{48, "You'll use the poor iron and learn."},
	{49, "Your hands are burnt again, Hynek. Let me see."},
	{50, "It's nothing, mistress."},
	{49, "It's a blister the size of a coin."},
	{48, "A smith without scars is a liar."},
	{48, "The new tax is robbery. I'll say it to the steward's face."},
	{49, "You'll say nothing to anyone. Eat."},
	{48, "A man has a mouth."},
	{49, "And a family. Eat."},
	{48, "Charcoal's short this winter. The burner sleeps more than he burns."},
	{49, "Then fetch it yourself."},
	{48, "And leave the forge cold?"},
	{50, "I'll go, master."},
	{50, "We're out of iron bars, master."},
	{48, "You're out of sense."},
	{50, "I looked everywhere."},
	{49, "He's scared to tell you, Vojtech."},
	{48, "The forge glow is the prettiest sight in the world."},
	{49, "You say that every night."},
	{50, "It's true, master."},
	{48, "The boy has sense."},
	{50, "I'd like my own mark on a blade."},
	{48, "When your blades don't snap."},
	{49, "Let him try one, Vojtech."},
	{48, "One. And I pick the steel."},
	{48, "A farmer's horse kicked me this morning."},
	{49, "Where?"},
	{48, "In the shin. I swore."},
	{50, "The whole street heard."},
	{49, "The orchard keeper complained again about the smoke."},
	{48, "I can't stop the fire."},
	{49, "You could move the chimney."},
	{48, "Move the chimney! I'll move the orchard."},
	{51, "That goat got into the turnip strip again."},
	{52, "Because you left the hurdle down, Prokop."},
	{53, "It was only a little bit down."},
	{51, "A little bit is all a goat needs."},
	{51, "The church roof drips on our bench again."},
	{52, "The priest says the rain's a blessing."},
	{51, "Then he can sit on our bench and be blessed."},
	{52, "Prokop. The children are listening."},
	{53, "The goat ate my shoe."},
	{54, "It ate both of mine."},
	{52, "You'll go barefoot till the cobbler is paid."},
	{53, "Then I'll tell the goat to pay him."},
	{52, "Two candles from the chandler's wife. We'll burn one tonight."},
	{51, "Save it. We can eat in the dark."},
	{54, "I don't like eating in the dark."},
	{52, "One candle, and we'll all pray for her."},
	{51, "If we eat the seed turnips there's no crop in autumn."},
	{52, "Then we don't eat them."},
	{53, "But I'm hungry."},
	{51, "Be hungry like a farmer, Adam. It's good practice."},
	{54, "I can count to thirty. The baker's son counted me to thirty."},
	{52, "Don't bother the baker's boy."},
	{54, "I counted our turnips. There are four."},
	{51, "Four. That's an honest count, I'll give her that."},
	{51, "The hens are shut in. I heard a wolf on the ridge."},
	{52, "Then the goat is in with us tonight."},
	{53, "In the house? She'll eat the bench."},
	{51, "Better the bench than the wolf having her."},
	{51, "My father once brought in forty sheaves from this strip."},
	{52, "Forty? Last year we had nine."},
	{51, "He had a better ox, and better luck."},
	{52, "He had a better wife, too, I'll wager."},
	{52, "Bless this pottage, Lord, and the hands that grew it."},
	{51, "And the goat that didn't eat it."},
	{52, "Prokop!"},
	{54, "Amen."},
	{51, "The rye on the far strip is ready, and I've only two hands."},
	{53, "I've two hands."},
	{51, "You've two hands that drop the scythe."},
	{52, "Ask the guest. Strong backs are rare."},
	{52, "Ida's feet are blue. I can't send her out."},
	{51, "Wrap them in sacking."},
	{54, "It itches."},
	{53, "Then don't move about. Sit by the fire."},
	{55, "The woad came out green again."},
	{56, "The crofter's wife will take green for the baby."},
	{55, "She can't pay for green. She can't pay for anything."},
	{56, "Then we give it for prayers, Vendula."},
	{56, "We should marry, Vendula. Before the summer."},
	{55, "Marry who? The bowyer smells of glue."},
	{56, "He smells of glue, but he has a roof."},
	{55, "So have we. And a loom. And no one telling us where to sit."},
	{55, "The big loom's creaking again."},
	{56, "Because you beat it, Vendula. Like it owes you coin."},
	{55, "It does owe me. Three bolts of cloth."},
	{56, "Then oil it, don't scold it."},
	{56, "The flax has been retting a week. The stream stinks."},
	{55, "That's the smell of linen."},
	{56, "That's the smell of the whole village complaining."},
	{55, "Let them. They wear the shirts."},
	{56, "That weaver in Talmberg asked for blue again."},
	{55, "Pavel. The one with the daughter."},
	{56, "He'll pay. He's proud, but he'll pay."},
	{55, "Then send the blue, and a bill."},
	{55, "Madder, woad and weld. I've a pot of each and no sun to dry the cloth."},
	{56, "We hang it in the loft."},
	{55, "The loft smells like a tavern."},
	{56, "Better than a tavern. It's warm."},
	{56, "I can't see the thread any more."},
	{55, "Light another candle."},
	{56, "They cost more than the thread."},
	{55, "Then spin by the fire, and listen to the wind."},
	{55, "The crofter's blanket is half done."},
	{56, "They can't pay for it."},
	{55, "It's a blanket for a baby. I'll charge the Lord."},
	{56, "The Lord's a slow payer."},
	{55, "The shearers brought wool, still full of burrs."},
	{56, "We spent two days picking it."},
	{55, "Burrs in cloth. Nobody will wear it."},
	{56, "They'll wear it. They'll complain, but they will."},
	{56, "The bowyer called at the door. He smelled of glue."},
	{55, "He always smells of glue."},
	{56, "He brought a fish, though."},
	{55, "A fish is a fish. Put it in the pot."},
	{57, "The steam trough cracked down the middle."},
	{59, "Because you left it dry."},
	{57, "Because oak is stubborn. Like my brother."},
	{58, "You're both stubborn, and I feed you."},
	{59, "The hunter wants a yew bow."},
	{58, "There's yew in the churchyard."},
	{59, "I'm not cutting the churchyard yew. I want to go to heaven."},
	{57, "Heaven doesn't need a bow, Kristof."},
	{57, "Every cart needs a wheel, and nobody can pay for one."},
	{58, "The reeve's cart pays."},
	{57, "The reeve's cart pays in candles."},
	{59, "At least we can work after dark."},
	{58, "The dyer sister asked after you again, Kristof."},
	{59, "Which one?"},
	{58, "Does it matter?"},
	{57, "It matters to the one who asked."},
	{57, "The abbey wants a cart. Oak axle, iron rim."},
	{58, "The abbey pays slowly."},
	{57, "The abbey pays in blessings."},
	{59, "Which don't buy iron."},
	{58, "There's sawdust in the soup again."},
	{59, "It's the wood. It floats in the air."},
	{58, "Then eat your soup outdoors."},
	{57, "Sawdust is good for the stomach."},
	{57, "The wood is snowed in. We'll burn the offcuts."},
	{59, "The offcuts are my bow staves."},
	{57, "Then burn the wheel ends. We'll start again."},
	{58, "Burn nothing. Put on a shawl."},
	{59, "The river's over the lower road."},
	{57, "Then the carts can't pass, and nobody needs wheels."},
	{58, "Nobody needs wheels now, you mean."},
	{57, "Nobody needs wheels, nobody eats."},
	{59, "I made a bow last year that threw an arrow through a door."},
	{57, "Through a rotten door."},
	{59, "Through any door."},
	{58, "Through the church door, is what he means."},
	{58, "I've a chest that wants a new hinge."},
	{57, "I'll see to it."},
	{58, "You said that at Easter."},
	{57, "Easter was a long time ago."},
	{61, "The chandler's wife paid in candles again."},
	{60, "We can't eat candles."},
	{61, "We can read by them."},
	{60, "You can't read, Bohumil."},
	{61, "The alehouse keeper wants pretzels for the feast."},
	{60, "Then he'll pay before I bake them."},
	{61, "He says he's good for it."},
	{60, "So was your father. And he's dead."},
	{60, "The miller's late again. The flour's damp."},
	{61, "Damp flour makes heavy loaves."},
	{60, "Heavy loaves still fill a man."},
	{61, "Nobody wants heavy."},
	{60, "My back aches from the oven."},
	{61, "I told you to rest."},
	{60, "Rest, and the village starves."},
	{61, "The village can eat yesterday's crusts."},
	{61, "I asked the chandler's daughter to walk with me after mass."},
	{60, "Her father would sooner see you hanged."},
	{61, "He said I must read the price of a candle."},
	{60, "Then learn it. It's one pfennig."},
	{60, "Who left the third loaf in so long?"},
	{61, "It was only a moment."},
	{60, "A moment! It's black as a cinder."},
	{61, "The crust is the best part, they say."},
	{60, "Bless this house and the oven that keeps it."},
	{61, "Bless the wheat, and the rye."},
	{60, "And the miller, God help him."},
	{61, "Amen."},
	{61, "The feast loaves want honey."},
	{60, "Honey's dear. The beekeeper wants silver."},
	{61, "Then we'll make plain ones."},
	{60, "Plain ones the priest will blame on me."},
	{60, "Every sheaf in the barn is a loaf in my oven."},
	{61, "The farms are late bringing the wheat."},
	{60, "They're late every year."},
	{61, "Because they love to watch us worry."},
	{60, "Your father sang when he fired the oven."},
	{61, "He sang badly."},
	{60, "He sang with love. That's not the same."},
	{61, "I miss him singing badly."},
	{62, "Another child coming in the crofter's house."},
	{62, "That's the sixth. God send them bread."},
	{62, "I'll bring a little tansy tea for the mother."},
	{62, "Feverfew for the fever, comfrey for the bones."},
	{62, "And prayer for the rest, because herbs don't mend a heart."},
	{62, "The barber-surgeon in the lowland bleeds everyone."},
	{62, "Bleeds them dry, then sends a bill."},
	{62, "I charge a loaf and a prayer."},
	{62, "If you see yarrow by the road, bring it to me."},
	{62, "It stops bleeding. I'm down to my last bundle."},
	{62, "My husband built this house with his own hands."},
	{62, "Thirty years he's been in the churchyard."},
	{62, "I still set his stool by the fire."},
	{62, "A cough in every house this winter."},
	{62, "Honey and thyme are all I have to give for it."},
	{62, "A warm room's harder to come by than either."},
	{62, "It's quiet with just me."},
	{62, "I talk to the pot. The pot doesn't answer."},
	{62, "But it never argues either."},
	{62, "The first nettles are up by the wall."},
	{62, "A good nettle soup mends a winter body."},
	{62, "I'm too stiff to bend. Another spring, and I'll not bend at all."},
	{62, "The old man in the hamlet has a bad cough."},
	{62, "I'd walk to him, but my knees won't."},
	{62, "A remedy needs a messenger."},
	{62, "I dreamed of the old fever year."},
	{62, "Eleven in the churchyard in one month."},
	{62, "I prayed with every one of them."},
	{63, "The church wants two hundred candles for the feast."},
	{64, "And they'll pay in prayers."},
	{63, "Prayers and the odd coin."},
	{66, "I'll dip the first fifty tonight."},
	{66, "The tallow stinks, father."},
	{63, "That stink built this upper floor, boy."},
	{66, "It's in my hair and my bread."},
	{64, "It's in my bedclothes. I've stopped noticing."},
	{65, "The baker's son asked if I'd walk with him after mass."},
	{64, "He can ask your father."},
	{63, "He can ask me when he can read a candle's price."},
	{65, "It's one pfennig, father. I told him."},
	{63, "The reeve checks my weights twice a year."},
	{64, "Because he thinks you cheat."},
	{63, "I cheat nobody. The wax shrinks."},
	{66, "Every candle shrinks when it burns."},
	{64, "The church wants beeswax, not tallow, for the high candles."},
	{63, "Beeswax is silver by the cake."},
	{64, "Then we ask the guest."},
	{63, "We don't ask guests to carry wax."},
	{63, "Candlemas is near. Every house wants a blessed candle."},
	{64, "And every house wants it for nothing."},
	{66, "I'll be dipping from dawn."},
	{65, "I'll trim the wicks."},
	{63, "The crofter paid for a candle with a turnip."},
	{64, "A good one?"},
	{63, "It had a worm."},
	{66, "We ate it anyway."},
	{63, "Forty years my father dipped candles. He built the lower floor."},
	{64, "And you built the upper."},
	{63, "And Viktor will build the roof."},
	{66, "I'll build a roof. I'd rather build a ship."},
	{65, "The wicks are twisted. I've blisters."},
	{64, "A chandler's daughter has blisters and a dowry."},
	{65, "I'd rather the blisters than the dowry."},
	{63, "You'll take both, as I did."},
	{63, "Tallow's short. The butcher says the pig's late."},
	{64, "He says it every year."},
	{63, "Then we ask the farms."},
	{66, "I'll ask them. I like the farms."},
	{67, "The Hare's the best alehouse this side of the river."},
	{68, "It's the only alehouse this side of the river."},
	{67, "That makes it the best."},
	{68, "It makes it the only, Otakar."},
	{68, "The bowyer drank on credit again. Three weeks."},
	{67, "He'll pay. A bowyer always comes good in the end."},
	{68, "He'll pay in arrows. I've no use for arrows."},
	{67, "Hang them on the wall. Customers like a wall."},
	{68, "The kegs are low. We need the brewer in Talmberg."},
	{67, "He'll want coin."},
	{68, "We've coin. Just not enough."},
	{67, "A guest who drinks three mugs fixes that."},
	{67, "Feast night. Every man in the parish will be here."},
	{68, "And every man's wife behind him."},
	{67, "The priest too, sneaking in at the back."},
	{68, "We'll have to count the stools twice."},
	{68, "The snow's heavy on the roof. I hear the beams."},
	{67, "They've held forty years."},
	{68, "Forty years of men leaning on them, drunk."},
	{67, "A roof's got to know its customers."},
	{68, "The sausage is nearly gone."},
	{67, "Pork's dear."},
	{68, "We're out of pork. I've nothing left to stuff."},
	{67, "Then it's cheese tonight, and a joke."},
	{67, "No customers tonight."},
	{68, "It's the rain. They'll come when it stops."},
	{67, "Thirst will bring them."},
	{68, "They'll come when the bowyer's paid."},
	{67, "A carter says there are bandits on the north road."},
	{68, "A carter says a lot over a mug."},
	{67, "He said they took a mule."},
	{68, "Then he sold the mule, and told a tale."},
	{68, "The priest preached an hour on drink."},
	{67, "He preached to an empty church."},
	{68, "He preached to a full alehouse after."},
	{67, "Aye. We charged for his water."},
	{67, "The road's open. Carters will come."},
	{68, "And they'll want a bed."},
	{67, "We've three beds."},
	{68, "Plus a stable full of other men's horses."},
	{69, "The eels are fat this year. Six in one trap this morning."},
	{70, "Six eels and not one sausage in the house."},
	{69, "Smoked eel keeps all winter. Sausage doesn't last the week."},
	{69, "Two traps empty again. Somebody lifts them before dawn."},
	{70, "Maybe the otter."},
	{69, "An otter doesn't carry off the trap as well."},
	{70, "Father says fish on Fridays is a blessing."},
	{69, "It's a blessing for me. For you it's a sentence."},
	{70, "Hold your tongue, Kilian. The children listen."},
	{71, "I'll catch a bigger trout tomorrow. With my hands again."},
	{72, "You'll fall in again."},
	{71, "I fell in once!"},
	{69, "Twice. And I pulled you out both times."},
	{69, "The net has more holes than net. It wants mending."},
	{70, "I mended it twice last week."},
	{72, "I can mend a little. I did one knot."},
	{70, "The miller took a tenth of our rye again."},
	{69, "The law says a sixteenth."},
	{70, "The law doesn't carry the sack up the hill."},
	{70, "The wind's coming straight through the reeds tonight."},
	{69, "Then pull the blanket higher, Johana."},
	{71, "My feet are cold. Can I put them in the ashes?"},
	{70, "You'll put them on the straw and sleep."},
	{69, "The ice is thick enough to cut a hole. Perch tomorrow."},
	{70, "Not on the pond. Not with the children near it."},
	{72, "I want to see the fish under the ice."},
	{69, "They want to see you too. With their teeth."},
	{70, "Say the prayer, children. The light's going."},
	{72, "Krystyna's tired. Can it be a short one?"},
	{70, "The Lord is never tired. You will say all of it."},
	{71, "I know the long one by heart."},
	{69, "They say bandits lie at the ford, taking toll."},
	{70, "They'll take nothing from us. We've nothing."},
	{69, "They'd take the boat, Johana."},
	{69, "The spring flood broke the weir again. Half my fish went through."},
	{70, "The mill needs the weir mended before we do."},
	{69, "The weir and the profit are the miller's. The lost fish are mine."},
	{70, "Four pfennig for a basket of eel. At the market they get ten."},
	{69, "The buyer in Talmberg knows we can't carry them farther."},
	{71, "I'd carry them farther. I'd carry them anywhere."},
	{74, "The drip's moved to the table now. Look, it's in the pot."},
	{73, "Then we're eating pottage with extra water."},
	{74, "You mend every roof but this one, Havel."},
	{73, "The reeds drowned in the flood. It's straw or nothing."},
	{74, "Straw lasts five years. Reed lasts twenty."},
	{73, "I'll be dead before the twenty anyway. So straw it is."},
	{73, "The count's man counted the chimneys. We've none, so he taxed the hearth instead."},
	{74, "Hearth tax on a hearth with no chimney."},
	{73, "He wrote it in the book. Once it's written, it's true."},
	{74, "Hear the wind in the thatch? It sounds angry."},
	{73, "It sounds like money I'll earn in the spring."},
	{74, "If the roof stays on till then."},
	{73, "A thatcher's roof leaks. A cobbler's children go barefoot."},
	{74, "And a thatcher's wife goes cold."},
	{73, "That's a poor joke, Sabina."},
	{74, "I learned it from you."},
	{73, "The Medars want their shed re-thatched before the swarms."},
	{74, "They pay in honey. We could use the honey."},
	{73, "They pay in honey if the bees allow it."},
	{73, "You've gone quiet again, Sabina. Is it the house?"},
	{74, "It's the evening, that's all. The house is too still."},
	{73, "I'd take noise over the quiet any day."},
	{73, "The priest says a prayer for rain on Sunday."},
	{74, "Say one for dry weather too, Havel. I'm sick of puddles."},
	{73, "He won't bless both. The Lord can't send rain and sun together."},
	{73, "Snow on a straw roof is a blanket, as long as it stays."},
	{74, "And the first thaw makes it a river."},
	{73, "Aye. Then I'm up the ladder in the cold."},
	{74, "That cough has been three weeks. See the widow."},
	{73, "It's the dust from the old thatch. It'll pass."},
	{74, "It won't pass, you stubborn man."},
	{74, "The widow's shutter is swinging off its hinge."},
	{73, "I'm a thatcher, not a carpenter."},
	{74, "You're a man with two hands. Go on."},
	{75, "Three pigs and the salt's dear. Smoke them all, I say."},
	{76, "The smokehouse holds two. The third hangs outside."},
	{79, "Then the crows get the third. I'll sit with it."},
	{75, "Salt is up another pfennig a measure. Robbery."},
	{76, "Then we smoke more and salt less."},
	{75, "Smoke takes wood, and somebody has to cut it."},
	{79, "The fisherman came again about the eel."},
	{75, "Tell him sausage costs more than eel."},
	{77, "He said he'd throw in a trout."},
	{75, "Then we're talking."},
	{77, "The herdsman's boar came in again. He wants us to kill it."},
	{75, "It's wild. It's the count's if anyone's."},
	{79, "The count's not here. The boar is."},
	{78, "The blood pudding's set this time, mother."},
	{76, "Then it's the only thing today that is."},
	{75, "Eat, Liba. We eat well in winter."},
	{76, "Liba's of an age. The miller's son looked at her at mass."},
	{78, "He looked at the roof, mother."},
	{75, "The miller's son has a wheel. That's something."},
	{75, "My arm aches. Fifty years of cleaving and it aches worse."},
	{76, "You're not fifty."},
	{75, "It feels like it. Pass the bread."},
	{79, "A year till I'm a master. Or so you said, master."},
	{75, "I said we'd see. You still lose the odd knife."},
	{79, "It wasn't lost. It was misplaced."},
	{76, "In the pigsty."},
	{76, "The flies are thick on the board. Cover it, Rupert."},
	{77, "I've covered it three times."},
	{75, "Then a fourth. A fly in the sausage costs me a customer."},
	{75, "The count's hunt took the deer in the north wood."},
	{76, "So no venison for us."},
	{77, "The forester told me the fox is left for us."},
	{76, "Eat while it's hot. The cold takes everything, even the stew."},
	{78, "I'll warm the bread by the fire."},
	{75, "Shut the dog out before it finds the sausage."},
	{80, "The neighbours say the smell reaches their beds."},
	{81, "The wind blows from us to the Medars."},
	{80, "Let it blow back, then. We're used to it."},
	{81, "Oak bark peels best while the sap's up. I'll start tomorrow."},
	{80, "And the forester will start on you."},
	{81, "It's dead wood, father. Nobody minds."},
	{81, "The priest moved us again, right to the back."},
	{80, "Back there we're nearer the door. Easier to slip out."},
	{81, "It's easier to be forgotten, father."},
	{80, "Your mother would have laughed at this stew."},
	{81, "She'd have laughed at the stink of it, too."},
	{80, "She did. Every day, for twelve years."},
	{81, "I've outgrown my shoes again."},
	{80, "You've feet like boats."},
	{81, "Then make boat shoes."},
	{80, "Nobody sits next to a tanner at the alehouse."},
	{81, "More room for us."},
	{80, "And fewer to hear our troubles."},
	{80, "The river is high. The limewater will wash clean."},
	{81, "The Rybars complain it runs red into their fish."},
	{80, "It's the leather. It does no harm."},
	{80, "Winter's the best time. The hides come in and the stench stays low."},
	{81, "The hides are frozen on the stretchers."},
	{80, "Then we thaw it. That's called work."},
	{81, "If I'm good, will I have the tubs one day?"},
	{80, "If you're good. And if you live."},
	{81, "I will live. I'm too stubborn not to."},
	{80, "We'll eat in the dark tonight. Candles cost."},
	{81, "I can find my mouth blind."},
	{80, "You always could."},
	{84, "The mead has cleared at last. It's gold, Dobromila."},
	{82, "It was cloudy last week."},
	{84, "Last week it was young. Now it's a man."},
	{83, "My eye is still puffy. Can you see it?"},
	{82, "I see it. I told you, Mlada."},
	{84, "A sting never harmed a beekeeper's child. Much."},
	{84, "The chandler wants all the wax for the Easter candles."},
	{82, "Then he'll pay a fair price."},
	{84, "I name the fair price here, not him."},
	{84, "A swarm hung in the pear tree this morning."},
	{83, "I saw it! It was like a beard."},
	{82, "I told you, don't go near it."},
	{84, "She stood and watched. She has the knack."},
	{84, "The skeps are hushed now. They hold their warmth in a ball."},
	{82, "They need no feeding if the honey's left."},
	{84, "I leave them plenty. They deserve it."},
	{82, "My honey cakes are the only sweet thing in this hamlet."},
	{83, "Mother, you say that every Sunday."},
	{82, "Because it's true every Sunday. Eat, Mlada."},
	{82, "The weaver's son asked after Mlada at the fair."},
	{83, "He looked at the hair ribbons, not at me."},
	{84, "A man who looks at ribbons might be useful."},
	{82, "The priest asked if we fasted the Ember days."},
	{84, "We did. Honey cakes aren't meat."},
	{82, "Erazim, honey cake is not a fast."},
	{84, "The lime trees are in flower. Listen to the hives."},
	{82, "Like a mill, but friendlier."},
	{83, "It sounds like singing."},
	{84, "Somebody lifted a skep from the Havirs' wall last week."},
	{82, "Not ours?"},
	{84, "Not ours. But I put the dog by the hives tonight."},
	{82, "The beeswax candles are for Sunday only."},
	{83, "I'd light one now. Just a small one."},
	{82, "For the mass, Mlada. Not for the cat."},
	{85, "The rye was more chaff than grain. I sieved it twice."},
	{86, "We'll eat it anyway."},
	{85, "We will. It's bread, near enough."},
	{85, "The count's men took the goose. The goose!"},
	{86, "It was only one goose, Blazej."},
	{85, "It was our goose. It was my goose."},
	{86, "Grandmother's cough is worse. The little ones too."},
	{85, "I'll fetch the widow's herbs tomorrow."},
	{87, "Can I go? I know the way."},
	{87, "Mother, may I have the last of the bread?"},
	{86, "If father's done."},
	{85, "I'm done. Take it, Kaspar. Take it."},
	{85, "Two days' hay for the yeoman and a bag of rye."},
	{86, "And the next day?"},
	{85, "The next day, hay for ourselves. If it doesn't rain."},
	{86, "We say the prayer, then bed. Kaspar, hands together."},
	{87, "I'm thanking God for the bread."},
	{85, "And for the roof. And for the soup. Amen."},
	{85, "Somebody took the stack behind the shed."},
	{86, "The cold took it, not a person."},
	{85, "The cold doesn't carry axes."},
	{85, "The fox took another hen. That's three."},
	{86, "We've four left."},
	{87, "I'll watch tonight. With the stick."},
	{86, "My back is bent from the field. I'll sleep before the candle."},
	{85, "There's no candle. We're to bed with the sun."},
	{86, "That's all the sleep we get."},
	{85, "We owe the miller two measures of rye."},
	{86, "We owe the priest a tithe."},
	{85, "We owe everyone but the Lord, and He's patient."},
	{88, "Fresh wolf tracks by the high fold again."},
	{89, "Then the lambs stay in the byre."},
	{90, "I'll sit up with my sling!"},
	{88, "Not you. Into bed with a blanket, Dalibor."},
	{89, "Take the dog, Simon. He's company at least."},
	{88, "The dog hides under the cart when he smells one."},
	{91, "He's a clever dog. He knows the wolves are bigger."},
	{88, "Shearing starts Monday if the weather holds."},
	{89, "I've sharpened the shears."},
	{90, "Can I hold the sheep this time?"},
	{88, "You can hold the sheep and hold your tongue."},
	{91, "The weaver in Talmberg wants all our fleeces."},
	{88, "He'll have them if he pays right."},
	{89, "Last year he paid like a miser."},
	{88, "I'll sleep in the byre tonight. The ewes are close."},
	{89, "You'll catch your death."},
	{88, "The ewes are warm. They don't kick if you're quiet."},
	{91, "Seven lambs! And one of them's black!"},
	{88, "The black one's unlucky, they say."},
	{89, "They say that of everything. It's a lamb."},
	{88, "The count's man counted the flock. Then he took the best ram."},
	{89, "For the tax."},
	{88, "For his own table. I saw him sell it."},
	{88, "The lambs are scouring. I need the herbs from the wood."},
	{89, "The widow knows which. Ask her."},
	{90, "I know a herb! The one with yellow flowers."},
	{89, "The wind's up. The shutters are rattling."},
	{88, "The flock's in. I counted twice."},
	{91, "Father counts the sheep to sleep."},
	{89, "The new cheese is ready. Cut the first wheel."},
	{88, "It's the best we've made."},
	{90, "You said that of the last one."},
	{89, "The priest wants the first lamb for Easter."},
	{88, "He's the Lamb of God himself, I'm told. And he wants mine."},
	{89, "Simon! Not at the table."},
	{92, "Found fresh boar tracks by the stream. Must be a big tusker."},
	{93, "And the forester's dog was sniffing about there."},
	{92, "The forester's dog can sniff. It can't carry a spear."},
	{93, "The forester stopped me at the well. Asked after you."},
	{92, "What did you tell him?"},
	{93, "That you're at home with the cheese."},
	{93, "The fox pelt fetched three pfennig. Three!"},
	{92, "Everyone's selling fox this year."},
	{93, "Then go hunt something rare."},
	{93, "Eat the venison. We didn't buy it."},
	{92, "And we certainly didn't catch it near the count's wood."},
	{93, "Robert! Not in front of the guest."},
	{92, "It was cold out there. My beard froze to the cloak."},
	{93, "Sit by the fire, then."},
	{92, "I'm sat. I'm so sat I'll never rise."},
	{92, "I've ten arrows left and four of them are bent."},
	{93, "The smith's boy sells arrows. Cheap ones."},
	{92, "Cheap ones are crooked. Crooked arrows catch nothing."},
	{92, "Bandits in the east wood. I saw smoke."},
	{93, "Smoke could be charcoal burners."},
	{92, "Charcoal burners don't leave a boot print the size of a barrel."},
	{93, "You said you'd cook tonight, Robert."},
	{92, "I did. I cooked the meat. You're eating it."},
	{93, "You roasted it black."},
	{93, "The larder's thin. A rabbit and a heel of cheese."},
	{92, "The deer come down to the stream at dawn."},
	{93, "Then be there at dawn, and bring one home."},
	{92, "My father hunted a bear in this wood. Last one, they say."},
	{93, "He told it differently each time."},
	{92, "A bear grows with each telling."},
	{93, "We've no salt to cure the boar, if you catch it."},
	{92, "Then we'll smoke it."},
	{93, "Smoke needs dry wood and ours is all damp."},
	{94, "The shaft groaned again last night. Like a cow in labour."},
	{95, "Then the timbers are going. Replace them."},
	{94, "Timber costs coin. Coin we haven't got."},
	{97, "Grandfather, tell the story of the ghost in the old shaft."},
	{98, "Not tonight, child. It's a cold one."},
	{97, "Please! You promised."},
	{98, "The ghost has no head. That's all I'll say."},
	{94, "The smith says our ore's full of slag."},
	{98, "The smith couldn't smelt butter."},
	{95, "He pays on time, Matyas. Be civil."},
	{96, "Father let me hold the lamp again today."},
	{95, "And you came up at noon, as promised."},
	{96, "I could stay down longer. I'm not scared."},
	{98, "Fifty years down and now I can't climb a stair."},
	{95, "You climb well enough to the pot."},
	{98, "The pot is the only thing that matters."},
	{94, "We're out of torches. I'll go dark tomorrow."},
	{95, "You'll go with the lamp and nothing else."},
	{94, "The lamp burns oil. We've none."},
	{97, "Pavla's allowed to the shaft mouth to wave Father down."},
	{95, "To wave. Not to go in."},
	{97, "I'll never go in. I'm a girl."},
	{95, "The damp's in everyone's chest this spring."},
	{94, "We'll burn the bark fires and dry out."},
	{98, "It's the mine, Monika. It breathes on us."},
	{94, "The rain's filling the lower seam. Tomorrow we bail."},
	{96, "I'll bail. I'll bail for hours."},
	{95, "You'll bail for two. Then the pot."},
	{94, "The count wants a third of the iron. A third!"},
	{95, "He wants a third of everything."},
	{98, "In my day, it was a tenth. And we were glad."},
	{99, "Fifty-one years we've been married, Kunhuta."},
	{100, "Fifty-two."},
	{99, "I won't argue. It was a good year, whichever."},
	{100, "The herdsman's wife brought milk this morning."},
	{99, "A good woman. A good one."},
	{100, "She sat a minute, and she asked after you."},
	{100, "Our boys are in the churchyard. Both of them."},
	{99, "Heinrich took the fever. Jan took the arrow."},
	{100, "I light a candle each Sunday. We've few candles."},
	{100, "Two eggs this morning. The hens are laying again."},
	{99, "Spring is kind to us."},
	{100, "Spring's kind to everyone, Augustin."},
	{99, "When I was young the count's father ruled. He was fair."},
	{100, "He hung four men on the first day."},
	{99, "Four fair hangings."},
	{100, "Tomorrow's the feast of the old saint. We'll walk to mass."},
	{99, "It's a mile. My knees are against it."},
	{100, "Your knees are against everything, Augustin."},
	{99, "The apples are falling. Nobody's gathering them."},
	{100, "We can't climb. We can't reach."},
	{99, "A boy could do it in an hour. For a few."},
	{100, "When one of us goes, the other won't last a month."},
	{99, "Then we'll go together. I've asked."},
	{100, "The Lord doesn't take orders."},
	{99, "My bones ache when the frost comes."},
	{100, "Mine too. Two old creaking gates."},
	{99, "A gate with no hinge. That's me."},
	{100, "The priest came by on Sunday. He blessed the bread."},
	{99, "He ate half of it."},
	{100, "It was blessed bread, Augustin. It was meant to be eaten."},
	{101, "The baker says my flour's gritty. Every year he says it."},
	{102, "It's the stones, Arnost. They want dressing."},
	{101, "The baker wants a free sack, that's what he wants."},
	{105, "The sail's torn. The wind took a corner."},
	{101, "Then mend it before the harvest."},
	{104, "It needs good linen. We've none."},
	{101, "Then find some."},
	{103, "Everyone hates the miller, father. Why make it worse?"},
	{101, "Because everyone needs the miller. That's why they hate him."},
	{102, "They hate you because you take a sixteenth and call it a fifteenth."},
	{104, "The brewer says you hold barley back for a better price."},
	{101, "The brewer can buy elsewhere."},
	{102, "There's no elsewhere, Arnost. We're the only mill."},
	{105, "I asked about my wage, master."},
	{101, "You ate today. That's a wage."},
	{102, "Pay him a pfennig, Arnost. Just one."},
	{101, "Wheat flour for the harvest feast. The finest."},
	{102, "Wheat's dear. It's three times the rye."},
	{101, "The feast pays for it."},
	{101, "The wheel froze in the night. The ice is thick on the paddles."},
	{105, "I'll break it off with the mallet."},
	{101, "Gently. Last year you broke a paddle."},
	{102, "The brewer's son is coming on Sunday. Wear the blue."},
	{103, "I'll wear what I like, mother."},
	{101, "She'll wear the blue. Brewers don't marry grey."},
	{104, "The stones hummed all night. I couldn't sleep."},
	{101, "That hum is money, Leopold."},
	{104, "It's money that wakes me."},
	{102, "The priest asks for a tenth of the flour for the poor."},
	{101, "The poor need no flour. The poor need work."},
	{102, "Arnost! The poor need both."},
	{101, "The fisherman's been fishing in my mill pond."},
	{102, "He's no money. Let him."},
	{101, "It's my pond. I'll not have it fished."},
	{106, "Free land, free men. My grandfather paid for this farm in silver."},
	{107, "Silver he took from a dead knight."},
	{106, "Mother!"},
	{107, "I was there. I saw the knight."},
	{108, "The farmhands ate the whole second loaf."},
	{111, "We worked the whole second field, mistress."},
	{108, "Then work the third, and eat crusts."},
	{113, "I'll eat the crusts."},
	{109, "The oxen won't pull for Cenek. They only pull for me."},
	{111, "I pull as hard as you."},
	{109, "You pull their tails."},
	{106, "Boys. Plough, then fight."},
	{113, "Can I drive the oxen tomorrow?"},
	{106, "When you can reach the yoke, Hugo."},
	{113, "I can reach it now."},
	{107, "On a stool, perhaps. Not on the ground."},
	{110, "The abbey's tenants were asking about our geese again."},
	{106, "They've fifteen. They need twenty."},
	{108, "We have thirty. We could spare five."},
	{106, "Spare five, and ten more come asking."},
	{106, "Another year of good barley, God willing."},
	{108, "You say it every year."},
	{106, "And every year the Lord provides."},
	{107, "And every year the tax collector arrives."},
	{106, "The wheat's ready. We need every hand."},
	{109, "We've the two farmhands and two hands apiece. That's all there is."},
	{108, "And the guest can lend a hand, if they've a mind to."},
	{106, "A guest who works eats twice."},
	{107, "That miller's boy keeps looking at you, Anezka."},
	{110, "He's looking at the bread."},
	{107, "He's looking at your face."},
	{108, "He's counting the dowry."},
	{107, "I was a girl when the war came through."},
	{106, "Mother, not at supper."},
	{107, "They burned the south barn and took the bull."},
	{113, "What did the bull do?"},
	{109, "The grey ox is lame in the near foot."},
	{106, "Then he rests till spring."},
	{111, "We can't plough with one ox."},
	{106, "Then we'll borrow one, and owe a favour."},
	{114, "The brothers want twenty geese by Martinmas. We have fifteen."},
	{115, "Then pray for five more."},
	{114, "I've prayed. The geese haven't heard."},
	{115, "They've heard. They're just slow."},
	{114, "Brother Hugh says the herb garden wants weeding before the frost."},
	{115, "Brother Hugh wants a lot."},
	{114, "He's the one who forgives our rent."},
	{115, "Then we weed. Until our knees break."},
	{115, "We can't read a word of the psalter on the wall."},
	{114, "The Lord can read it for us."},
	{115, "The Lord's not at my table."},
	{114, "He's in every bite."},
	{115, "Fish today. The monks gave us a pike."},
	{114, "A good pike."},
	{115, "A good bone, with a little pike on it."},
	{114, "Say grace, wife."},
	{114, "The snow's up to the door."},
	{115, "Then the monks can't collect their rent."},
	{114, "They'll come on stilts."},
	{115, "They'll come on goose-back, for all I care."},
	{115, "Martinmas is nine days off."},
	{114, "I know it."},
	{115, "Twenty geese, and a bag of coin."},
	{114, "And a prayer. And a loaf, if God allows."},
	{114, "Brother Hugh asked after the geese today."},
	{115, "He asks every day."},
	{114, "He's a good man, just hungry."},
	{115, "All the brothers are hungry. They don't say it."},
	{115, "The beans are in. The peas are next."},
	{114, "The peas want a warm week."},
	{115, "Everything wants a warm week."},
	{114, "Aye. And a dry one."},
	{114, "The fire's low. Add a log."},
	{115, "It's the last one."},
	{114, "Then we'll sit close."},
	{115, "We always do."},
	{114, "We owe the abbey a barrel of rye."},
	{115, "We owe everyone a barrel of something."},
	{114, "We'll pay in geese."},
	{115, "In geese, in prayers, and in hope."},
	{116, "Bled three men today, set a leg and pulled a tooth."},
	{117, "Did you wash?"},
	{116, "Washing's for monks."},
	{117, "Then the monks live longer than your patients."},
	{117, "The herb widow says you bleed people dry, Kasimir."},
	{116, "The herb widow sells weeds and prayers."},
	{117, "And her patients live."},
	{116, "Mine live too, when they're strong."},
	{116, "The duke's page has a cough. I was called to the manor."},
	{117, "And you bled him."},
	{116, "I bled him a little."},
	{117, "A little more and he'd be a ghost."},
	{116, "A carter's leg, broken in two places."},
	{117, "And he screamed."},
	{116, "He bit the strap and wept."},
	{117, "Next time, give him ale first."},
	{117, "The poultice pot is empty."},
	{116, "Then fill it."},
	{117, "I need herbs, Kasimir. From the woods."},
	{116, "Then ask the guest. I've a patient at noon."},
	{116, "My hands are tired. Forty men is a lot of blood to let."},
	{117, "Then rest."},
	{116, "If I rest, there's a man out there who needs me."},
	{117, "Then he can wait for tomorrow. Eat your supper."},
	{116, "Twelve cases of marsh fever down in the lowland."},
	{117, "Burn the reeds."},
	{116, "The reeds aren't the cause."},
	{117, "Then why do the sick live in the reeds?"},
	{116, "Every house wants a bleeding before winter."},
	{117, "And they pay in cabbages."},
	{116, "Cabbages are good for the blood."},
	{117, "Cabbages are good for the pot."},
	{116, "A woman's tooth. Rotten as an old plank."},
	{117, "She cried for an hour."},
	{116, "The tooth cried louder."},
	{117, "And you laughed."},
	{116, "The rich man's gout is worse."},
	{117, "He eats meat every day."},
	{116, "I told him to eat less."},
	{117, "He told you to mind your trade."},
	{118, "The duke wants forty mail shirts before the spring muster."},
	{120, "Forty! We've rivets for ten."},
	{118, "Then we'll make rivets, all night."},
	{119, "You said that last winter and slept at the bench."},
	{120, "The smith in Talmberg can't make a helmet that fits a child."},
	{118, "The smith in Talmberg makes horseshoes. Let him."},
	{120, "He makes bad horseshoes."},
	{119, "Be kind, Fridrich. He's a neighbour."},
	{120, "We're burning charcoal like it was straw."},
	{118, "Forty shirts burn forty sacks."},
	{120, "The charcoal-burner's cart is late."},
	{119, "He's always late. Pay him early."},
	{119, "Come to the table, Bernard."},
	{118, "A moment. One more link."},
	{119, "You said that an hour ago."},
	{118, "Then it's an hour nearer finished."},
	{120, "Wenzel found a rivet in his sleeve this morning."},
	{118, "A good rivet?"},
	{120, "A flat one."},
	{118, "Then it's yours. Keep it as a charm."},
	{119, "Fridrich has no winter coat."},
	{120, "I've a leather apron."},
	{119, "You've a leather apron with holes."},
	{118, "A coat costs a day's work."},
	{120, "I singed my eyebrow off at the forge."},
	{118, "Both of them?"},
	{120, "One. The other's going."},
	{119, "You look surprised, Fridrich."},
	{118, "The muster is in six weeks. Forty shirts, and I've made twelve."},
	{120, "Twelve?"},
	{118, "Twelve good ones."},
	{119, "Eat. You can't make mail on an empty stomach."},
	{118, "The duke's man came to count the shirts."},
	{119, "And?"},
	{118, "He counted fourteen and said fifteen."},
	{120, "He's a bad counter."},
	{118, "He's the duke's counter. His counting is law."},
	{119, "The house is quiet when you sleep, Bernard."},
	{118, "That's the only quiet we have."},
	{119, "Then sleep more."},
	{118, "I'll sleep when the shirts are done."},
	{122, "Twelve of the duke's letters go out at Prime, with twelve seals to press."},
	{123, "I warmed the wax, master."},
	{122, "And the seals?"},
	{123, "The seals are cold, master. They're metal."},
	{123, "The ink froze in the pot last night, master."},
	{122, "Then warm it at the fire and don't let me see you do it."},
	{123, "Yes, master."},
	{122, "And fetch a clean cloth for the guest."},
	{122, "Sit, guest. Eat. You're the first company at this table since the season began."},
	{123, "I've set two places, master."},
	{122, "Set three. One for the stranger."},
	{123, "Yes, master."},
	{122, "A sealed letter for the reeve's clerk. It must not be opened."},
	{123, "I won't open it, master."},
	{122, "You couldn't read it if you did."},
	{123, "That's why I'm honest, master."},
	{122, "Parchment is dear. A sheet is a calf's whole skin."},
	{123, "Two calves for the duke's letters."},
	{122, "Three, if I spoil a page."},
	{123, "I won't spoil a page, master."},
	{122, "I read by one candle. Not two."},
	{123, "One is dim, master."},
	{122, "One is thrifty."},
	{123, "Yes, master. Dimly thrifty."},
	{122, "A man came with a forged charter last week."},
	{123, "He was very polite, master."},
	{122, "He spelt 'Bohemia' with three e's."},
	{123, "A shocking error, master."},
	{122, "The roads are open and the letters multiply."},
	{123, "Eleven since Tuesday, master."},
	{122, "A dull Tuesday, then."},
	{123, "Yes, master."},
	{123, "I've been with master eleven years."},
	{122, "Twelve."},
	{123, "Twelve, master. I stand corrected."},
	{122, "You've never been corrected. It's why I keep you."},
	{122, "The parchment curls in the heat."},
	{123, "I weighted the corners, master."},
	{122, "With what?"},
	{123, "The duke's seal, master. It's heavy."},
	{124, "The duke hunts on Thursday. Every hound needs to be fit."},
	{127, "The old bitch is lame, father."},
	{124, "Then she stays home, and we pray for no boar."},
	{125, "Pray for no rain, too."},
	{125, "Poachers again in the east wood. The forester found snares."},
	{124, "I'll find whoever set them. The duke will hang them."},
	{127, "Hanging seems a lot for a hare."},
	{124, "A hare is the duke's hare."},
	{126, "Father, let me ride on the hunt this time."},
	{124, "When you can sit a horse at a gallop, Adela."},
	{126, "I can gallop!"},
	{125, "In the yard, with a halter on."},
	{127, "The old bitch is stiff in the cold."},
	{124, "She's twelve. That's old for a hound."},
	{127, "She still has a nose."},
	{124, "A nose and four bad legs."},
	{125, "The hounds eat more than the family."},
	{124, "They work harder."},
	{127, "They don't wash dishes."},
	{125, "No. But they bark through the night."},
	{124, "There's a fresh boar track by the stream, wide as my hand."},
	{125, "Then stay off the stream."},
	{124, "It's my stream, Rosina."},
	{125, "The boar thinks it's his."},
	{124, "I heard wolves by the ridge last night."},
	{127, "How many?"},
	{124, "Three. Maybe four."},
	{125, "Bring the hounds in. And bar the door."},
	{127, "I hit the target at forty paces."},
	{124, "The target was a barn."},
	{127, "A moving barn, father."},
	{126, "You hit the barn. I hit the crow behind it."},
	{125, "Three hares in the pot, and the duke none the wiser."},
	{124, "They were hares from my own meadow."},
	{125, "You've no meadow."},
	{124, "I have the grass beneath my boots."},
	{124, "The arrows are crooked. Every one of them."},
	{127, "I fletched them myself."},
	{124, "They fly like drunk geese."},
	{127, "Then the geese were drunk before me."},
	{128, "Three travellers stuck by the snow on the pass. They'll drink us dry."},
	{129, "They'll pay us well for it, Walter."},
	{128, "They'll pay in promises."},
	{129, "I'll take promises and a deposit."},
	{130, "The grey horse kicked me again."},
	{128, "Then stand where she can't reach."},
	{130, "She reaches everywhere."},
	{129, "That's why she's called the grey devil."},
	{129, "A merchant came through with news of war in the south."},
	{128, "There's always a war in the south, mother."},
	{129, "This one is bigger."},
	{128, "Aye. It's always bigger."},
	{129, "Warm yourself by the fire, traveller. The pass is cold this winter."},
	{128, "Mother, that's a guest, not a traveller."},
	{129, "Everyone's a traveller on this mountain."},
	{130, "Even the goat."},
	{128, "We burn more wood than any house in the county."},
	{129, "We freeze less than any house in the county."},
	{128, "Six bundles a day in winter."},
	{130, "I carry them. My arms are long now."},
	{129, "Oats are low. The horses eat like kings."},
	{128, "They pay like beggars."},
	{129, "The travellers pay, not the horses."},
	{128, "Then they pay too little."},
	{130, "Is it my day's pay today?"},
	{128, "Your pay's a bed and a bowl."},
	{130, "The bowl's small."},
	{129, "Give him a penny, Walter."},
	{128, "There's a dozen pilgrims on the road."},
	{129, "Pilgrims eat and sing."},
	{128, "And pay in blessings."},
	{129, "Blessings don't fill a keg."},
	{128, "A pilgrim, a monk and a merchant walk into the pass."},
	{129, "Walter, don't."},
	{128, "The merchant sold the pilgrim a map and the monk blessed it."},
	{130, "And then?"},
	{129, "They say bandits hold the east road."},
	{128, "They say that every summer."},
	{129, "This time they took a mule."},
	{130, "I'd hide in the hay."}
};

