new const g_FamilyVoiceText[2144][] = {
	"God give you good day. Mind the mud, it's been a wet week.",
	"No need. A good turn costs little.",
	"Jakub Brabec. I work the low strip below the road, same as my father did.",
	"Dorota's my wife, and the three little ones are ours. Old Ludmila sits by the fire and tells us how it used to be.",
	"I'd have the children eat better than I did. They will, if the rye holds.",
	"The reeve's clerk counted our sheaves twice this year. That man trusts nobody with a scale.",
	"Dry at last. The strip needed it after all that rain.",
	"Rain again. The low strip will be a pond by Vespers.",
	"Fog on the strip. I can't see the far hedge, so I'm mending harness.",
	"Nothing grows under snow, but at least the ox gets a rest.",
	"I keep to the strip. Folk say the wardens ride the main road, and that the woods past the ford aren't safe.",
	"Take a hen off my yard and I'd want him named. The guards bind him, and then it's the gaol or the stocks.",
	"Thin on the low strip, thanks to the rain. The upper rows look better.",
	"Nothing I'd trust a stranger with. The strip's weeded, the wood's cut. Try me tomorrow.",
	"Dorota will set a bowl for you at {meal}. It's pottage, but it's hot.",
	"Out on the strip with the hoe, before the weeds get ahead of me.",
	"Come in, child, come in. Sit where the draught can't find you.",
	"Bless you for it. It's a long while since anyone thanked an old woman first.",
	"Ludmila. They call me Grandmother, so you may as well.",
	"Three grandchildren under this roof, and Jakub and Dorota feeding us all. God is good.",
	"I had four of my own, and I buried two. So I hold the little ones a bit tight.",
	"I hear little now. The children bring me the rest, and half of it is Matej blaming Bara.",
	"A dry day. My knees are grateful.",
	"Damp gets into my bones. I'll not be out in this.",
	"Fog like milk. When I was young it meant a wolf was near, but that's an old wife's tale.",
	"Say a prayer for anyone on the road tonight, with this snow coming down.",
	"I haven't left this village in twenty years. The wardens watch the roads, I'm told. The bandits stay in the woods.",
	"Taking what isn't yours is a sin. The guards will bind him, and the priest will have a word after.",
	"Smaller, and the winters were longer. We kept three cows then, and nobody was hungry in {season}.",
	"Oh, child, there's nothing I need. Ask Dorota, she's the one with the pot.",
	"Dorota will have a bowl for you at {meal}. An old woman likes to see a guest eat.",
	"Sitting. And praying for the three of them, since they won't do it themselves.",
	"Good day. Wipe your feet if you're coming in, I only swept.",
	"That's kind. We don't get much of it here.",
	"Dorota, Jakub's wife. If it needs mending or feeding, it's mine.",
	"Jakub, three children and Ludmila by the fire. That's six mouths and me.",
	"They're loud, but I'd miss the noise. I won't tell them.",
	"The pot's empty by Thursday and the onions are late. That's all the news this house has.",
	"Fine for the washing. It'll all be out on the hedge before noon.",
	"Rain on the washing again. Everything in this house smells of wet wool.",
	"The children keep getting lost between the hen coop and the door in this fog.",
	"With snow coming, every bundle of firewood we've got goes on the fire.",
	"I go as far as the baker's and back. Jakub says the wardens keep the road. I take his word.",
	"We've nothing worth taking. The hens, perhaps. If someone saw a thief, it's the guards he should tell.",
	"We get by. There's always a mouth to feed and a floor to sweep. The pot's low by Thursday.",
	"Nothing today, thank you. Ask again when the pot's empty, which is Thursday.",
	"Come at {meal}. It's a thin pot, but you're welcome to a bowl of it.",
	"Stirring the pot and counting the onions. Again.",
	"Hello! Are you from the road? Did you see any wolves?",
	"That's all right! Mother says I should say that.",
	"I'm Vojta. I'm the oldest, so I get the sacks.",
	"There's me, Bara and Matej. Matej's the one who throws stones.",
	"Grandmother says I'm like Father. I think she means I'm stubborn.",
	"Matej threw a stone at the potter's cat again. It hissed at him and everything.",
	"It's so dry that Mother let me out without a coat!",
	"I like jumping in the puddles when it rains, but Mother doesn't.",
	"Fog! You can't see the hen coop. I got lost for a bit.",
	"Snow! We built a snowman and Matej knocked it down.",
	"Father says stay near the houses. There are bad men past the ford.",
	"They put thieves in the stocks and everyone throws things. I'd throw an egg.",
	"Hide and seek in the hay is best. Mother says I'm too old for it, but she never finds me.",
	"I'm only the sack carrier. Father gives out the jobs.",
	"Mother says you can eat with us at {meal}. Bring your own spoon.",
	"Taking the seed sacks to the strip. They're heavy.",
	"Hello! Do you like hens? I've got one that follows me.",
	"Mother says that's the right thing to say back. You're welcome!",
	"I'm Bara. I'm the one with the hen.",
	"Vojta and Matej are my brothers. They're loud. I'm not.",
	"Grandmother lets me sit with her. She doesn't mind that I don't talk much.",
	"My hen doesn't lay, but Mother says I can keep her anyway. That's the news.",
	"The hen can come out of the coop when it's dry.",
	"The hen hates the rain. I don't like it much either.",
	"I held onto my hen in the fog so we wouldn't get lost.",
	"Snow! My hen walked in it and made little tracks.",
	"Wolves are on the road. Mother says so, and Father says bandits. I stay by the gate.",
	"Someone could take my hen. I'd cry and cry and then I'd tell Father.",
	"I count the eggs and draw in the dust with a stick. Don't tell Vojta.",
	"I've nothing to give. I'd give you my hen, but she won't lay.",
	"Come to supper. My hen will sit under the table.",
	"Feeding my hen. Then I'm sitting with Grandmother.",
	"Hello! I didn't do it, whatever it was.",
	"That's all right! Do you want to see my stone?",
	"Matej. I'm the youngest. It wasn't me.",
	"I've got Vojta and Bara. Bara tells on me.",
	"Grandmother says I'm the loudest. That's because I'm the smallest.",
	"The potter's cat looked at me funny. That's all.",
	"Dry, so I can throw stones in the yard.",
	"Rain! I can jump in the mud and Mother can't catch me.",
	"You can't see where the stone goes in fog.",
	"Snow! I can make snowballs. They're better than stones.",
	"The road has wolves on it. I'm not scared. A bit scared.",
	"People who steal get put in the stocks. I'd go and watch.",
	"Stones, mostly. And I'm good at the hay when Mother's not looking.",
	"I haven't got anything, honest. Ask Vojta, he's got all the jobs.",
	"You can eat with us. Mother won't mind. I don't like the onions, you can have mine.",
	"Throwing stones at a post. I'm getting good.",
	"Good day. Mind the warp, don't lean on the loom.",
	"Well met. Kind words are welcome here. We don't hear many.",
	"Pavel Tkac, weaver. If it's cloth you want, it's mine or it doesn't exist here.",
	"Agnes is my wife. Johanka's our daughter. She spins, though she's still learning.",
	"Agnes says what I'm afraid to hear. I couldn't work without her.",
	"A merchant wants blue cloth by the end of {season}. Blue! Where does a man find blue?",
	"Dry air is kind to the loom. The yarn holds its tension.",
	"Damp wool, damp weather. The warp snapped twice already.",
	"Fog is damp too. I'll weave plain grey today.",
	"Snow keeps customers away. At least the wool stays dry in here.",
	"I send cloth with the merchants. Folk say a road under the wardens is safe. Off it, I wouldn't risk a bolt.",
	"Bolts have a way of walking off a drying rack. Whoever sees it should name the man. The guards do the rest.",
	"Cloth is slow work, and the warp broke twice today. I'm not feeling clever.",
	"Nothing right now. The fleeces haven't come in, and the loom's full.",
	"Come to eat at {meal}. Agnes always cooks more than we can finish.",
	"At the loom. Another bolt for the merchant.",
	"Looking for the loom, or the weaver?",
	"Not at all. You've a good heart.",
	"Agnes, Pavel's wife. I keep the thread and the house. Pavel keeps to the loom.",
	"Pavel at the loom, and Johanka at the wheel. I'm the one in between.",
	"Johanka's at the age where she watches the church door. I watch her watching.",
	"The crofter's boy keeps looking at my daughter in church. I've told her to sit on the other side.",
	"Good drying weather. I'll have every skein out on the line by noon.",
	"Pavel's cross because the skeins won't dry in this wet.",
	"I'll mend today. Nobody comes to buy in this fog.",
	"Snow on the sill. I've put an extra shawl on Johanka.",
	"The merchants carry our cloth and say the road's quiet. I'd sooner not test it.",
	"A village this size knows who steals. Say what you saw to the guards and let them bind him.",
	"We get by, thank God. Pavel's cloth sells and my thread holds. Johanka's skeins are lumpy.",
	"Not today. Pavel might have something when the wool arrives.",
	"You'll find a place at our table at {meal}. Don't mind Pavel, he'll be at the loom until grace.",
	"Sorting thread and keeping an eye on Johanka.",
	"Hello. Have you been to Talmberg church? Who sits where?",
	"Oh. It's nice when someone says it.",
	"I'm Johanka. I spin. Mother says it's lumpy, but I'm improving.",
	"Mother and Father, and the wheel. There isn't room for much else.",
	"I want to see the fair. Mother says I'm too young to go alone.",
	"The crofter's boy stares at me at mass. I haven't told anyone but you.",
	"Dry! I can spin by the window with the shutters open.",
	"The wool smells of sheep when the rain gets into it.",
	"The fog's so thick I can't even see the church from here.",
	"It goes quiet when it snows. I spin better that way.",
	"I haven't been anywhere. Father says the road is safe if you keep to it.",
	"In the pillory people throw rotten fruit. I'd feel sorry for whoever it was.",
	"Mother says my yarn is lumpy. I call it character. We've agreed not to discuss it.",
	"I've only skeins and Mother's opinions to give. Neither's worth your time.",
	"Come and eat with us at {meal}. Mother cooks plenty, and Father talks about wool.",
	"Spinning. Four skeins done, more to go.",
	"Good day. State your business, or at least your name.",
	"Noted, and appreciated.",
	"Mikulas Pisar, clerk to the reeve. I keep the rolls, so I know who owes what.",
	"My wife Hedvika, my son Ota and my daughter Marketa. They keep my accounts in order, more or less.",
	"Ota wants to join the night watch. He'll learn his letters first.",
	"The count's tax rolls don't add up. Somebody has been hiding sheaves. I will find out who.",
	"A dry day suits the harvest count. I've been out since Prime.",
	"Rain blurs the ink. I've moved the rolls to the dry shelf.",
	"Nobody's visible to count in this fog. I'll write up what I have.",
	"Snow means paperwork. The count's men like to quarrel about deliveries when they can't get out.",
	"Every toll and every tax passes through my hands. The wardens keep the main roads clear, and the books say so.",
	"A theft witnessed goes on the roll of the wanted, with a bounty. The fine is paid at the gaol register. I keep the copy.",
	"The rolls are full of names that still owe. The count wants his share, same as every year.",
	"No copying today. The rolls are written up and I've nothing to spare.",
	"Hedvika keeps a decent table. You may sit at it at {meal}.",
	"At the rolls. There's always another page.",
	"Do come in, but mind the rolls. My husband will see.",
	"You're kind. It makes a change.",
	"Hedvika, wife of the reeve's clerk. I keep the house. The clerk keeps the rolls.",
	"Mikulas is out at his work most days. Ota and Marketa are mine, and they keep me busy.",
	"Marketa gets ribbons from the merchant's son. I'm returning them. We owe that family nothing.",
	"Mikulas hides sheaves in his rolls. No, that's what the farmers say, not what I say.",
	"A fine day. I'll air the linens.",
	"In this rain Mikulas will track mud across my floor again.",
	"There's nothing to do in fog but sit with a lamp and sew.",
	"Snow keeps the house quiet. That's worth a lot.",
	"Mikulas knows the roads. I know the market. That's enough for me.",
	"We keep the door latched and the plate counted. Anyone who sees a theft should say so, and Mikulas writes it down.",
	"We eat well and the house is warm. A clerk's wife can't complain, though I always find a way.",
	"Copying is Mikulas's, and he won't hand it to a stranger. I've none today.",
	"You may eat with us at {meal}. Grace is long in this house, I'll warn you.",
	"Setting the house in order before Mikulas gets home.",
	"Are you a guard? I want to join the watch.",
	"It's no trouble. Father says courtesy costs nothing.",
	"Ota. I'm the clerk's son. I'm learning my letters, mostly.",
	"Father keeps the rolls and Mother the house. Marketa's my sister, and she's always got a ribbon.",
	"Father says guards can't read their warrants. I think I'd manage anyway.",
	"The smith says the new tax is robbery. Father says if I write it down, the smith would hang.",
	"No rain, so the guards drill in the yard. I watch from the wall.",
	"I'll practise my letters on the damp slate while it rains.",
	"Good fog for slipping out unseen, but Father notices everything.",
	"The guards stamp their feet and look cold in the snow. I'd still like to be one.",
	"I'd patrol the roads if they'd let me. The wardens have the main road. The bandits wait off it.",
	"Thieves are bound by the guards and put in the gaol. I'd like to be the one doing the binding.",
	"Carrying messages, fetching ink, running errands. It's work, but I'd rather be on the night watch.",
	"Father says no errands for now. Ask me when the ink runs out.",
	"Come and eat at {meal}. Father likes a guest. It's a chance to show off his Latin.",
	"Running errands for Father. And practising my letters when he's not watching.",
	"Hello. Have you seen the fair yet? They say there's a juggler.",
	"How polite. Father would approve.",
	"I'm Marketa. My father is the clerk. I'm used to people asking.",
	"Father, Mother, Ota. And me, mostly with my sewing.",
	"Mother says I mustn't take ribbons from the merchant's son. I think they're pretty.",
	"The merchant's son brought me a ribbon from the fair. Mother says to give it back.",
	"Dry enough to sit by the window with my sewing.",
	"Rain makes everything grey. I'd like to be at the fair, where it's colourful.",
	"The fog makes the village look like a dream.",
	"We never go anywhere when it snows.",
	"The merchants keep to the road and say it's safe. I've never been past the mill.",
	"Mother says thieves end up in the gaol. That sounds dreadful, but I'd still lock my door.",
	"Our table's good and the house is clean. Father's the clerk, which puts us ahead of most folk.",
	"Nothing for you. Ask Father, he's always short of something.",
	"Come and eat at {meal}. Mother won't make a fuss. She'll be too busy with the ribbon.",
	"Sewing by the window. I'm making a collar.",
	"Morning, or whatever it is. I've been up since Prime.",
	"Think nothing of it. A loaf is a loaf.",
	"Stepan Pekar, baker. If you've eaten bread in Talmberg, you've eaten mine.",
	"My wife's Klara. Kuba the apprentice lives under our roof and burns half the rings.",
	"Half the village owes us for bread. Klara writes it down, because I can't.",
	"Up before Prime again tomorrow. The oven doesn't wait, and neither do the debtors.",
	"Dry flour sifts without lumps. That's all I ask of the sky.",
	"Damp flour makes heavy loaves. I'll bake smaller today.",
	"Fog slows the whole village. I'll have bread left over.",
	"Snow means firewood. I burn a bundle for every dozen loaves.",
	"Flour comes in by the road and bread goes out. I've never had trouble, but I lock the door at night.",
	"Two loaves lifted off the cooling board last week. Name him to the guards if you see him. I'd like my loaves back.",
	"The first batch is in and the second's rising. Ask me again at noon.",
	"Not today. The oven's loaded and I've hands enough. Come back tomorrow.",
	"Come at {meal}. There's always bread, and Klara will find the rest.",
	"Kneading and shaping, then loading the oven. After that it all starts again.",
	"Smell that? The oven's fresh!",
	"That's kind of you. We try.",
	"Klara, Stepan's wife. I keep the tally, because he can't write.",
	"Stepan, and Kuba the apprentice. We've no children, but Kuba eats like three.",
	"Stepan works too hard. I tell him, and he tells me the oven doesn't wait.",
	"The crofter's wife sent cheese for the bread we gave them. Good people. Others I could name.",
	"The dough rises well when the air is dry. Bread's easy today.",
	"Rain on the roof and the oven warm. I wouldn't be anywhere else.",
	"Nobody's out in the fog, nobody's buying. I'll sit and sew.",
	"Take a warm loaf to your neighbour when it snows, if you've got one.",
	"Merchants bring salt along the road and tell me it's safe. I'd never go alone.",
	"A stolen loaf usually means a hungry man. The guards will bind him all the same, and I'd say it's a pity.",
	"We've never been short of customers. Half the village owes us, though. I write it all down.",
	"Stepan may need a hand, but he'll say he doesn't. I've nothing today.",
	"Come and eat at {meal}. Nothing's worse than cold bread at somebody else's table.",
	"Counting the day's loaves and writing up what's owed.",
	"Hello. Sorry about the soot, I burnt the second batch.",
	"Oh. That's all right. I'm still learning.",
	"Kuba. I'm the baker's apprentice. I'm the one covered in flour.",
	"The master and his wife keep me here. My own family are far away.",
	"Mistress Klara treats me like a son. The master treats me like an apprentice.",
	"The miller's flour has grit in it again. Master says sift it twice.",
	"The dough can rise out in the sun today, it's so dry.",
	"I'm keeping the oven warm. Rain's beating on the door.",
	"Fog, so I can't see to the miller's. Perhaps the flour will arrive late.",
	"I'll fetch extra wood before the snow piles up.",
	"The master says the road's safe, but I never go alone.",
	"Two loaves went missing from the board. I was blamed first, then I wasn't.",
	"I'm up before Prime and my arms ache by noon. The master says I'll thank him later.",
	"Nothing from me. The master decides who does what.",
	"Come and eat with us at {meal}. Mistress Klara won't mind one more.",
	"Sifting the flour twice, like the master said.",
	"Watch your head, there's a beam coming.",
	"Glad to help. Mind the shavings on your way out.",
	"Radek Tesar, carpenter. If it's made of wood in Talmberg, I probably cut it.",
	"Two sons, Lukas and Vit. Their mother died. I don't cook as often as I should.",
	"They're good lads. Lukas wants to take the cooper's daughter to the fair, and I haven't said no.",
	"The mason wants scaffold poles before {season} is out. We have no seasoned oak.",
	"It's fine enough to saw in the yard. The sawdust blows off clean.",
	"Wet wood warps. I'll plane inside and wait.",
	"A saw can find a thumb in fog. We'll work by the bench.",
	"Snow's good for hauling logs on a sledge. Bad for everything else.",
	"Timber arrives by road and I pay the woodcutter for what shows up. The wardens see to the road itself.",
	"Planks vanish from the yard. If you see a man with my oak on his shoulder, tell the guards. They'll bind him.",
	"Good wood and steady work. I've a table to build, a scaffold to cut and two sons arguing.",
	"Not today. Ask Lukas, he may have a job.",
	"Come and eat at {meal}. Vit cooks. It'll be stew.",
	"Planing planks for the mason's scaffold.",
	"Hello. Sorry, sawdust. It gets everywhere.",
	"Don't mention it. Father taught us that.",
	"Lukas, the carpenter's elder son. I'm learning the trade.",
	"Father, Vit and me. We lost Mother some years ago.",
	"I'd like to take the cooper's daughter to the fair. I haven't asked Father yet.",
	"We've no seasoned oak left. Green wood warps, and the mason won't wait.",
	"We can work outside today. The shavings fly everywhere, mind.",
	"Rain, so we're indoors. The shop smells of damp pine.",
	"Father won't let us cut in fog. You can't see your own hands.",
	"With the snow we'll haul wood on the sledge, if the track holds.",
	"The woodcutter hauls logs by road. I've never seen a bandit, though Father says they exist.",
	"Somebody took our nails last month. Father reported it, but nobody was ever found.",
	"I'm learning joinery and scaffolds. I make mistakes. Father fixes them.",
	"Father decides that. Ask him, or wait for the next order.",
	"Come and eat at {meal}. Vit's stew is edible, most days.",
	"Cutting joints for the scaffold. Slowly, so Father doesn't shout.",
	"Hello. Are you hungry? I made stew.",
	"It's nothing. I'm glad you liked it.",
	"Vit, the younger son. I'm the one who cooks, when anyone does.",
	"Father and Lukas. We eat what I put in front of them.",
	"Lukas thinks he's the master already. I let him think so.",
	"The innkeeper's table wobbles. He asked if we'd fix it for ale, and Father said coin.",
	"Dry day. I've hung the washing and the stew's on.",
	"The stew's thin today. Blame the rain on the roof.",
	"I can't see the pot from here in this fog. I'm staying by it.",
	"Snow. Stew, then, and more of it.",
	"I don't go far. They say the road's safe, but I like the kitchen.",
	"Our firewood goes missing. I'd tell the guards if I knew who.",
	"Sawing, sweeping, cooking. Nobody thanks me for the cooking.",
	"Firewood's short at our table. Father's in the workshop, ask him about the rest.",
	"Come to eat at {meal}. It's stew again, but there's plenty.",
	"Stirring the stew and fixing the leg of a stool.",
	"Welcome, welcome! Sit anywhere. There's ale if you've coin.",
	"No bother. Come back, and bring friends.",
	"Bohus Hospodsky. I keep the inn. If something happened in Talmberg, I heard it first.",
	"Zuzana's my wife. Our children Eliska and Tomas help in the taproom. It's a family business.",
	"My daughter sees more than she tells. A good quality in an innkeeper's child.",
	"Two travellers left without paying last night. Two! I'll be watching the door now.",
	"Dry roads mean more travellers. Good for business.",
	"Rain keeps them indoors. They drink more when it's wet.",
	"Fog, and the coach is late. The inn will be full by dusk.",
	"Snow fills the taproom and the fire's hot. I've no complaints.",
	"Travellers say the main road is safe. The ones who leave it don't always arrive.",
	"Dice cheats and tab dodgers, mostly. A theft anyone saw goes on the roll. I'd like a few names on it.",
	"The taproom is loud and the till is light. Travellers talk when they drink, though half of it is lies.",
	"Not at the moment. Ask me after the next delivery, the kitchen's always short of something.",
	"Come at {meal} and bring an appetite. Zuzana's pottage is the best in Talmberg.",
	"Counting kegs and watching who walks in.",
	"Welcome. Close the door. The draught kills the fire.",
	"You're kind. We're used to rougher guests.",
	"Zuzana, Bohus's wife. I do the cooking and the counting. When he's drunk I do the talking too.",
	"Bohus, Eliska and Tomas. We live behind the taproom, so the noise comes with us.",
	"Bohus naps at the counter. I tell him he's lucky I'm here to watch.",
	"What's said in our rooms stays there. Though I'll tell you the old regular cheats at dice.",
	"I've three kegs ready for the thirsty ones the dry weather brings.",
	"With the rain, the taproom's full and I'm pouring until midnight.",
	"Travellers come in late and hungry in fog. I'll keep the pot warm.",
	"Everyone wants to stay when it snows. I put an extra blanket upstairs.",
	"Guests say the road's quiet. I've learned not to believe that.",
	"Dice cheats and guests who slip off unpaid. A real theft goes to the guards, who bind him and haul him away.",
	"The same as always, with spilled ale and somebody telling the same story again.",
	"Ask Bohus, he's always short of something. I've nothing for you myself.",
	"You can eat with us at {meal}. There's always a bowl for a guest.",
	"In the kitchen, and counting what the guests have eaten.",
	"Welcome to the inn. Do you want ale or a bed?",
	"Not at all. People rarely say it, so I notice.",
	"Eliska. I serve the taproom and I see everything.",
	"Mother and Father. My brother Tomas. The inn is our whole life.",
	"A guard asked me who stays upstairs, and I said nothing. Mother says I did right.",
	"Everyone knows the old regular cheats at dice. Everyone still plays him.",
	"It's dry, and the tables will be busy. I'll be on my feet all day.",
	"Rain, and the benches are full. Everyone smells like wet wool.",
	"Nobody can find the door in the fog. They follow the noise.",
	"They stamp the snow off and ask for hot ale.",
	"Everyone who's walked the main road says it's safe. Nobody says that about the woods.",
	"People who steal are caught in the end. The guards come for them, and then it's the gaol.",
	"Pouring, carrying, listening. Most of what I hear I keep to myself.",
	"Father decides that. Ask him, or wait until the kitchen runs short.",
	"Come and eat at {meal}. Mother will find you a seat near the fire.",
	"Wiping tables and listening. You'd be surprised what people say.",
	"Hello, welcome. Ale's by the cup. The stew's by the bowl.",
	"No trouble. Happy customers come back.",
	"Tomas, the innkeeper's son. I carry the kegs, and I hear the complaints.",
	"Father runs the taproom, Mother the kitchen, Eliska the floor. I carry things.",
	"Father thinks I'll take over. I'm not sure the inn is enough for me.",
	"The brewer's ale is sour this week. People are complaining. Father tells them it's a new kind.",
	"Dry, so the kegs come easily. The cart is back by noon.",
	"The cellar floods a bit when it rains. I'm bailing it out.",
	"The cart will be late in the fog. We'll run low.",
	"I'm stacking firewood in the snow until my arms ache.",
	"I carry kegs from the brewer. The road's fine, and the wardens are there if it isn't.",
	"The regular cheats at dice. I've seen it. Nobody does anything.",
	"Kegs, floors, firewood. Father counts the coin, I do the lifting.",
	"I can ask Father. He might have something for later.",
	"Eat with us at {meal}. There's room at the table.",
	"Hauling kegs from the cellar. The ale won't pour itself.",
	"Don't touch the green ware. It dents if you breathe on it.",
	"Kind of you. Few say it to a potter.",
	"Zdenek Hrncir, potter. Every mug in the inn came off my wheel.",
	"Petr is my son. His mother's in the churchyard, so it's the two of us and the kiln.",
	"Petr has her hands. He throws a cleaner pot than I do, and I'll never tell him.",
	"Half the pots cracked in the kiln. Wet clay and a cold night, that's all it takes.",
	"Green ware dries in a day when the air is dry. Three when it's damp.",
	"The clay won't dry in this and the kiln won't draw. I'll throw indoors and wait.",
	"Fog sits in the clay pit. I'll go for clay when it lifts.",
	"Snow means the kiln stays lit and I stay close beside it.",
	"Pots break on a cart, so I carry what I can on my back. The wardens help by clearing trouble off the road.",
	"Three mugs gone off my shelf. Name the thief to the guards and they'll bind him. I want my mugs back more than his hide.",
	"Slow work. One wrong thumb on the wheel and an afternoon is gone.",
	"The kiln's full, and I'd rather nobody walked in front of it. No work today.",
	"There's pottage at {meal}. Petr takes his turn at the pot, so it'll be thin.",
	"At the wheel, throwing twenty mugs for the inn.",
	"Hello. Mind the jugs by the door.",
	"Oh, that's good to hear. I'll tell Father.",
	"Petr. I'm the potter's son and I fire the kiln.",
	"Father and me. Mother died, and the house has been quiet since.",
	"Father says Mother would know what went wrong with the batch. I think so too.",
	"The innkeeper wants twenty mugs. Father is worried, but I think it's good news.",
	"It's dry, so the green ware can go outside today.",
	"It's raining. I like the sound on the roof while I work.",
	"Fog by the stream. I can't see if the clay pit is still dug out.",
	"Snow makes the kiln steam. It's the warmest room in Talmberg.",
	"I stay home. Father says carts break more pots than the wardens save.",
	"A mug went from the shelf last week. Father says I left the door open.",
	"Throwing small pots, sweeping, keeping the kiln lit. Father lets me do the neat ones.",
	"Father decides that, and he's at the wheel.",
	"Come at {meal}. Father will say it's plain. He's right.",
	"Wedging clay and stacking pots for the kiln.",
	"Morning. Stay clear of the brown cow, she's in a mood.",
	"It's nothing. Cows never thank me, so I'm out of practice.",
	"Ondrej Pastyr. I mind the village cows and a few sheep.",
	"Magda and the two children, Jindrich and Veronika. We're five in one house, with the byre next door.",
	"The children are good at the byre work, which is a mercy. Magda wants more for them.",
	"The brown cow won't eat. She stands and stares at the wall, same as last {season}.",
	"Dry pasture goes thin. I'll move them down to the stream.",
	"Rain is good for grass. Bad for hooves and a man's temper.",
	"In fog I can't see the herd, so I count twice and listen for bells.",
	"Snow keeps them in the byre, eating what I've saved.",
	"I drive the herd along the road to the high meadow. The wardens keep it, but wolves don't read their orders.",
	"A cow gone from the pasture is a month's milk lost. Name the thief, and the guards bind him.",
	"Fed and counted, twice a day. A cow doesn't leave a note when she wanders.",
	"Feed's short, but I've no job to spare you today. Ask in a day or two.",
	"There's bread and cheese at {meal}. Magda won't turn anyone away.",
	"Counting the herd again.",
	"Not the mud, please, the children just scrubbed. Good day.",
	"That's kind of you. It's rare.",
	"Magda, the herdsman's wife. I make the cheese.",
	"Ondrej, Jindrich and Veronika. And the cows, who eat better than we do.",
	"I'd like the children to learn their letters. Ondrej says the cows don't read.",
	"The count's men leave us little cheese. That's the news. It's old.",
	"Dry day. I'll set the cheeses on the sill.",
	"Wet again. The cheese rinds sweat, and the children smell of wet sheep.",
	"Fog. The little ones can't find the byre. I can't find the little ones.",
	"With snow on the ground everyone stays in, and I count heads at every meal.",
	"I send the cheese with Ondrej. He says the road is safe enough.",
	"A neighbour lost a hen last month. Whoever saw it should have said so. The guards would have done the rest.",
	"A pot on, a cheese to turn and a child with a sore knee. We manage.",
	"There's nothing I can hand you today. Ask Ondrej, he usually has a job.",
	"Come at {meal}. Bread and whey, nothing grand. You're welcome to it.",
	"Turning cheeses and washing small faces.",
	"Hello! Did you come from the wood? I saw a wolf there.",
	"Oh, that's nothing! Father says I talk too much.",
	"Jindrich. I help with the sheep.",
	"Father, Mother and Veronika. And a cow called Brownie.",
	"Veronika cries when a calf is born. I tell her it's only a cow.",
	"There's a big grey wolf at the edge of the wood. Father says it's a dog, but it isn't.",
	"Dry! We can take the sheep up the hill.",
	"Rain, so we're in the byre. I count sheep until I fall asleep.",
	"Fog, and the sheep vanish. I have to listen for their bells.",
	"Snow. The sheep don't mind, but my fingers do.",
	"Father says never go on the road alone. Wolves and bandits, he says.",
	"If I saw one, I'd tell Father first, then the guards.",
	"Hiding in the hay. Mother says I'm too old for it. I'm not.",
	"I'm not allowed to hand out jobs. Ask Father.",
	"Mother makes cheese! You can have some at {meal}.",
	"Watching the sheep. For wolves.",
	"Hello. The calf licked my face. Do you want to see?",
	"You're nice. The calf likes you too.",
	"Veronika. I like the calves.",
	"Mother makes cheese and Father counts cows. Jindrich is my brother.",
	"Jindrich tells me wolves eat girls. Mother says he's lying.",
	"A calf was born. It licked my face. That's the news.",
	"Dry means the calf can go out and I can go with her.",
	"When it rains the calf stays inside, and so do I.",
	"I can't see the calf from the door when it's foggy.",
	"Snow. I put straw down for the calves.",
	"I never go on the road. Father says it's for carts.",
	"Thieves go in a cage and people look at them. I'd look away.",
	"I play with the calf. Mother says wash my hands first.",
	"I'm too small to give out work. Ask Mother.",
	"We have milk at {meal}. You can have my cup.",
	"Feeding the calf with my hand.",
	"Good day, friend. Mind the windfalls, the wasps love them.",
	"Not at all. An old man likes to be useful.",
	"Vaclav Zahradnik. I keep the orchard and the hives.",
	"Anna and I have sat at this table forty years. The children are grown and gone.",
	"Anna keeps me honest. She says my apples are better than my stories.",
	"The bees swarmed again, the third time this {season}. They don't like the smith's smoke.",
	"The bees are out from dawn and the apples are filling. I can't complain.",
	"Little for me to do outside, with the bees kept in the hive by the rain.",
	"Fog in the orchard. The trees look like old men waiting.",
	"Snow on the branches. I'll shake the weight off the young trees.",
	"My apples go to market by cart, so I'm glad the road has wardens. I stay put.",
	"Boys lift apples. I let them, mostly. A real thief gets named to the guards.",
	"Slow and sweet. The south tree has worm, so that one goes into the cider.",
	"Wax for the jars, I'd say, but I've nothing for you today. Another time.",
	"Anna keeps hens, so there'll be eggs at {meal}. Sit down with us.",
	"Checking the hives and picking up windfalls.",
	"God give you good day. Come in out of the wasps.",
	"Kind of you to say. Bless you.",
	"Anna, Vaclav's wife. I keep the hens and the house.",
	"It's just the two of us now. Forty years at one table.",
	"Vaclav says the table held up better than we did. I say it's the same oak and the same stubbornness.",
	"The south apple tree has the worm. Vaclav won't cut it, but he'll cider it.",
	"A good day to beat the rugs and air the loft.",
	"I light the hearth when it rains and mend by the window.",
	"I say an extra prayer in fog for anyone on the road.",
	"Nobody should be out in snow without a fire at home.",
	"I hear the roads are safe if you keep to them. At my age I keep to the doorstep.",
	"The guards deal with thieves. I'll say a prayer for him, and a few words to the hens.",
	"Eggs, apples and each other. Enough for us.",
	"Nothing before the apples are ready for the brewer. Come back then.",
	"Stale bread, fresh eggs. It's what we have at {meal}, and you're welcome.",
	"Feeding the hens and sorting apples for the brewer.",
	"Watch your feet, the dust stings.",
	"Hm. Stone never says that, so it's pleasant when folk do.",
	"Dietrich Zednik, master mason. The castle wall is mine to mend, whether they pay or not.",
	"Katerina is my wife. Ulrich lodges here and learns the trade. He thinks he knows it already.",
	"Katerina says there's stone dust in the bread. She's right, and I can't stop it.",
	"There's a crack in the castle wall you could put your fist in. It's been getting worse since the steward's grandfather.",
	"Good mortar weather. We'll mix all day if the lad keeps up.",
	"Mortar won't set in the rain, so the wall waits.",
	"Fog on the scaffold. I won't send a man up there blind.",
	"Snow stops everything. Stone cracks if it freezes wet.",
	"Stone comes by cart. I've lost a block to a ditch, never to a bandit. Credit to the wardens.",
	"Cut stone goes missing off sites. A man hauling it away should be named to the guards.",
	"Slow and heavy. I measure three times, cut once and curse the count's steward.",
	"Not today. When I need blocks fetched, you'll hear of it.",
	"Come at {meal}. Shake the dust off first, Katerina's particular.",
	"Marking blocks for the castle wall.",
	"Do come in, but beat the dust off first.",
	"Thank you, that's kind. It's been a week of dust.",
	"Katerina, wife of the mason. I keep the house. I sweep the stone out of it.",
	"Dietrich and Ulrich, and me. The dust belongs to all three.",
	"Ulrich eats like a horse and works like a boy. Dietrich says it'll pass.",
	"The count didn't pay for the chapel steps. I'll believe the wall money when I see it.",
	"Dry day. I'll shake out every blanket.",
	"The washing is hopeless in this rain.",
	"Fog makes the dust cling to everything in the house.",
	"The house is quiet when it snows, except for the dust.",
	"To the market and back is all I go. They tell me the road is safe enough.",
	"The poor steal from the poor, mostly. If you saw it, tell the guards. It's the gaol or a fine.",
	"Meals, sweeping, a hearth to keep going. Dietrich would say that's all a house needs.",
	"Nothing I can send you for today. The larder is full enough.",
	"Come at {meal}, if you don't mind stone dust in the bread.",
	"Sweeping stone dust, again.",
	"Hello. Careful, that block is mine and it's not finished.",
	"Cheers. Don't tell the master, he'll think I'm soft.",
	"Ulrich, journeyman. I lodge here and learn the stone.",
	"I've no family here. The master and his wife are as near as I've got.",
	"I'd like my own mark one day. The master says not until I cut a clean arch.",
	"The steward says the crack has been there since his grandfather. The master says it has grown.",
	"It's clear, so I'm out with chisel and mallet until dark.",
	"Rain sends me into the shed to cut templates.",
	"Fog on the wall. I can't see the line.",
	"Snow. My hands go stiff, and the mallet slips.",
	"I came to Talmberg by road. It was fine, but I kept to the middle.",
	"Tools get taken before stone does, so I sleep with my chisel under my pillow.",
	"Cutting, hauling, listening to the master. Hard on the hands, and he's usually right.",
	"The master hasn't given me anything to pass on today.",
	"Come and eat at {meal}. The mistress is a good cook, whatever the master says.",
	"Cutting the same arch for the third time.",
	"Hm. Day. Don't stand under the felled ones.",
	"Aye. Fair enough, and I'll remember it.",
	"Jan Lesak. Forester. I mark the trees and keep the poachers out.",
	"My brother Hanzl. We share the house by the woods.",
	"Hanzl falls asleep at the clamp. But he's my brother. The only one I've got.",
	"Someone has been felling oak in the count's wood. Fresh stumps by the stream.",
	"Firm paths in dry weather. A good day to walk the boundary.",
	"The woods drip in this rain and the tracks fill with mud.",
	"Fog in the trees. I stay by the stream and listen.",
	"Snow shows tracks. Boar, deer, boots. I read them all.",
	"The wardens keep the main road. Off it, bandits wait in the woods. I should know, I walk them.",
	"Poachers and wood thieves. Name them to the guards and they're bound, then fined or put in the stocks.",
	"Boar in the thickets, poachers after the count's deer, and a brother who sleeps at the clamp. I watch all three.",
	"Not today. Rope, maybe, tomorrow.",
	"Sausage and bread at {meal}. Nothing more.",
	"Walking the boundary and counting the fresh stumps.",
	"Hello. Careful with your boots, the clamp is still smoking.",
	"Welcome. A thank you is rare out here.",
	"Hanzl, charcoal burner. I live in smoke, so excuse the smell.",
	"Jan's my brother. We've no wives, no children. Just the woods.",
	"He says I fall asleep at the clamp. He's right. A burn takes days and a man needs rest.",
	"The clamp burned too hot last night. Half of it is ash.",
	"A good day for a burn. I'll stack more tomorrow.",
	"Rain would put out the clamp. I'll watch it all night.",
	"Fog and smoke together. I can't tell which is which.",
	"The clamp keeps me warm in snow. That's the only good in it.",
	"I take charcoal to the smith by the road. The wardens ride it, and I've not been robbed yet.",
	"Charcoal's black, so a thief is easy to spot. Give his name to the guards, and they bind him.",
	"Pine in, charcoal out. A burn takes days, and it's gone in one afternoon at the forge.",
	"Kindling for the clamp, I'll want tomorrow. Nothing today.",
	"Come at {meal}. Jan will complain, but it's a pot big enough for three.",
	"Watching the clamp. Don't breathe too deep.",
	"Stand still a moment, I'm measuring you by eye.",
	"Most kind. Customers mostly thank me when the coat fits.",
	"Matous Krejci, tailor. I made that coat the clerk is wearing, and he owes me for it.",
	"Lida, my wife, who dyes and sews. We've no children, only customers.",
	"Lida's hands are blue to the wrist. She says it's the dye. I say it's the cheapest gloves in Talmberg.",
	"The reeve's clerk wants a black lined coat by Sunday. He'll pay late, but he pays.",
	"Dyed cloth sets best hung out in dry air. Lida's already pegged the blue.",
	"The blue runs when rain hits the line, and I lose a day.",
	"Nobody wants a fitting in fog, so I sew by the window.",
	"Everyone wants a warm cloak once it snows. The line outside my door is long.",
	"Cloth travels well if the road is safe. I trust the wardens that far.",
	"A bolt of cloth is easy to take. Tell the guards if you see it go, and they'll bind the man.",
	"A coat is a week's work. The black one for the clerk is trickier than it looks.",
	"The shop has what it needs. No work today.",
	"Come at {meal}. Lida makes a good soup, and she won't let you near the dye.",
	"Cutting the black coat for the clerk.",
	"Hello, dear. Mind the dye pot, it's hot.",
	"Dear, that's rare. Not many bother.",
	"Lida. I dye the cloth and my husband cuts it.",
	"Matous and me, and a house full of fabric. We've no children.",
	"Matous never stops. I tell him to eat. He asks if the cloth is ready.",
	"The weaver's daughter brought the wrong skein again. Be kind, her father works her hard.",
	"No rain, so the blue goes out on the pegs.",
	"Rain washes the dye out. I'll boil the pot indoors.",
	"The yellow won't set in fog and my hands ache.",
	"It's snowing, and my hands are blue to the wrist. The dye pot is the warmest thing in the house.",
	"I stay in Talmberg. The cloth gets through, so I suppose the wardens earn their pay.",
	"Skeins go missing off the line. If you saw someone, tell the guards.",
	"I dye, rinse and dry all day. Blue sells and brown doesn't, so it's woad for me.",
	"Not today. The onion skins can wait.",
	"You may eat with us at {meal}. Keep your hands clear of the dye pot.",
	"Boiling the dye pot and checking the colours.",
	"Good day. Fine cloth, spices, salt? Or just passing through?",
	"A pleasure. I do remember the folk who say it.",
	"Konrad Kupec, merchant of Talmberg. If you want something, I know a man who has it.",
	"Gertruda is my wife, Albrecht my son, Elsa my daughter. And a servant girl who sees everything.",
	"Albrecht wants to take the cart to the fair. I say when he can judge cloth by touch alone.",
	"Salt's up a third since the toll bar went up on the old road. The count owes us for the wine again.",
	"Dry roads. The carts roll and the goods sell.",
	"In rain the carts bog down and I wait on the goods.",
	"No cart will move in fog. I sit and watch the ledger.",
	"Snow. The market empties and my ledger fills.",
	"Safe enough on the main road. I've lost goods to bandits off it, so I keep my route short.",
	"Thieves cost merchants dearly. A witnessed theft goes on the roll of the wanted. I'd like a few names there.",
	"Coin is tight and people haggle over every penny this year.",
	"I've nothing to send out today. The ledger is balanced for once.",
	"Gertruda keeps a rich table. Come at {meal}.",
	"Counting salt and checking the ledger.",
	"Good day. You look like someone with business. Do sit.",
	"You'd be surprised how few bother to say it.",
	"Gertruda, Konrad's wife. I keep the books he'd rather forget.",
	"Konrad, Albrecht, Elsa. And a household that costs more than it earns.",
	"Elsa has a ribbon from the reeve's clerk's household. I told her to return it.",
	"The count owes us for the wine again. Konrad grumbles. I write it down.",
	"The cart leaves early on a day like this.",
	"The cart waits in the rain and the bread goes stale.",
	"Nothing travels in fog, so we wait.",
	"We sit inside in snow and count what we've sold.",
	"Konrad takes the main road. We've never lost the cart, but we've lost goods.",
	"If someone steals from a merchant's cart, we say so. The guards put him on the roll.",
	"Nobody's hungry here and we pay our dues. Coin is tight, but we manage.",
	"Nothing needs carrying today. Check with Albrecht, he keeps the notes.",
	"Come and eat with us at {meal}. We keep a good table.",
	"Writing up the accounts. They never balance on the first try.",
	"Hello! Have you been to the fair? There's a spice stall that sells real pepper.",
	"My pleasure. Father says courtesy sells.",
	"Albrecht, the merchant's son. I'm learning the trade.",
	"Father, Mother and Elsa. And a cart I want to take to the fair.",
	"Father says I can't judge cloth yet. I say I can, but I haven't told him.",
	"A trader from the south has real pepper. Father says the weights are false.",
	"A good day for the cart, and for dreaming about the fair.",
	"The cart's stuck in the yard in this rain, and so am I.",
	"Fog means no road, so the fair waits.",
	"In snow I sort the ledger.",
	"I want to ride the roads and see the fair. Father says stay on the main one.",
	"I'd chase a thief myself. Father says that's why I'm not driving the cart yet.",
	"Learning prices, weights and who owes whom. It's more than it sounds.",
	"Father hasn't given me anything to send out. Ask me after the fair.",
	"Come to eat at {meal}. Mother always sets too much out.",
	"Going through the ledger and thinking about the fair.",
	"Hello. The reeve's clerk's wife is cross about something. Did you hear?",
	"That was nicely said. I'll remember you.",
	"Elsa. The merchant's daughter. I know all the gossip.",
	"Father, Mother, Albrecht. And a house full of ledgers.",
	"Mother tells me what not to say. I say it anyway, if it's good.",
	"The reeve's clerk's wife sent a note. She's angry about something, as usual.",
	"It's dry, so I'll walk down to the market.",
	"I'll sit by the window and watch who goes by in the rain.",
	"Nobody comes by in fog, so there's nothing to see.",
	"Snow. I'll read by the fire.",
	"The main road is safe, Father says. I don't go far.",
	"If I saw a theft I'd tell the guards. Then everyone would hear, because I'd tell them.",
	"I sew and watch and listen. A merchant's house hears everything.",
	"I've nothing to give you. Ask Mother, she keeps the notes.",
	"Come and eat at {meal}. Mother won't mind, she likes a full table.",
	"Sewing by the window and listening.",
	"Eh? Oh. Good day, lad. Sit, if you can find a stool that isn't a barrel.",
	"You're a polite one. That's rarer than good oak.",
	"Bartolomej. Martin's father. The better cooper, whatever he tells you.",
	"Martin is my only son. His mother's gone, so we keep each other.",
	"He says my cough is worse. It's the damp in the cellar, but he fusses.",
	"Martin's hoops are too loose. I can see the light through them. He says it's the window.",
	"My chest likes a dry day, at least.",
	"Rain gets into the bones. I'll sit by the fire and criticise the staves.",
	"Fog brings the cough back. I'm staying in.",
	"In my day we hauled barrels on sledges in the snow and thought nothing of it.",
	"Barrels roll well on a good road. I hear the wardens keep it. In my day we kept it ourselves.",
	"Staves walk off. A thief who takes oak deserves the stocks. Tell the guards.",
	"Barrels held twenty years in my day. Now the hoops come loose at the first frost.",
	"Ask Martin about staves. All I can offer is a stool and a lecture.",
	"Martin cooks at {meal}, which is a mercy and a danger. Sit, if you dare.",
	"Sitting by the fire and watching Martin work.",
	"Good day. Stand back from the staves, they spring.",
	"Thanks. Few notice the work that holds the ale.",
	"Martin Bednar, cooper. If it holds liquid in Talmberg, I probably made it.",
	"My father lives with me. He's old and he coughs, and he never stops talking.",
	"Father says my hoops are loose. I don't tell him he's right.",
	"The brewer wants six barrels before harvest. I'm short of staves.",
	"Dry air shrinks the wood. I soak the staves before I raise a barrel.",
	"The wood swells in the rain, which tightens the hoops. I'm working in the shed.",
	"Staves sweat in fog. I wait it out.",
	"In snow I work close to the fire. Barrels set slowly.",
	"Staves come by road from the woodcutter. The wardens keep it clear.",
	"Hoops and staves walk off. If you see it, name the man to the guards.",
	"A barrel is forty staves and two hoops, and every one of them has to be right.",
	"No call for you today. The shed's stocked and the staves are seasoned.",
	"Father will tell you how it used to be at {meal}. Bring patience, I'll bring the bread.",
	"Raising a barrel for the brewer.",
	"Ah, a thirsty face. Welcome to the brewhouse.",
	"Think nothing of it. A good turn deserves a good pint.",
	"Jiri Sladek, brewer. If you've drunk ale in Talmberg, you've drunk mine.",
	"Bozena is my wife and the alewife. The twins are Hana and Jana.",
	"The twins drink when I'm not looking. They'll make fine brewers or terrible wives.",
	"The innkeeper says the ale's sour. He says a lot of things, and he pays late.",
	"The grain keeps in this air and the mash ferments well.",
	"Damp barley in the rain makes the malt slow.",
	"The yeast gets lazy in fog. I'll wait for it.",
	"Snow suits me. Ale keeps in the cold.",
	"Barrels go by cart. The wardens watch the road, and I watch the cart.",
	"A barrel is easy to roll off in the dark. If you saw it, say so to the guards.",
	"Mash, boil, ferment, wait. Then somebody says it's sour.",
	"Nothing you can help with today. The mash is in and the barley sacks are full.",
	"Bozena makes a good pottage, and there's always ale. Come at {meal}.",
	"Stirring the mash and tasting. Only tasting.",
	"Hello, dear. Careful of the floor, it's sticky.",
	"Thank you, love. The kindness is worth more than the coin.",
	"Bozena, Jiri's wife. They call me the alewife, and they're right.",
	"Jiri. The twins Hana and Jana. We make ale and noise.",
	"I tell the girls no more ale till they're married. They drink it anyway.",
	"The last brew was sour. Jiri left the mash too long, but he won't admit it.",
	"The mash stays steady when it's dry. I can breathe.",
	"The cellar smells like wet bread when it rains.",
	"Nobody comes in on a foggy day. I'll scrub the barrels.",
	"In snow it's hot ale for everyone, and they're glad of it.",
	"The carts go out with ale and come back with coin. The road must be safe enough.",
	"Tap thieves and tab dodgers. Say so to the guards and they'll bind them.",
	"We brew, we sell, we count. The inn drinks more than it pays, as usual.",
	"Nothing today, love. I've hops enough for the week.",
	"The girls will set a place for you at {meal}. Mind the floor, it's sticky.",
	"Counting jugs and shouting at the twins.",
	"Hello. Jana drank from the barrel. It wasn't me.",
	"You're welcome. Mother says I should say that.",
	"I'm Hana. I'm the elder twin, by a bit.",
	"Mother, Father and my sister. We all smell of malt.",
	"Jana copies me. I don't mind much.",
	"Jana drank from the barrel when Father wasn't looking. I told.",
	"Dry! We can play in the yard.",
	"When it rains the cellar floods a bit and we're not allowed down.",
	"In fog we can't see the barrels from the door.",
	"When it snows Mother puts hot ale out and we get a sip.",
	"Roads are for carts, says Father. I'm not allowed near.",
	"People who steal get caught. I'd tell Mother.",
	"Hiding behind the barrels. Jana always finds me.",
	"I'm too little to give out jobs. Ask Father.",
	"There's pottage at {meal}. Jana will sit on your left. She steals from your bowl.",
	"Sweeping the brewhouse floor. Jana is meant to help.",
	"Hello! I did not drink from the barrel.",
	"Thank you! You're nice.",
	"I'm Jana. I'm the younger twin, and I'm faster.",
	"Hana is my sister. We share everything, even trouble.",
	"Hana tells on me. I tell on her back.",
	"Hana says I drank from the barrel. I only tasted it. It was sour.",
	"Dry! I can run up the hill.",
	"Rain! I can splash in the yard.",
	"I hide in fog and Hana can't find me.",
	"Snow! I make little cakes of it.",
	"I like the road. Mother says stay close to the gate.",
	"If I saw a thief I'd run and tell. Probably. After I finished running.",
	"Racing Hana. I win. Mostly.",
	"I'm too small. Mother has all the jobs.",
	"Mother lets us have a sip of ale at {meal}. It's sour, but it's allowed.",
	"Chasing Hana round the barrels.",
	"Good day. Keep clear of the anvil, it bites.",
	"Good of you. A smith hears more hammer than thanks.",
	"Vojtech Kovar, smith. If it's iron in Talmberg, it passed through this forge.",
	"Marie is my wife. Hynek is the apprentice. He lives with us. Burnt hands, mostly.",
	"Marie fusses over Hynek's burns. I tell her a burn teaches faster than a lecture.",
	"The count's men brought twelve spears to mend. They'll pay in thanks, and thanks don't buy charcoal.",
	"The forge draws well on a dry day, and the iron takes the heat clean.",
	"The forge roars under the rain on the roof. I don't mind it.",
	"Fog drifts in through the door. I keep the fire hot and the hammer busy.",
	"In snow mine's the only warm room in Talmberg.",
	"Iron goes out by cart under the wardens' eye. Nails travel better than most things.",
	"Tools walk off from forges. If you see a man with my hammer, name him to the guards.",
	"Iron takes years to learn and a bad heat spoils it. I'm still learning.",
	"Charcoal's in and the nails are made. I've no work to hand you today.",
	"Marie puts a pot on, and she won't hear a refusal. Come at {meal}.",
	"Mending the count's spears at the anvil.",
	"Good day. Don't touch anything hot, which is everything.",
	"That's kind. A smith's household doesn't hear it much.",
	"Marie, the smith's wife. I keep the house and the burns bandaged.",
	"Vojtech and Hynek. Hynek's not mine, but he eats like he is.",
	"Vojtech shouts at Hynek. I fix the damage afterwards.",
	"The new tax is robbery, Vojtech says. I tell him to say nothing to anyone.",
	"Fine for drying. The washing will still smell of smoke.",
	"The wash won't dry in the rain. The soot gets everywhere.",
	"Fog mixes with the charcoal smoke and I can't see the yard.",
	"Snow. The forge is the warm spot, so everyone's underfoot.",
	"I don't go far. The wardens keep the road clear, I'm told.",
	"A neighbour lost a pan. If you saw something, tell the guards.",
	"We get by. The forge pays, the pot's on, and Hynek's hands heal.",
	"Vojtech's the one who knows what's short, not me. I've no work for you today.",
	"You'll sit by the fire at {meal}. Mind the sparks. The whole house smells of smoke.",
	"Bandaging Hynek's hands and stirring the pot.",
	"Hello. Sorry about the soot, I'm still learning.",
	"Oh. Thanks. Master doesn't say that much.",
	"Hynek, the smith's apprentice. I work the bellows and the quench.",
	"The master and mistress keep me. My own people are far off.",
	"The mistress treats my burns. The master tells me to do it right next time.",
	"I quenched an axe head too early and it cracked. The master made me forge another.",
	"The charcoal burns clean on a dry day.",
	"The forge is warm in the rain and I'm glad to be inside.",
	"Fog hides the anvil from the door.",
	"I'm happy to stand at the forge when it snows.",
	"Nobody bothered me on the way to Talmberg.",
	"Somebody took a rasp from the bench. I'm afraid the master thinks it was me.",
	"Bellows, quench, carry, sweep. My arms ache, but the master says it'll pass.",
	"I'm not allowed to hand out work. Ask the master.",
	"The mistress cooks well. You'll like it at {meal}, if you can sit near the fire.",
	"Working the bellows and trying not to burn anything.",
	"Good day. Excuse the smell, it's the goat.",
	"Kind of you. That's not said often on this strip.",
	"Prokop Dvorak. I farm a strip of turnips and feed five mouths and a goat.",
	"Ruzena, Adam and Ida. The goat's practically family.",
	"The children are thin. I'd feed them better if the soil would let me.",
	"The church roof leaks right over our bench. Even God doesn't want us dry.",
	"The turnips need rain, but a dry spell is easier on the roof.",
	"At least the turnips drink when it rains.",
	"In fog the goat wanders off and I follow her.",
	"Turnips keep in the ground under snow. That's our supper.",
	"I seldom leave the strip. The wardens ride the road, they say.",
	"A turnip stolen is a supper lost. I'd tell the guards, if I knew who.",
	"Turnips, mostly, on a small thin strip. We eat what grows.",
	"Turnip seed is what I'd want, and I haven't got it. No work for you today.",
	"It's turnips at {meal}, but you're welcome to a share.",
	"Weeding the turnips. The goat eats the weeds.",
	"God give you good day. You've caught me with the pot.",
	"Bless you. We don't have much to offer.",
	"Ruzena, Prokop's wife. I stretch the pot.",
	"Prokop, Adam and Ida. And a goat that eats shoes.",
	"The children are growing, and I can't keep up with the shoes. The goat helps.",
	"The chandler's wife gave us two candles. I've said a prayer for her.",
	"A dry day. The washing will be stiff, but clean.",
	"The roof leaks when it rains, so I put out every pot.",
	"The children vanish behind the hen coop in fog.",
	"When it snows we huddle and say our prayers.",
	"I don't go on the road. We have our strip and the church.",
	"A neighbour lost eggs. If you saw it, tell the guards. I'll pray for him anyway.",
	"We eat what there is. Prokop works the strip, I stretch the pot, and God provides the rest.",
	"A hen has stopped laying. I'm afraid there's nothing for you.",
	"Come at {meal}. You'll find a bowl, though not much in it.",
	"Stretching the pot and praying for a better harvest.",
	"Hello. The goat ate my shoe.",
	"Don't mention it! I have one shoe now.",
	"I'm Adam. I'm the oldest.",
	"Mother, Father and Ida. And the goat, who's a menace.",
	"Father says I'm a good worker. I think he's just hungry.",
	"The goat ate my shoe. Then it ate Ida's. It's a very bad goat.",
	"Dry. I'll go barefoot, like a king.",
	"The shoe's gone, so I stay in when it rains.",
	"I can't find the goat in fog.",
	"My feet are cold in the snow. The goat ate my shoes.",
	"Not far. The road's for carts, Father says.",
	"A thief took a hen last week. I'd have caught him, if I'd been there.",
	"Chasing the goat. It's faster than I am.",
	"I'm too young. Ask Father.",
	"Come and eat at {meal}. It's turnips. The goat can't have yours.",
	"Chasing the goat out of the turnips.",
	"Hello. I only have one shoe.",
	"You can hold the goat, if you like.",
	"I'm Ida. I'm the second child.",
	"Adam, Mother and Father. And the goat.",
	"The goat likes me. Everybody else it eats.",
	"The goat ate both my shoes. Mother says I must wait till harvest.",
	"Dry. I can sit outside.",
	"Mother puts a pot where it drips when it rains.",
	"I hold on to the goat in fog.",
	"My feet get cold when it snows.",
	"I never go on the road.",
	"Thieves go in a cage. I'd cry.",
	"Sitting with the goat. She's warm.",
	"I can't give you work. I'm tiny.",
	"Come to supper. It's turnips. The goat sits by the door.",
	"Feeding the goat so it doesn't eat the other shoe.",
	"Oh, good day. Mind the dye, it stains.",
	"Noted, and I'll put it down against the blue.",
	"Vendula Barvirova, the elder sister. We dye and weave.",
	"Alzbeta is my sister. We keep this house between us.",
	"Alzbeta wants to marry. I tell her the bowyer smells of glue.",
	"The woad came out green again. Green! Nobody wants green.",
	"Cloth dries clean and fast. Even the green one.",
	"In rain the woad vats stay cool. The cloth stays wet.",
	"The colours won't set until the fog lifts.",
	"I stay by the vat in snow and keep it warm.",
	"Cloth goes to Talmberg by road. The wardens watch it, I'm told.",
	"Skeins go missing from the line. Tell the guards if you see someone taking them.",
	"Years to learn the vat, and this woad batch went wrong in a moment.",
	"The vat's busy and Alzbeta's at the loom, so no work today.",
	"Alzbeta cooks at {meal}. She's a better cook than she admits.",
	"Stirring the woad vat and hoping it turns blue.",
	"Hello! Don't mind the green, it's an accident.",
	"Thank you! That's very kind.",
	"Alzbeta, the younger sister. I weave. I talk to customers.",
	"Vendula is my sister. We run the dye house together, mostly arguing.",
	"I'd like to marry before {season} ends. Vendula says nobody's good enough.",
	"A weaver from Talmberg came asking for blue. Pavel, the one with the daughter.",
	"The skeins dry on the line in an afternoon. I've nothing to complain of.",
	"The blue runs in the rain and Vendula sulks.",
	"In fog I stay at the loom and gossip with the door.",
	"The dye house is the warmest in the village when it snows.",
	"Now and then I go to Talmberg. The road's fine, and the wardens nod.",
	"A skein went off the line. If you saw him, tell the guards.",
	"Weaving, listening, wondering who'll marry whom.",
	"I've a blanket on the loom and Vendula's got the vat. Nothing for you today.",
	"Come to eat at {meal}. Vendula won't mind, much.",
	"Weaving a blanket for the crofter's baby.",
	"Good day. Watch the spokes, they're not set.",
	"Wheels turn better with a kind word. Mine do, anyway.",
	"Benes Kolar, wheelwright. If it rolls in the village, I made it or I'll mend it.",
	"Bohdana is my wife. Kristof, my brother, makes bows and lives with us.",
	"Kristof is in love with a dyer. He doesn't know which.",
	"Every cart in the village needs a new wheel and nobody can pay. The steam trough has cracked.",
	"Wheels set tight in dry weather. The carts roll out and I get paid.",
	"The rims warp in rain. I wait.",
	"No cart moves in fog, so there's no business.",
	"Snow means sledges. I'll mend a runner.",
	"Wheels break on bad roads. The wardens keep the main one, so it's the side tracks that cost me.",
	"Wheels get taken off carts left out at night. Tell the guards if you see it.",
	"A crooked felly and the whole wheel wobbles. It takes years to cut them true.",
	"Nothing today. Planks and nails will be wanted tomorrow.",
	"Bohdana feeds everyone who stands still. Come at {meal}.",
	"Fitting an iron rim to a cart wheel.",
	"Hello, dear. Come in, there's sawdust everywhere.",
	"It's nice when someone says it. It's a loud house.",
	"Bohdana, the wheelwright's wife. I keep two brothers fed.",
	"Benes and Kristof. They argue about wood, and I referee.",
	"Kristof should marry. The dyer sister asked after him again.",
	"The dyer sister keeps asking after Kristof. He says which one. I say does it matter.",
	"It's a good day to sweep the sawdust out of the house.",
	"The shop smells of wet oak when it rains.",
	"In fog I can't see the dyers' house from the door.",
	"We keep the fire in when it snows and the men sulk about the cart business.",
	"Benes drives to Talmberg. He says the road's quiet.",
	"Tools go missing. If you see someone with Benes's chisel, tell the guards.",
	"We eat, we sweep, we mend. A wheelwright's house is never quiet.",
	"Ask Benes. I've got nothing today.",
	"Kristof won't mind the extra mouth. Come at {meal}.",
	"Sweeping up the shavings, again.",
	"Hello. Careful, the stave is green.",
	"Aye, thanks. Not much talk in a workshop.",
	"Kristof, the bowyer. I make bows for the hunters.",
	"My brother Benes and his wife. I've no wife of my own, they remind me.",
	"Bohdana says a dyer asked after me. I don't know which. I hope the one who doesn't scold.",
	"The hunter wants a yew bow. Real yew. I can't find it this side of the river.",
	"The staves season evenly in this air. I'm glad of it.",
	"A bow stave warps in the rain if I'm careless.",
	"In fog I scrape a stave and hope.",
	"Cold bends yew badly, so I wait out the snow.",
	"I walk the road to the hunter's. The wardens keep it and the hunter keeps the woods.",
	"A stave was taken from the rack. If you hear of it, tell the guards.",
	"Scraping, bending, waxing. A good bow takes a year and a bad one takes a day.",
	"I've no stave to fetch, and Benes has the nails. No work for you.",
	"Bohdana makes extra. Come at {meal}.",
	"Scraping a bow stave and thinking about the dyer.",
	"God give you good day. Warm bread, if you've got a coin.",
	"That's kind of you. A kind word is welcome at the oven.",
	"Svatava Peckova, widow. I keep the village oven.",
	"Bohumil is my son. My husband is in the churchyard.",
	"Bohumil can't read, but he can bake. I bless him for it.",
	"Every loaf in this village passes through my oven. They still bargain over it.",
	"Good flour weather, thank God.",
	"The wood is damp in rain. I burn extra and pray the loaves rise.",
	"The village is quiet in fog. I say my prayers while the dough proves.",
	"In snow I pray for travellers and bake for them.",
	"I don't travel. Bohumil carries bread to the Hare, and the road is kind to him.",
	"A stolen loaf is a hungry man. The guards will bind him all the same.",
	"The village oven is busy from Prime. There's never a loaf left over.",
	"The wood's stacked and my back is better, love. No work today.",
	"Bless this house and the oven that keeps it. Come at {meal}.",
	"Kneading the day's dough and saying my prayers over it.",
	"Hello. Flour on the floor, mind your step.",
	"Thanks. Mother says courtesy is half the baking.",
	"Bohumil, the baker's son. I carry the bread and the burns.",
	"Mother is the baker. I'm her son. Father's in the churchyard.",
	"Mother can't lift the wood any more. I do it, and she tells me how.",
	"The chandler's wife paid in candles again. We can't eat candles, but I can't read by them either.",
	"Dry weather means no cover on the baskets.",
	"I run when the rain hits the baskets.",
	"I'm slow with the bread in fog. Nobody minds.",
	"In snow I carry the bread with a blanket over it.",
	"I take pretzels to the Hare. The road is short and safe.",
	"Someone lifted a basket of rings. Tell the guards if you see it.",
	"Carrying wood, shaping loaves, running baskets. Mother does the thinking.",
	"Nothing today. Mother will tell me what's short.",
	"Come at {meal}. Mother will make room by the oven.",
	"Carrying a basket of pretzel rings to the Hare.",
	"God be with you. You look tired. Sit.",
	"Bless you, child. That's more than most say.",
	"Radmila. They call me the herb widow. I tend the sick and bring the children.",
	"I live alone. My husband is in the churchyard thirty years now.",
	"I deliver half the children in the village, so I have a great many godchildren.",
	"Another child is coming in the crofter's house. That's the sixth, and they've little to feed them.",
	"The herbs dry well in this air. I'll gather what I can before it turns.",
	"Rain brings coughs. Somebody will knock on my door before nightfall.",
	"The fever grows in fog. I keep the fire hot and the pot on.",
	"In snow I'm called out for chills and frostbite. Keep warm.",
	"I go where I'm called, and the main road has wardens. I carry a stick anyway.",
	"Somebody took my yarrow from the sill. Tell the guards, but I forgive him.",
	"Coughs and fevers, as always. Look after yourself and you'll stay out of my hands.",
	"Nothing today, child. The shelves are stocked and the herbs are in.",
	"A healer's table is poor, but it's honest. Come at {meal}.",
	"Sorting herbs and mixing remedies.",
	"Good day. Mind the tallow, it spits.",
	"Obliged. A civil word is cheaper than a candle.",
	"Emil Svickar, chandler. Half the light in this village comes from my vats.",
	"Berta, my wife. Rozalie and Viktor, our children. We're the richest house in the village, so everyone says.",
	"Rozalie has a baker's son after her. I'll listen when he can read the price of a candle.",
	"The church wants two hundred candles for the feast. Two hundred! They'll pay in prayers and the odd coin.",
	"Wicks stay straight when it's dry. Good for dipping.",
	"Rain makes the tallow sweat. I'll dip tomorrow.",
	"Fog and candles go together. I'm selling more than I can make.",
	"Snow brings the orders in. Everyone wants light before four.",
	"Candles go out in straw baskets. With the wardens on the road I don't lose sleep over them.",
	"A box of candles vanished from the church last year. Whoever took it, the guards want his name, and so do I.",
	"Busy. The feast needs two hundred candles. I've half the wax I need.",
	"Nothing today. The vats are full and the wicks are cut.",
	"Berta sets a fine table at {meal}. You'll leave smelling of tallow, I'm afraid.",
	"Dipping candles. Twelve at a time, a hundred times over.",
	"Hello, dear. Mind your sleeves near the vats.",
	"How kind. Not everyone remembers.",
	"Berta, Emil's wife. I keep the books and the children. Everyone's secrets come to me too.",
	"Emil, Rozalie and Viktor. And a house that smells of tallow, which I stopped noticing years ago.",
	"Rozalie's baker's son can ask Emil for her hand. He'd have to read the price list first.",
	"The church pays in prayers. I'd like some coin to go with them.",
	"A fine day to air the linens.",
	"Rain. The washing smells of tallow whatever I do.",
	"With the fog down, half the village is at our door for candles.",
	"The shop smells of fat and smoke in the snow, but it's warm.",
	"Emil sends candles by cart. I hear no complaints from the road.",
	"Everyone knows who steals in a village this size. Say it to the guards. The rest follows.",
	"We're comfortable, thank God. Others are not. The chandler's wife knows it.",
	"I've nothing for you. Ask Emil, he counts the wax.",
	"Sit at our table at {meal}, and keep clear of the vats.",
	"Counting candles and keeping Viktor out of the tallow.",
	"Hello. Do you know the baker's son? He's rather nice.",
	"That was well said. You've good manners.",
	"Rozalie. The chandler's daughter, if you hadn't guessed by the smell.",
	"Father, Mother. My brother Viktor. And a house of candles.",
	"The baker's son asked to walk with me after mass. Mother said he can ask Father.",
	"Everyone's talking about the church feast. Two hundred candles, and nobody to light them.",
	"It's dry, so I might walk after mass, if somebody asks me.",
	"It's wicks indoors for me when it rains.",
	"Fog. The baker's son will lose his way, I think.",
	"We light every candle in the house when it snows.",
	"I walk to the church and back. That's far enough.",
	"I'd tell the guards. A candle is worth more than people think.",
	"Trimming wicks, carrying candles, dreaming about the baker's son. Mother says I'm too young.",
	"Ask Mother about the candles. I've no work for you today.",
	"Mother keeps a good table. You can eat with us at {meal}, and sit by me.",
	"Wrapping candles and watching the road.",
	"Hello. Careful, the tallow's still hot.",
	"Thanks. Father says I should smile more.",
	"Viktor. The chandler's son. I dip the wicks.",
	"Father, Mother and Rozalie. We live over the shop.",
	"Father says the stink built the upper floor. I'm tired of it.",
	"The whole house stinks of tallow. Father calls it the smell that paid for the upper floor.",
	"The candles set faster when the air's dry. I'm not complaining.",
	"I dip slower when it rains, or the wicks sag.",
	"The tallow smell hangs in the house when there's fog.",
	"I keep the vats warm all day when it snows.",
	"Candles go to the church, and it's a short quiet walk.",
	"A box of candles went missing. Father blamed me, then the church.",
	"Dipping, carrying, scraping tallow off my hands. It takes a good day to make a few dozen.",
	"The flax thread's in. I've nothing to hand out.",
	"Mother won't mind a guest at {meal}. Just don't touch the tallow.",
	"Dipping wicks, again. Eleven more dozen.",
	"Come in, come in! The Hare is the best alehouse this side of the river.",
	"Glad to. Tell your friends.",
	"Otakar Krcmar. I keep the Hare. If you've a thirst, I've the cure.",
	"Milada is my wife. We keep the Hare between us, and nobody else works here.",
	"Milada watches the coin. I watch the guests. It works.",
	"The bowyer has drunk on credit for three weeks. He'll pay, they always do eventually.",
	"Dry evenings mean full benches outside. I've dragged out every stool.",
	"When it rains everyone crowds in and the floor floods.",
	"Travellers find the Hare by its lamp in fog.",
	"Snow. Hot ale sells itself.",
	"After the third cup they all swear the main road is safe.",
	"Men skip without paying. Tell the guards if you see a thief. I'll remember you at the bar.",
	"A good night at the Hare is full benches and no fights. Most nights I get one of the two.",
	"Kegs are full for once. No work today.",
	"The first cup's on the house at {meal}. The second isn't.",
	"Polishing cups and counting the kegs.",
	"Welcome. Close the door, the draught puts out the lamp.",
	"It's good to hear. Most of them just shout for ale.",
	"Milada, Otakar's wife. I pour, I count, and I hear everything.",
	"Otakar and me. No children, only regulars.",
	"Otakar boasts about the Hare. It's the only alehouse here, so he doesn't have to.",
	"The bowyer drinks on credit. Three weeks now. I've kept count.",
	"The doors stand open and the regulars wander in. It's a pleasant day.",
	"The regulars turn up early when it rains.",
	"The lamp's no use in fog and still they find us.",
	"The fire roars in the snow and every bench is full.",
	"I've heard no talk of trouble on the road. Not lately.",
	"Skipping a bill is theft too. Say so to the guards. I'll add the name to my list.",
	"Pouring and counting. The same faces, the same stories, and a few new lies.",
	"Pantry's stocked. I've nothing for you today.",
	"You'll eat with us at {meal}. There's always a plate for a guest, and a cup too.",
	"Slicing sausage and writing up the credit.",
	"Morning. Mind the nets, they're wet.",
	"Aye. Thanks to you too.",
	"Kilian Rybar. I fish this stretch of the river, and the eels know me.",
	"Johana, my wife. Benedikt and Krystyna, the children. We eat fish, mostly.",
	"Johana wants a sausage. I tell her fish is lighter on the purse.",
	"The eels are running early this {season}. We'll set the traps tonight. Someone's been lifting my fish upstream.",
	"Low water and sulky fish. The trout sit at the bottom and ignore me.",
	"Rain brings the fish up. A good day for the net.",
	"Fog on the water. I can't see the float, so I listen for the splash.",
	"The river runs slow in snow, and so do the fish.",
	"I walk the bank more than the road. The wardens ride the high road. The river's mine.",
	"My traps keep losing fish. If you see him, tell the guards, and tell me first.",
	"Some days the river gives and some days it doesn't. Today it gave a little.",
	"Nothing today. I've bait enough and the boat floats.",
	"It's fish at {meal}, but it's fresh. Sit by the fire.",
	"Checking the traps and mending the nets.",
	"Hello. Don't mind the smell, it's fish again.",
	"That's good to hear after a day of smoke and fish.",
	"Johana, Kilian's wife. I smoke the fish and mend the nets.",
	"Kilian, Benedikt and Krystyna. We're always wet and always fishy.",
	"I'd love a sausage. Kilian says fish is cheaper. He's right. I hate it.",
	"Fast days, the whole county eats like us and pays more for it. That's the only good in it.",
	"The fish dry well on the line. I'll smoke the rest tonight.",
	"The smoke hangs low in rain and the children smell of it.",
	"I smoke the eels slow when the fog's down.",
	"In snow we eat smoked fish and sit close to the fire.",
	"The guards and carts have the road. I stay home.",
	"Fish go from the traps. Kilian would like to find who's doing it.",
	"We get by. There's fish for supper and fish for breakfast. The children are growing.",
	"Nothing needs carrying today. Ask me when the smoker's full.",
	"Yes, it's fish at {meal}. I'd give you sausage if I had any.",
	"Smoking the catch and mending the nets.",
	"Hello! I caught a trout with my hands today. A big one.",
	"Sure! Do you want to see my trout?",
	"I'm Benedikt. I'm the eldest. I can catch trout with my hands.",
	"Father fishes, Mother smokes, and Krystyna says I'm lying.",
	"Krystyna says the trout was small. It wasn't.",
	"I caught a trout with my bare hands. Krystyna says it was small. It wasn't.",
	"The river's low when it's dry. The trout hide.",
	"Rain! The river's high and the fish come up.",
	"Fog on the water. You can hear the fish jump.",
	"The river's so cold in the snow that my hands go numb.",
	"I stay on the bank. Father says fish don't use the road.",
	"Father's traps lose fish. I'd catch the thief with my hands.",
	"Splashing, fishing, being told to come back. Father says I'll be a fisher too.",
	"I'm too small. Father gives out the work.",
	"It's fish for supper! Mother lets me pick the bones out.",
	"Wading in the shallows. Looking for trout.",
	"Hello. That trout was small, whatever Benedikt says.",
	"You can have the fish tail.",
	"I'm Krystyna. I'm the second child. I tell the truth.",
	"Mother and Father and Benedikt. We share everything, even the smell.",
	"Benedikt boasts. I tell him the truth. We argue a lot.",
	"Benedikt's trout was small. I saw it. He says it was big.",
	"Dry days we can sit on the bank.",
	"I stay in with Mother in the rain.",
	"Fog. I can't see the river.",
	"Snow. I help Mother with the smoking.",
	"I stay by the river and never go on the road.",
	"Fish went missing and I told Mother.",
	"Helping Mother and watching Benedikt show off.",
	"Ask Mother. I only hold the bucket.",
	"It's fish. Always fish. You can have the tail.",
	"Cutting fish for the smoke.",
	"Don't stand under the ladder. Good day.",
	"Thanks. A kind word keeps the reed from rotting.",
	"Havel Pokryvac, thatcher. I roof the hamlet, and mine leaks.",
	"Sabina is my wife. It's the two of us and a drip.",
	"Sabina says do ours first. I say a thatcher's roof always leaks.",
	"The reed bed by the river flooded. It's straw this year, and straw rots.",
	"This is the weather for laying thatch. I'm up the ladder by Prime.",
	"Nobody thatches in rain. I sit and listen to my own roof drip.",
	"Fog on the ridge. I won't climb in fog.",
	"Thatch holds the snow and the roof groans.",
	"Reed comes by cart. The road's fine, and the wardens keep it so.",
	"Bundles of reed go missing. If you see a man with a stack, tell the guards.",
	"The roofs wear out faster than I can lay them. Suits me, it keeps me fed.",
	"The ladder's in the yard and the rope's tied. I've no work for you.",
	"Sabina says grace before the drip finds the pot. Come at {meal}.",
	"Binding reed bundles for the neighbour's roof.",
	"Hello there. Mind the pot, the roof drips there.",
	"God bless you. That's kind.",
	"Sabina, the thatcher's wife. I keep the pots under the drip.",
	"Havel and me. No children, only drips.",
	"Havel says a thatcher's roof always leaks. I say a thatcher's wife prays.",
	"Our roof drips on the bed. Havel does everyone else's first.",
	"At last I can dry the bedding.",
	"Every pot we own is on the floor when it rains.",
	"The damp gets into everything in fog.",
	"Snow sits on the thatch and by evening the drip begins.",
	"I stay close to home. The roads are for the wardens and the carts.",
	"Our bucket walked off. If you see it, tell the guards.",
	"We get by. A roof over us, mostly, and each other.",
	"We're short of nothing today but a dry roof.",
	"Come at {meal} and sit away from the drip.",
	"Moving pots and praying for dry weather.",
	"Good day. Mind the hooks, they're sharp.",
	"Thanks. A butcher doesn't hear that often.",
	"Lambert Reznik, butcher. Every sausage in the hamlet has my hand in it.",
	"Frantiska, my wife. Rupert, Liba and Silvestr. We butcher as a family.",
	"Rupert wants to run the shop. I tell him when he can break a pig in under an hour.",
	"Three pigs to slaughter before {season} ends, and the salt's gone up again.",
	"The hams cure well in dry air. That's one thing in my favour.",
	"The smokehouse eats more wood in the rain.",
	"I smoke the meat longer in fog, it hangs heavy.",
	"Snow. Frost helps the cure. I slaughter before it thaws.",
	"Pigs come by cart. Wardens or no wardens, I count heads when they arrive.",
	"A ham went missing from the smokehouse. Tell the guards what you saw. I'll be grateful.",
	"Hard on the arms, soft on the nose. A pig takes an hour, and a sausage takes a day.",
	"Not today. The salt store is full.",
	"There's always sausage at {meal}. Frantiska won't hear of anything else.",
	"Breaking a carcass and filling casings.",
	"Hello. Wipe your boots, I only scrubbed the floor.",
	"Well said. Come back when the sausage is ready.",
	"Frantiska, Lambert's wife. I cook what he butchers.",
	"Lambert, Rupert, Liba, Silvestr. A full house, and a full table.",
	"Liba wants to marry someone from the village. I tell her pick someone who eats less.",
	"The blood pudding's not set. We'll eat it runny. Nobody complains.",
	"The sausage dries nicely today. I'll hang more.",
	"The smokehouse leaks a bit when it rains.",
	"Fog. The smell of smoke hangs in the yard.",
	"We eat more in the snow. The bread runs low.",
	"Lambert takes the cart to market. He says it's safe enough.",
	"Meat vanishes. If you see it, tell the guards.",
	"I'll admit the table is good. A butcher's house doesn't go hungry.",
	"Nothing needs carrying at the moment. Come back when the sausage is cured.",
	"There's more than enough. Sit down at {meal}.",
	"Stirring the pudding and tasting the sausage.",
	"Hello. Don't mind the apron, it's been a day.",
	"Thanks. Father doesn't say that.",
	"Rupert, the butcher's son. I'm learning the knives.",
	"Father, Mother, Liba and Silvestr. We butcher together.",
	"Father says I can't run the shop yet. I'm nearly ready.",
	"A boar came through the herdsman's pens. He wants us to butcher it, if he catches it.",
	"Meat keeps better when it's dry. Father says so, at least.",
	"I cut in the shed when it rains.",
	"The smoke hangs over the hamlet in this fog.",
	"Snow. I slaughter a pig before the cold sets in.",
	"Sausages go to the alehouse, and it's no distance.",
	"A ham is gone. Father blames Silvestr, but it wasn't him.",
	"Cutting, hanging, hauling. My arms ache and the knife's heavy.",
	"Father hasn't given me anything to pass on.",
	"Mother makes extra. Come at {meal}, and bring a strong stomach.",
	"Cutting joints and hanging them in the smokehouse.",
	"Hello. Don't mind the smell, it's the pudding.",
	"Thanks, that's lovely of you to say.",
	"Liba. The butcher's daughter. I stir the pudding.",
	"Mother, Father, Rupert and Silvestr. A loud house.",
	"I'd like to marry, but the boys smell of pigs. So do I.",
	"The blood pudding's runny. Mother says eat it anyway.",
	"I can hang the sausages outside when it's dry.",
	"The smokehouse stinks worse in rain.",
	"Fog. The yard is full of smoke.",
	"Snow. The pudding sets faster.",
	"I stay in the hamlet. Nobody walks the road but carters.",
	"I'd tell everyone, if I saw a thief.",
	"I stir the pudding and I listen. You'd be surprised what gets said at a butcher's block.",
	"Nothing today. Ask Mother about the pudding.",
	"You can eat with us at {meal}. Mother won't ask questions.",
	"Stirring the pudding and listening to Mother.",
	"Hello. Careful with the hooks.",
	"Thanks. That's not a word you hear from the master.",
	"Silvestr, journeyman butcher. I lodge here and work the cleaver.",
	"I've no family nearby. The Reznik house feeds me.",
	"The master's a fair man. The mistress is a better cook than most.",
	"The fisherman wants sausage for eel. I told him we trade for anything that keeps.",
	"The meat dries clean today. I'm hanging it before the wind turns.",
	"Wet days I work under the shed roof.",
	"Fog. The smell lingers.",
	"Snow. I haul wood to the smokehouse.",
	"The main road was safe when I came.",
	"I was blamed when the ham went missing. I want the real thief found.",
	"I cleave, hang and scrub from dawn. Hard work, but the food is good.",
	"Nothing for you. Ask the master.",
	"You'll eat well here at {meal}. The mistress sees to that.",
	"Scrubbing the block and sharpening knives.",
	"Good day. Stand upwind.",
	"Few thank a tanner. I'll take it.",
	"Gabriel Kozeluh, tanner. Every boot in the hamlet comes from my tubs.",
	"Damian is my son. His mother's in the churchyard.",
	"Damian is a good lad. He hates the smell, but he'll get used to it.",
	"The priest says we can't stand at the front at mass because of the smell. We pay our tithe like everyone.",
	"Hides dry well in this weather. The smell carries to the next hamlet, sorry.",
	"The tubs overflow when it rains. The yard turns to swamp.",
	"Fog traps the smell. Don't come close.",
	"Snow. The tubs freeze. I wait.",
	"Hides come in by cart and arrive whole. The wardens deserve some credit.",
	"Hides vanish from the rack. If you see a man with one, tell the guards.",
	"Slow and foul. A hide takes months in the tub. Oak bark is what makes it leather.",
	"Tubs are full and the rack is stocked. No work today.",
	"You may not like the smell, but you'll like the stew. Come at {meal}.",
	"Turning hides in the tubs.",
	"Hello. Sorry about the smell. You get used to it. Nearly.",
	"People don't usually say that near the tannery. I'll remember it.",
	"Damian, the tanner's son. I strip bark and turn hides.",
	"Father and me. Mother passed away. The smell reminds me of her, oddly.",
	"Father says I'll learn the trade. I'd like to learn another.",
	"The priest said we can't stand at the front. Father says we'll pray louder at the back.",
	"I'll strip oak bark in the wood. It peels easily when it's dry.",
	"The bark peels easily in the wet.",
	"Fog. I can't find the oaks.",
	"Snow. The bark tubs freeze over.",
	"I walk the woods more than the road. The oaks are off it, and bandits are rare.",
	"A hide is gone. Father thinks it was the cobbler.",
	"Bark, hides, scrubbing, all day. Everything smells, but nothing goes to waste.",
	"Nothing for you today. Father decides.",
	"It's plain at {meal}, but it doesn't smell.",
	"Stripping oak bark in the wood.",
	"The honey cakes are cooling, which always puts me in a good mood.",
	"Thank you, dear. Take a honey cake with you.",
	"Dobromila, Erazim's wife. I make the honey cakes and keep the bees away from the door.",
	"Erazim and our daughter Mlada. Between the bees and the girl, I'm never alone.",
	"Mlada gets stung and shows everyone. I'd rather she kept her distance.",
	"The church wants all our wax. Fine, but then the church can come to us for honey too.",
	"The bees are out in this dry weather and the whole hamlet hums.",
	"Rain keeps the bees in. Erazim sulks, and I bake.",
	"Fog keeps the bees home, and me with them.",
	"With the hives asleep under the snow, I count the honey jars.",
	"I keep to the hamlet. The wardens ride the road, and Erazim carries the honey.",
	"Hives get robbed now and then. The thief gets stung first, then he meets the guards.",
	"We eat sweet and live plain. There's always honey, and rarely anything else.",
	"All the honey's jarred. There's no work today.",
	"There'll be honey on the bread at {meal}. Sit down.",
	"Baking honey cakes and keeping an eye on Mlada.",
	"Hello! Look, I got stung on the eyelid.",
	"You're welcome! Do you want a honey cake?",
	"I'm Mlada. I help with the bees.",
	"Father keeps the bees and Mother bakes. I get stung.",
	"Mother says stay back from the skeps. I forget.",
	"A bee stung me on the eyelid. It swelled up and I could hardly see.",
	"Dry! The bees fly and the clover blooms.",
	"It's raining, so the bees went in their skeps. I went in too.",
	"I can't see the hives in fog but I can hear them humming.",
	"The skeps are all covered up with snow. I'm eating a honey cake.",
	"The bees are enough for me. I never go on the road.",
	"Someone stole a honey crock. He must have been stung.",
	"Watching the bees and carrying honey. Mother says stay back, but I want to see.",
	"Mother or Father gives out the jobs. I only carry honey.",
	"We have honey at {meal}! You can have my spoon.",
	"Carrying honey crocks and avoiding the bees.",
	"Hush a moment. The skep is humming. Good day.",
	"Thanks. The bees thank you too, in their way.",
	"Erazim, beekeeper. I keep the skeps behind the house.",
	"Dobromila and Mlada. And forty thousand bees, depending on the month.",
	"Dobromila says I say that about every batch of mead. It's true each time.",
	"The mead is ready, the best batch in three years. Ask Dobromila, she's tasted it.",
	"The clover is thick and the bees are happy. I'm happy too.",
	"The bees stay in when it rains, so I mend the skeps.",
	"No use opening the skeps in fog. I leave the bees alone.",
	"Once the snow comes I wrap the skeps in straw.",
	"I take honey to the chandler's by the road. The wardens keep it safe.",
	"A robbed hive is a poor sight. Tell the guards if you see a man with a skep.",
	"Smoke, honey, wax. I smoke them calm and take what they spare.",
	"The skeps are calm and the kindling's stacked. Nothing for you.",
	"There'll be honey cake after the meal. Come at {meal}.",
	"Smoking the skeps and checking the wax.",
	"Morning. Don't expect much from this house.",
	"Aye. Thanks. That's new in this house.",
	"Blazej Chudoba. I'm a cottar. I work other men's fields.",
	"Apolena, my wife. Kaspar, the boy. And the old woman's cough.",
	"I'd like my boy to eat. That's all I ask.",
	"The count's men took our goose for the tax. The goose! The yeoman paid in half-chaff rye.",
	"Hay weather. I'll cut for the yeoman.",
	"The fields flood in rain, and so does our floor.",
	"Can't see the field I'm meant to mow in this fog.",
	"Snow. No work, no wages.",
	"The yeoman's is a walk away. The road is fine, and I've nothing to steal.",
	"A man with nothing is no thief's mark. The guards won't mind us.",
	"Hard on the back and thin on the pay. It's honest work, and it's all we have.",
	"Nothing I can spare. Come back after the harvest.",
	"You've chosen a poor table, but it's warm. Come at {meal}.",
	"Mowing the yeoman's hay for rye.",
	"God give you good day. You'll excuse the house.",
	"We've little to thank anyone with. It's good to hear it.",
	"Apolena, Blazej's wife. I keep what little there is.",
	"Blazej and Kaspar. And an old cough in the corner we all share.",
	"I worry for the little one. He's hungry all the time.",
	"The goose is gone, taken for the tax. We ate nothing that night, but God provides.",
	"The blankets can go on the line today.",
	"Rain. The thatch leaks, and I pray it stops.",
	"I say a prayer for everyone out in the fog.",
	"We huddle together in the snow and say the rosary.",
	"I don't go far. The road is for the guards and the lucky.",
	"Hunger makes thieves. I pray for them. I lock my door.",
	"Whatever there is, we eat. God provides, though some nights He's slow.",
	"We need more than we can give, so nothing today.",
	"It's thin, but you're welcome at {meal}.",
	"Mending clothes and praying for a better winter.",
	"Hello. I'm hungry. Do you have bread?",
	"Thank you! I mean it.",
	"I'm Kaspar. I'm the eldest. I'm always hungry.",
	"Mother and Father. And Grandmother's cough.",
	"Father works hard. Mother says it's for me.",
	"The yeoman paid Father in rye that's half chaff. Mother says be thankful.",
	"Dry. I can look for berries.",
	"The floor gets wet when it rains.",
	"I can't find my way to the stream in fog.",
	"I'm cold and hungry in the snow.",
	"I don't go on the road. Mother says it's far.",
	"I'd take bread, if I saw some. I wouldn't tell the guards.",
	"Looking for food. Berries, mostly, or nuts.",
	"I'm too hungry to be busy. There's no work for you here.",
	"We have turnips at {meal}. I'll share.",
	"Looking for mushrooms. They're free.",
	"Morning. Mind the dog, he's friendlier than he looks.",
	"Thanks. A kind word means more up here.",
	"Simon Ovcak. I keep the sheep on the high pasture.",
	"Tereza, my wife. Dalibor and Nela, the children. And a dog that's afraid of wolves.",
	"Dalibor wants to fight wolves with a sling. I'd like him to live.",
	"We lost two lambs on the high pasture. Wolves, or something worse.",
	"The grass thins, so I move them higher up. They don't like it.",
	"Rain brings the wolves lower. I stay awake with the flock.",
	"I can't see the fold from the hut in fog. I count by sound.",
	"When snow comes the sheep come down, and the wolves with them.",
	"I walk the high track. The wardens don't come this far, and the wolves know it.",
	"Sheep rustlers are rare, but wolves are worse. Tell the guards if you see either.",
	"Fed and counted, and the dog still won't go near the tree line. I don't blame him.",
	"The flock is fed and the dog is in. I've no work for you today.",
	"There's mutton at {meal} and a fire. Mind the dog.",
	"Counting the flock and watching the tree line.",
	"Hello. Mind your head on the beam.",
	"It's rare up here, so I'll take it.",
	"Tereza, Simon's wife. I wash the wool and make the cheese.",
	"Simon is on the pasture most days. Dalibor and Nela keep me company.",
	"I tell Simon to take the dog. The dog won't go. So Simon takes a stick.",
	"The weaver in Talmberg wants our fleeces. All of them, he says. That's a good price.",
	"The wool dries white in this air.",
	"The fleeces smell like a wet dog when it rains.",
	"I keep the children close in fog.",
	"The cheese rests and the sheep wait out the snow.",
	"I never go down. Simon says the road's safe in daylight.",
	"There's nothing here to steal but wool. If you see one, tell the guards.",
	"The wool sells, the cheese keeps and the children eat. We manage.",
	"The cheese is salted for the week. No work today.",
	"I'll cut a wedge of cheese for you at {meal}.",
	"Washing fleeces in the stream.",
	"Hello! Do you want to see my sling?",
	"Welcome! I can hit a post from twenty paces.",
	"Dalibor. I'm going to be a shepherd and a wolf-fighter.",
	"Father and Mother and Nela. And Spot, who won't fight.",
	"Mother says I'm not to chase wolves. I practise anyway.",
	"Wolves took two lambs. I wasn't there. I'd have hit them with my sling.",
	"Good weather for aiming. I hit the post five times.",
	"My sling gets wet in the rain and the stones slip.",
	"I can't see the target in fog.",
	"Snow. I hit a tree with a snowball.",
	"Father says stay near the fold.",
	"A thief would get a stone in the head.",
	"Practising my sling. Mother says I'll hurt someone.",
	"I'm not big enough to give out work. Father does it.",
	"There's mutton at {meal}! Mother lets me carve.",
	"Slinging stones at a fence post.",
	"Hello. Do you like lambs?",
	"I like you. Do you want to hold a lamb?",
	"I'm Nela. I look after the lambs.",
	"Mother, Father and Dalibor. And thirty lambs, give or take.",
	"Dalibor shouts at wolves. I sing to the lambs.",
	"The weaver wants all our fleeces. I want to keep one lamb.",
	"The lambs run about when it's dry.",
	"The lambs shiver in the rain, so I hold one.",
	"Fog. I hear them bleating but I can't see them.",
	"I carry a lamb inside when it snows.",
	"I stay with the lambs.",
	"If someone took a lamb, I'd cry.",
	"Feeding lambs, naming them. Father says don't name them.",
	"I'm small. Ask Mother.",
	"We have cheese at {meal}. You can hold a lamb after.",
	"Feeding a lamb from a rag.",
	"Quiet, if you will. I'm listening for a tusker.",
	"Thanks. Keep your voice down, the woods hear.",
	"Robert Lovec, hunter. I live by the woods and what's in them.",
	"Irena, my wife. It's just the two of us.",
	"Irena minds the pelts and my temper. She does both well.",
	"Boar tracks by the stream. A big tusker. I'll have him before {season} ends, if the forester doesn't have me first.",
	"The leaves crackle underfoot and the deer hear me coming. Rain would help.",
	"Rain muffles my steps. Good for stalking.",
	"Fog hides me and the deer. I wait.",
	"Snow shows every track. I follow the boar.",
	"I keep off the roads. Bandits lurk in the same woods, and I'd rather they didn't see me.",
	"Poachers ruin the game. The guards catch them, and the count fines them hard.",
	"The thickets hold boar, and poachers come for the count's deer. I watch for both.",
	"Nothing today. The arrows are made and the shafts are straight.",
	"It's venison at {meal}, and don't ask where I got it.",
	"Following the boar tracks along the stream.",
	"Hello. Don't mind the pelts, they're drying.",
	"Thanks. It's kind of you to say.",
	"Irena, Robert's wife. I cure the pelts and cook what he brings.",
	"Robert and me. We've no children, only fox pelts.",
	"Robert thinks the count's wood is any wood the forester can't see. I worry.",
	"The fox pelt fetched less than we thought. Everyone's selling fox this year.",
	"The pelts dry stiff in this weather. I'll soften them with fat.",
	"Rain. The pelts smell, and so does Robert.",
	"Robert's out in the fog and I wait for him to come home.",
	"Snow. He tracks, I cook.",
	"I don't go far. Robert says the main road is safe.",
	"Poaching is a hanging offence, some say. I'd say it's a fine and the stocks.",
	"The table's good, though not always honestly. Robert brings in the venison.",
	"The meat is salted and the pelts are drying. Nothing to do today.",
	"It's venison. We didn't buy it, so don't ask. Come at {meal}.",
	"Scraping a pelt and salting meat.",
	"Morning. Sorry about the dust, it comes up with me.",
	"Thanks. The mine doesn't say it.",
	"Valentin Havir, miner. I work the iron seam below the hill.",
	"Monika, my wife. Dusan, Pavla. My father Matyas. Five in one house.",
	"Dusan holds the lamp now. I'm proud of him, and I'm afraid.",
	"Good iron in the new seam, but the shaft groans at night. Nobody should go down after dark.",
	"The shaft stays dry and the ore comes up easier. I'll take it.",
	"Rain gets into the shaft. We pump all day.",
	"Fog over the hill. Underground, it's the same dark.",
	"The shaft stays warm in snow. That's the only mercy.",
	"Ore goes out by cart. The wardens keep the road, and I trust them more than the props.",
	"Ore doesn't get stolen. Tools do. Tell the guards if you see someone with my pick.",
	"Cold, dark and dusty. The ore comes up in baskets. My father's chest says it's not worth it.",
	"The shaft is propped and the torches are lit, so there's no job today.",
	"Monika makes a hearty stew. Come at {meal}.",
	"Working the new seam. Two more baskets before dusk.",
	"Hello. Careful with the lamp, it's the only one.",
	"Rare enough to hear in a mining house. I'm glad of it.",
	"Monika, Valentin's wife. I keep the lamp lit and the family fed.",
	"Valentin, Dusan, Pavla and Matyas. I keep them all above ground.",
	"I told Dusan he'll come up every night, or I'll go down after him.",
	"The shaft groans at night. I tell Valentin not to go down after dark.",
	"Clothes will dry today, so I'll wash the dust out.",
	"When it rains everything's black with ore dust and wet.",
	"I worry more when there's fog.",
	"I keep the fire burning and the lamp lit through the snow.",
	"I don't go far. Valentin takes the ore, and I stay with the children.",
	"Not much worth taking at ours. If you see one, tell the guards.",
	"The mine pays and the pot's full, mostly. We get along.",
	"Nothing needs carrying today. Ask me when the bar is ready.",
	"It's stew at {meal}, and there's plenty.",
	"Scrubbing the dust out of the clothes.",
	"Hello. I hold the lamp now. Father lets me.",
	"Cheers. That's good of you.",
	"Dusan. I help Father in the mine. I'm learning.",
	"Father, Mother, Pavla and Grandfather. And ore dust in the bread.",
	"Mother says I'll come up every night. I intend to.",
	"Father lets me hold the lamp. The shaft groans, but I'm not scared.",
	"The mine gets dustier when it's dry.",
	"Rain. We pump the water out.",
	"We don't notice fog underground.",
	"Snow. It's warm below, so I don't mind.",
	"I carry ore to the cart. The road starts at the hill.",
	"Tools go missing. Father blames me, then the smith.",
	"Carrying baskets, holding the lamp, listening for creaks. Father says I'll make a miner.",
	"Father says no errands today. The shaft needs me more.",
	"Mother cooks well. Come at {meal}.",
	"Hauling ore baskets up the shaft.",
	"Hello. Grandfather tells the best stories.",
	"I like you. You can sit with Grandfather.",
	"I'm Pavla. I'm the littlest.",
	"Mother, Father, Dusan and Grandfather. And a house full of dust.",
	"Grandfather coughs. I sit with him.",
	"Grandfather told the story about the ghost in the old shaft. It's scary.",
	"The dust blows everywhere when it's dry.",
	"Rain. I stay in and listen.",
	"Fog is spooky, like the story.",
	"I put on two pairs of socks when it snows.",
	"I don't go on the road.",
	"I'd hide, if I saw a thief.",
	"Listening to Grandfather. His stories are better than games.",
	"Grandfather says I'm too little for jobs. He tells stories instead.",
	"We have stew at {meal}. Grandfather will tell a story.",
	"Sitting with Grandfather and listening.",
	"Eh? Who's that? Come closer, my eyes are all dust.",
	"Aye, lad. You've manners, which is more than most.",
	"Matyas. They called me Old Matyas when I was forty. Worked below fifty years.",
	"Valentin is my son. Monika's his wife. The young ones are my grandchildren.",
	"I tell Pavla the ghost story. She likes the lamps flickering.",
	"The new seam has good iron. It's the shaft that worries me. I heard that groan before the old shaft fell.",
	"My chest likes dry air. I sit outside and cough in peace.",
	"The cough comes back with the rain.",
	"When I was young we called fog the miner's breath.",
	"Snow. I'm warm below, cold above.",
	"I went to Talmberg once, forty years ago. The road hasn't changed.",
	"In my day, a thief lost a hand. Now it's the stocks. They get off lightly.",
	"Smaller, and the winters were longer. Every miner knew every other's cough.",
	"Nothing for you, lad. Ask Monika.",
	"My chest keeps me at the table. Come at {meal}.",
	"Sitting by the fire and telling the same stories.",
	"Good day, friend. It's quiet here, as you'll have noticed.",
	"God bless you. We don't get many visitors.",
	"Augustin Stary. My wife is Kunhuta. We've been here a long time.",
	"Kunhuta and me. Fifty-one years married. She says fifty-two.",
	"Our sons are in the churchyard. We visit them, and at home we talk about them.",
	"The hens are laying again. Two eggs this morning. That's news for us.",
	"No rain today, so we sit outside.",
	"Rain keeps us in, and we say the rosary.",
	"We don't go out when there's fog.",
	"Snow. The old bones know it before the sky does.",
	"We haven't left the hamlet in years. The herdsman's wife brings milk, and that's our road.",
	"We've nothing to steal. If anyone does, the guards bind him.",
	"Smaller, and the winters were longer. The nights were darker too, and we all went to bed early.",
	"Nothing today, friend. We've eggs enough for two old people.",
	"We'd love the company. Come at {meal}.",
	"Sitting in the sun with Kunhuta.",
	"God give you good day. Do sit down. We so rarely have anyone.",
	"Bless you. It's lovely to be thanked.",
	"Kunhuta, Augustin's wife. I keep the hens and the house.",
	"Augustin and me, and the hens. Our boys are in the churchyard.",
	"We talk too much when guests come. Forgive us. Nobody visits.",
	"The hens started laying again. Two eggs this morning. That's welcome food in a small house.",
	"I'll sit by the door while it stays dry.",
	"Rain. I pray for our sons.",
	"I say the rosary when the fog comes down.",
	"We keep the fire in and the door shut when it snows.",
	"I don't travel. The only traveller I see is the herdsman's wife with the milk.",
	"A thief would be disappointed here. The guards bind them anyway.",
	"Two eggs and a loaf between us, and each other. That's plenty at our age.",
	"Come back when the wood's low, dear. Nothing today.",
	"We'd be glad of your company at {meal}.",
	"Feeding the hens and talking to Augustin.",
	"Good day. Mind the dust, it gets into everything.",
	"Hm. Thanks. That's more than I usually hear.",
	"Arnost Mlynar, the miller. Everyone needs me, so everyone grumbles.",
	"Vilma is my wife. Blanka and Leopold are ours, and Lojza works the mill for me.",
	"Blanka says everyone hates the miller. I tell her it means they all depend on him.",
	"The baker says my flour is gritty. He says it every year. The stones do need dressing, I'll admit.",
	"The stream runs low, but the wind turns the sails. The stones don't mind.",
	"The wheel turns well in rain. The flour comes out damp.",
	"The stones turn the same in fog. It's the farmers who stay home.",
	"Snow fills the mill with visitors. It's the warmest place in the hamlet.",
	"Grain comes in by cart and goes out by cart. I count every sack, and I trust the wardens with the road.",
	"A miller who steals loses his trade. A customer who steals loses his hand, or his purse.",
	"The stones turn and the grain comes through. Everyone grumbles about my share, as always.",
	"Sacks are filled and the sail is mended for now. No work today.",
	"Vilma sets a plain table with bread on it. Come at {meal}.",
	"Dressing one millstone after another.",
	"Come in. Mind the flour, it's on the floor and the bench.",
	"Thanks. We don't hear much of it in the mill.",
	"Vilma, the miller's wife. I sift, sweep and keep the books.",
	"Arnost, Blanka, Leopold, and Lojza who lodges with us. Everyone is covered in flour.",
	"Arnost grumbles, but he's fair. The farmers don't see it.",
	"The stones need dressing. I told Arnost. He says wait until harvest.",
	"I'll sweep out the mill while it's dry.",
	"Rain makes the flour cling to every pot and bench.",
	"Fog and flour. I can't see across the room.",
	"The grain comes in over winter, so we eat better once the snow falls.",
	"I go as far as the church. Arnost has the carts for the rest.",
	"Someone took a sack of flour. The guards ask, and everyone shrugs.",
	"Well enough fed. A miller's house is never short of bread, even when other things run short.",
	"Nothing I'd send you for today. Come back after the harvest bell.",
	"There's flour on the table, but not in the soup. Come at {meal}.",
	"Sifting flour and keeping Lojza out of the sacks.",
	"Hello. Don't mind the flour, it's in my hair too.",
	"Father never says that to anyone. I'll tell him you did.",
	"Blanka, the miller's daughter. Everyone knows my father.",
	"Father, Mother and Leopold. And Lojza, who's always in the way.",
	"I tell Father why the village hates him. He says it's because they need him.",
	"The baker says Father's flour is gritty. Mother agrees with the baker.",
	"I might walk down to the stream.",
	"Rain. The wheel turns. I sit in the shed.",
	"The mill looks like a ghost when the fog sits on it.",
	"Snow. The whole mill is white.",
	"Church is as far as I go. The road's fine.",
	"If someone steals, everyone hears about it. A village this size has no secrets.",
	"Sifting, sweeping, listening to who's quarrelling with whom.",
	"Ask Father about the sail. I've nothing today.",
	"Mother will set a place for you at {meal}.",
	"Sweeping the mill floor and hearing the news.",
	"Have you a sack to grind? Or only a question?",
	"Not at all. Good manners make customers.",
	"Leopold, the miller's son. I learn the stones.",
	"Father, Mother, Blanka and Lojza. We live in the mill house.",
	"Father says I'll be miller. I'd like to see more than the wheel.",
	"The brewer says Father is holding barley back for a better price. Father says the brewer can buy elsewhere.",
	"The sails turn fast. The sacks pile up.",
	"A wet day suits the wheel.",
	"In fog we grind by sound.",
	"The stream freezes in snow, so we use the wind.",
	"I carry flour to the baker in Talmberg. The road's good.",
	"Sacks go missing. Father counts them twice.",
	"Weighing, sacking, carrying. Father checks every sack.",
	"Nothing I need fetched. Come after the harvest comes in.",
	"Come at {meal}. There'll be bread and the flour dust is free.",
	"Weighing sacks and writing the tally.",
	"Sorry about the flour on the floor.",
	"Thanks. The master's a quiet man.",
	"Lojza, the mill hand. I do the lifting.",
	"I've no family here. The master and mistress keep me fed.",
	"The master's fair, and the mistress sneaks me extra bread.",
	"The sail tore again. The wind took a corner off. We'll patch it before harvest.",
	"The wind is steady, so I'm greasing the axle.",
	"The wheel's slick in rain, so I watch my footing.",
	"Fog. I can't see the sail.",
	"I climb up to clear the sails when it snows.",
	"The hamlets are my round, and the road gives me no trouble.",
	"Sacks vanish. The master blames me, then the carter.",
	"Lifting sacks, greasing the axle, listening for the wrong sound. Every day it's heavier.",
	"The sacks can wait, so there's no work today.",
	"Come at {meal}. The mistress cooks well.",
	"Hauling sacks to the grinding floor.",
	"Good day. You're on my land, but you're welcome.",
	"Right. A free man pays his debts, and a decent one remembers them.",
	"Dominik Sedlak, yeoman. My grandfather bought this farm with silver.",
	"My mother Vlasta, my wife Barbora, three children and two farmhands. A full table.",
	"Mother says the silver came off a dead knight. I say it's paid for now.",
	"Nobody takes land from a free man. The abbey tenants ask about our geese. I tell them to ask politely.",
	"The oxen pull easy and the hay is good. A fair day.",
	"When the rain comes I look at the rye and say a prayer.",
	"Fog swallows the farmhands. I only hear them arguing.",
	"Snow gives the oxen a rest, and me too.",
	"The roads are fine if you keep to them. I send my hands in pairs.",
	"Livestock thieves are the worst. I'll name them to the guards and I'll thank the one who saw it.",
	"The low field is thin, the high is good. The oxen are fed and so are the hands.",
	"Not a thing I'd give a stranger today. Ask me again tomorrow.",
	"There's a place for any guest at a yeoman's table. Come at {meal}.",
	"Walking the fields and counting the oxen.",
	"Come here, child. Let me look at you.",
	"Manners. That's rare, and I like it.",
	"Vlasta, old mother of this house. I knew them all as babies.",
	"Dominik is my son. Barbora's his wife. The children are mine to scold.",
	"Dominik forgets where the silver came from. I tell him. It keeps him humble.",
	"Everyone but Dominik knows the silver for this farm came off a dead knight.",
	"On a dry day I sit in the sun and watch the hands.",
	"My hip tells me about rain before the sky does.",
	"When I was young, fog meant the ghost of a knight.",
	"Snow sends the farmhands for wood. I count every log.",
	"I haven't been on the road in years. Dominik says it's safe, and he's usually right.",
	"A thief in my day got what he deserved. Now it's the guards and a fine.",
	"Everything was smaller when I was a girl, and the winters were longer. The land was ours even then.",
	"Nothing from me. Ask Barbora about the hens.",
	"An old woman likes a crowded table. Come at {meal}.",
	"Sitting by the hearth and watching the farmhands.",
	"Mind the geese, they bite.",
	"I'll tell the hands you said so. They'll be astonished.",
	"Barbora, Dominik's wife. I run the kitchen and the hens.",
	"Dominik, Vlasta, Florian, Anezka, Hugo, and two farmhands who eat like four.",
	"The farmhands ate the second loaf again. I've started counting.",
	"The hens are laying lightly. I need barley for the feed.",
	"Dry weather, and the washing's done in an hour.",
	"I keep a pot on and an eye on the roof while it pours.",
	"The geese vanish into the fog and reappear.",
	"Once the snow is down I feed twelve people on what I'd feed ten.",
	"I send eggs to the abbey by the road, which is safe enough. The monks are kind.",
	"Eggs go missing. If you see a man with a basket, tell the guards.",
	"Nobody goes hungry in a yeoman's house, thank God.",
	"The hens aren't laying enough to spare anything. No work today.",
	"There's room, if you don't mind the farmhands. Come at {meal}.",
	"Gathering eggs and counting the loaves.",
	"Careful, the oxen are in a mood today.",
	"Thanks. The oxen don't say it.",
	"Florian, the yeoman's eldest. I drive the oxen.",
	"Father, Grandmother, Mother, Anezka, Hugo and the hands. A busy house.",
	"The oxen only pull for me. Cenek says it's because I smell like them.",
	"The oxen won't pull for Cenek. Father says it's his temper.",
	"The ground is hard and the ploughing's slow. I'd like a bit of rain.",
	"Rain floods the furrows.",
	"Fog means I lead the oxen by hand.",
	"I haul wood on the sledge once snow lies.",
	"The abbey road is a safe track, so that's where I take the oxen.",
	"Last winter a yoke was stolen. Father paid a smith to make another.",
	"Ploughing, yoking, hauling. The oxen are patient, and so am I.",
	"Ask Father about the oats. There's nothing from me today.",
	"Mother keeps a full table. Come at {meal}.",
	"Yoking the oxen for the north field.",
	"Hello. Did you come by the abbey road? Did you see the monks?",
	"That was polite. Not everyone on this road is.",
	"Anezka, the yeoman's daughter. I carry the news and the eggs.",
	"Father, Mother, Grandmother and the boys. A lot of noise.",
	"Mother says I talk too much. Grandmother says I take after her.",
	"The abbey's tenants keep asking about our geese. They want twenty by Martinmas.",
	"I'll carry the eggs to the abbey. It's a pleasant walk.",
	"I sit by the fire with the mending when it rains.",
	"The fog makes the abbey bell sound close.",
	"We keep warm and trade stories while the snow falls.",
	"The monks wave when I walk the road to the abbey with the eggs. It's a fine road.",
	"Someone took our goose. We think it's the abbey tenants. I've said nothing.",
	"Carrying messages, collecting eggs, hearing the gossip. It's a lively farm.",
	"Nothing today. Ask Mother.",
	"Mother won't mind a stranger at {meal}. Bring your own cup.",
	"Carrying eggs to the abbey and gathering news.",
	"Hello. We're not supposed to talk on the job, so quickly.",
	"Thanks. The mistress is stingy with that.",
	"Libor, farmhand. I plough and hoe.",
	"No family here. The yeoman's table is my table.",
	"Cenek and I share a bed and a bowl. We argue, but he's decent.",
	"The mistress says we ate the whole second loaf. We worked the whole second field, though.",
	"The hoe runs easy. I might finish the row.",
	"Mud to the knees when it rains.",
	"The fog hides the end of the row.",
	"Snow. I clear the yard and feed the oxen.",
	"The market with the yeoman is a long road on foot.",
	"My hoe got stolen. I looked everywhere.",
	"Dawn to dusk, and a hard bed. But the bread is good and the master is fair.",
	"Nothing for you. The master decides.",
	"The mistress counts the loaves, so bring your own appetite at {meal}.",
	"Hoeing the north field.",
	"The oxen hate me, so keep your distance.",
	"Cheers. It's rare around here.",
	"Cenek, farmhand. I do what I'm told. The oxen don't.",
	"No family. The yeoman feeds me.",
	"Libor is a good mate. The oxen are another matter.",
	"The oxen won't pull for me. Florian says it's because I shout.",
	"Dry. I'll cart hay.",
	"I mend harness when it rains.",
	"Fog's bad. I lose the oxen.",
	"Snow. I sweep the yard.",
	"I haven't been on the road much. It's long.",
	"My knife was stolen. I think it was Libor, but it wasn't.",
	"Hauling, shovelling, being shouted at. It's work, and it's food.",
	"The master decides what's wanted. I just push the barrow.",
	"The mistress is stingy but fair. Come at {meal}.",
	"Mucking out the byre.",
	"Hello! Can I drive the oxen tomorrow?",
	"You're welcome! I'm big enough.",
	"I'm Hugo. I'm the youngest. I'm nearly big enough.",
	"Father, Mother, Grandmother, Florian, Anezka, Libor and Cenek. And me.",
	"Father says when I can reach the yoke. I'm growing.",
	"Father says I can drive the oxen when I can reach the yoke.",
	"Dry! I'll go and see the geese.",
	"Rain means puddles. I jump in them.",
	"I get lost in the fog and then somebody finds me.",
	"Snow! I make a goose out of snow.",
	"I'm not allowed on the road.",
	"If a thief came, the geese would bite him.",
	"Chasing geese, feeding hens, watching the oxen.",
	"I can't give you work. I'm still waiting for my own.",
	"There's lots at {meal}! You can sit by me.",
	"Chasing the geese out of the barley.",
	"God give you good day. You've come a long way, I think.",
	"Bless you. A kindness is a prayer.",
	"Benedikt Klasterny, the abbey's tenant. We keep the geese for the brothers.",
	"Marta, my wife. We keep the farm between us and the abbey.",
	"Neither of us can read. The Lord can read the psalter on the wall for us.",
	"The brothers want twenty geese by Martinmas. We have fifteen. We're praying for five more.",
	"The geese forage well when it's dry.",
	"Rain. The geese like it. I don't.",
	"When the abbey bell rings through the fog, I say a prayer.",
	"We bring the geese in when the snow comes.",
	"The abbey road is quiet. I walk to the brothers with the geese.",
	"A fox is a worse thief than a man. The guards can't bind a fox.",
	"Lean this year. The Lord provides the rest, and the brothers feed us.",
	"Nothing today. The brothers will send word when they need something.",
	"Bless this food, and the abbey that feeds us. Come at {meal}.",
	"Tending the geese and counting the flock.",
	"Come in, come in, and mind the gosling.",
	"God keep you. We get few thanks, and fewer visitors.",
	"Marta, Benedikt's wife. I look after the geese and the soup.",
	"Benedikt and me. The abbey is our other family.",
	"Everyone needs something from us. The brothers, the geese, and our own bellies.",
	"Brother Hugh needs the herb garden weeded before the frost. We have that work ahead.",
	"Dry enough for the geese to go out today.",
	"The rain makes the geese happy. It does nothing for me.",
	"Fog means a prayer for the brothers.",
	"Snow keeps the soup on the hearth all day.",
	"Feast days I go to the abbey. The road is quiet and the bell is clear.",
	"Eggs and goslings go missing. We pray for the culprit and tell the guards.",
	"What the abbey feeds us is enough. I'm grateful for it.",
	"Come back when Brother Hugh sends word. There's nothing today.",
	"There's soup, and we say grace first. Come at {meal}.",
	"Weeding the abbey herb garden and feeding the geese.",
	"Good day. Anything to cut, pull or set? No? Then stay a moment.",
	"Kind of you. Few people thank the man who pulls their tooth.",
	"Kasimir Ranhojic, barber-surgeon. I cut hair, pull teeth and set legs.",
	"Kordula is my wife. We keep this house, and a good many bandages.",
	"Kordula tells me to wash before I touch the bread. I tell her blood dries.",
	"Three men bled, a leg set and a tooth pulled today. A good day. It's not yet Vespers.",
	"Fine weather, and the men fall off ladders. Business is good.",
	"Wet weather brings me coughs and slipped knees. I'm busy.",
	"In fog the carts overturn and people walk into walls. I'll have patients by dusk.",
	"Snow gives me frostbite and broken wrists. I keep the fire hot.",
	"Patients come from the road with arrow wounds. I'd say it's not as safe as they claim.",
	"Thieves come to me with cuts. I patch them, then the guards take them away.",
	"Bleeding, bandaging and lancing. It's no more than a craft, whatever the monks say.",
	"The shelves are full and the herbs are in. I've nothing for you today.",
	"Kordula keeps a clean table, whatever she says of me. Come at {meal}.",
	"Boiling bandages and sharpening the lancets.",
	"Hello. Wipe your feet. The surgeon brings in blood.",
	"That's kind of you to say. Kasimir hears it from patients, never from visitors.",
	"Kordula, Kasimir's wife. I wash what he stains.",
	"Kasimir and me. We've no children, only patients.",
	"I tell him the herb widow's patients live. He says that's luck.",
	"Kasimir bleeds half the village. The herb widow in the other village says he bleeds them dry.",
	"A good day to boil the linens.",
	"Bandages hang for days in the rain and never dry.",
	"Patients wander in lost when the fog is down.",
	"The shop smells of vinegar and wool when it snows.",
	"Patients come from the road. I hear stories I'd rather not.",
	"A thief came in with a gash. Kasimir stitched him and the guards came after.",
	"Plenty on the table, and a surgeon's house is never short of coin. It does smell.",
	"Ask Kasimir about linen. I've no work today.",
	"Don't ask what's in the sausage. Come at {meal}.",
	"Washing bandages and arranging the herbs.",
	"Good day. Keep clear of the rivet bench.",
	"Obliged. The duke doesn't say it.",
	"Bernard Platnyr, armourer. I make mail and plate for the duke's men.",
	"Adelheid is my wife. Fridrich and Wenzel are journeymen and live under our roof.",
	"Adelheid says I fell asleep at the bench last {season}. She's right.",
	"The duke wants forty mail shirts before the spring muster. We've rivets for ten.",
	"Iron won't rust in dry air. The rivets sit well.",
	"I oil everything in the rain and keep the forge covered.",
	"Fog. I can't see my own hammer, so I stop work.",
	"Once it snows, the forge is the only warm room in the village.",
	"The duke's men escort the mail. The road's safe enough when the guards ride it.",
	"Tools get stolen. A rivet is worth a coin. A thief who takes them gets bound.",
	"One bad ring and the whole shirt fails. I check every one, and the duke wants forty.",
	"Nothing today. Ask me when the duke's order is in.",
	"Adelheid keeps a hearty table. Come at {meal}.",
	"Riveting mail rings, one by one.",
	"Hello. Don't touch the rings, they're sharp.",
	"That's kind. The men only grunt.",
	"Adelheid, Bernard's wife. I feed the forge and its keepers.",
	"Bernard, Fridrich and Wenzel. Three men who eat like twenty.",
	"Bernard promised to sleep at night. He sleeps at the bench.",
	"The duke wants forty shirts. Bernard says he'll work all night. I say the bench is hard.",
	"I'll air all the rooms.",
	"In the rain the forge smoke hangs in my kitchen.",
	"With fog outside and smoke inside, I can't see the hearth.",
	"The men are underfoot all day when it snows.",
	"The road is the duke's business. I keep to the village.",
	"Rivets go missing. If you see someone, say so.",
	"We eat well, and the duke pays. I'd like him to pay faster.",
	"Ask Bernard. I've nothing today.",
	"There's room at {meal}. Bring an appetite, the men eat like twenty.",
	"Cooking for four hungry men.",
	"Hello. Mind the sparks.",
	"Cheers. The master would call that soft, but I'll take it.",
	"Fridrich, journeyman. I make rivets and rings.",
	"I lodge with the master. His wife keeps us fed.",
	"Wenzel and I share a room. He snores.",
	"Forty mail shirts wanted and rivets for ten. The master says we'll make more.",
	"The forge runs hot. I'm wet through already.",
	"Rain on the roof. The forge is cosy.",
	"Charcoal smoke sits low when there's fog.",
	"I stand by the forge when it snows.",
	"I walked to the village. The road was clear.",
	"A handful of rivets went missing. The master blamed me.",
	"Rivets all night, rings all day. My hands are black now, but I'll be a master someday.",
	"The master has the orders. He's not mentioned any today.",
	"The mistress's soup is the best in the village. Come at {meal}.",
	"Making rivets until the master says stop.",
	"Hello. Careful, that's hot.",
	"Cheers. You're all right.",
	"Wenzel, journeyman. I polish plate.",
	"I lodge with the master. Fridrich snores.",
	"The mistress feeds us well. The master works us hard.",
	"The smith in Talmberg can't make a helmet that fits a child. Fridrich says it's funny.",
	"The polish takes well in this air.",
	"Rain means oiling the plate.",
	"Fog. The metal sweats.",
	"Plate is cold in snow, and my fingers go numb.",
	"I haven't been out much. The road to Talmberg is long.",
	"A polishing cloth is gone. I think Fridrich.",
	"Polishing, scouring, hauling. It's hard on the hands.",
	"Nothing from the bench today. The master will say.",
	"There's plenty at {meal}. Fridrich snores, but he's decent company.",
	"Polishing a breastplate until it shines.",
	"Good day. Do stand clear of the ink, please.",
	"Thank you kindly. God keep you.",
	"Jeronym Kancler, scribe to the duke. I write what he says, and sometimes what he means.",
	"Bertold keeps the house. Other than him, I live alone with my pen.",
	"A scribe has few visitors. When one comes, I'm glad.",
	"The duke's letters go out at Prime. Twelve letters, twelve seals. I've a long morning ahead.",
	"The ink flows evenly today. I give thanks for it.",
	"Rain curls the parchment. I lose a morning to it.",
	"Fog makes the ink slow and the candle dim.",
	"The ink froze last night. Bertold warmed it, though he thinks I didn't see.",
	"The duke's riders carry my letters. Whatever the road is like, they arrive.",
	"A stolen letter is worse than a stolen purse. The guards take it seriously.",
	"The duke's dues, the duke's debts. I write it all and nobody asks me what I think of it.",
	"No work until the duke's letters are sealed. Come back then.",
	"You're the first guest we've had since the season began. Sit and eat at {meal}.",
	"Writing the duke's letters. Eight left.",
	"Good day. The master is writing. Please speak softly.",
	"That's kind. The master will be pleased to hear it.",
	"Bertold, the scribe's manservant. I keep the house and the fire.",
	"The master and me. We've no one else.",
	"The master is gentle but works too long. I make sure he eats.",
	"The ink froze in the pot last night. I warmed it by the fire. Don't tell him.",
	"Dry air is kind to the parchment. The master is cheerful.",
	"He sighs when the rain curls his parchment.",
	"Fog. I light every lamp.",
	"In snow I stack firewood to the ceiling.",
	"I don't leave the house. The master's letters leave without me.",
	"Nothing's been stolen. The master keeps the door locked. I keep the key.",
	"Dawn till dark I keep the fire, the ink and the master fed. It's quiet, good work.",
	"Nothing from me. The master gives the errands.",
	"The master will be glad of company. Come at {meal}.",
	"Fetching wood and warming the ink.",
	"Quiet, please. The hounds are listening.",
	"Thanks. The duke's hounds appreciate it too.",
	"Gotthard Lesmistr, the duke's huntsman. I keep the hounds fit and the boar away.",
	"Rosina is my wife. Adela and Lambert, our children. And twenty hounds.",
	"Adela wants to ride on the hunt. I say when she can sit a gallop.",
	"The duke hunts on Thursday. Every hound has to be fit, and the old bitch is lame.",
	"Dry ground holds the scent and the hounds run well.",
	"Rain washes the scent. I keep the pack in.",
	"Fog. We don't hunt in fog.",
	"Tracks show clear in snow, and the hounds are happy.",
	"The duke's men patrol. Poachers hide in the east wood, and I'll find them.",
	"Poachers set snares. If you see a snare, tell the guards, and tell me.",
	"Poachers want the duke's deer and boar lurk in the thickets. I watch for both.",
	"The hunt is on Thursday and everything's in hand. No work today.",
	"Rosina keeps a good table. Come at {meal}.",
	"Walking the hounds and checking their paws.",
	"Hello. Wipe your feet, the hounds track mud everywhere.",
	"It's rare in a house full of hounds. I'm glad of it.",
	"Rosina, the huntsman's wife. I keep the house and the leftovers.",
	"Gotthard, Adela and Lambert. And the hounds, who think they live here too.",
	"Gotthard's a good man. He's out at dawn and home at dusk, and smells of dog.",
	"The forester found snares in the east wood. Gotthard is furious.",
	"The washing smells of hound, as ever.",
	"The kennel floods in the rain.",
	"The hounds howl all through the fog.",
	"Snow. We keep the fire high.",
	"The road is for the hunt. I stay home with the hounds.",
	"Poachers again. If you see anything, tell the guards.",
	"We eat venison and sleep with dogs. It's a good house, if a loud one.",
	"Ask Gotthard about that. Nothing from me today.",
	"There's venison at {meal}. Come and eat.",
	"Cooking for the hunt and washing hound blankets.",
	"Hello! Have you been on a hunt? I'm going on Thursday.",
	"Thank you! Father says I must say that.",
	"Adela. The huntsman's daughter. I ride better than Lambert.",
	"Father, Mother, Lambert. And the hounds.",
	"Father says when I can sit a gallop. I can, but he doesn't believe me.",
	"The duke hunts on Thursday. I'd like to ride. Father says no.",
	"Perfect for riding the paddock.",
	"The hounds are so loud when it rains.",
	"Fog. I'll groom the horse.",
	"The hounds go mad for snow.",
	"I ride the forest tracks. The road's too dull.",
	"I'd chase a thief on horseback. Father says that's a bad idea.",
	"Riding, feeding hounds, dreaming of the hunt. It's the best house in the village.",
	"Father decides what gets done. He hasn't said anything.",
	"Mother makes venison. Come at {meal}, and sit by me.",
	"Grooming my horse and counting the days until Thursday.",
	"Hello. Careful, that hound bites.",
	"Cheers. I'll tell the pack.",
	"Lambert, the huntsman's son. I feed the hounds.",
	"Father, Mother, Adela. And twenty hounds.",
	"The old bitch is lame. I told Father, and he wasn't pleased.",
	"Father says the old bitch is lame and stays home. We pray for no boar.",
	"The hounds run and I run after them.",
	"The kennel stinks worse in the rain.",
	"The hounds get restless in fog.",
	"I still carry meat to the kennel, snow or not.",
	"I follow Father on the forest tracks. The road's for carts.",
	"A haunch of venison went missing and I'm blamed.",
	"Feeding, walking, brushing. The hounds love me, and the old bitch loves me most.",
	"The hounds need me. Ask Father if there's anything else.",
	"Mother cooks the hounds' scraps too. Come at {meal}.",
	"Carrying meat to the kennel.",
	"Come in, come in! Out of the wind. Take the seat by the fire.",
	"My pleasure. A kind word warms anyone on the pass.",
	"Walter Horsky. I keep the inn on the pass. Everyone stops here, or freezes.",
	"My mother Notburga, and Fricek the stable boy. Between us we feed whoever comes.",
	"Mother runs the kitchen and the inn. I run the money. She runs me.",
	"Three travellers are stuck by the snow on the pass. They'll drink us dry.",
	"The pass is clear, so the travellers keep coming. I'm watching the kegs.",
	"Up here rain turns to sleet by evening.",
	"Fog on the pass. Travellers lose the road and find the inn.",
	"The inn is full in snow, and the cellar's low.",
	"The pass is high, and bandits are fewer than the cold. Keep to the road and you'll arrive.",
	"Thieves rarely come up here. If you see one, tell the guards, though they're far.",
	"Winter's slow and summer's busy. The travellers who cross the pass drink like they've been to war.",
	"Nothing today. Ask me when the next coach is due.",
	"Warm yourself first. The pass is cold this {season}. Come at {meal}.",
	"Stacking firewood and counting the kegs.",
	"Come in, traveller. You look half frozen.",
	"Thank you for it. It's a long way up, and a kind word is rare.",
	"Notburga, Walter's mother. I run the kitchen and most of the talk.",
	"Walter, my son. Fricek, the stable boy, though he's more like a grandson.",
	"Walter worries about the money. I worry about the people.",
	"A merchant came through with news of a war in the south. Walter says there's always a war in the south.",
	"The pass is quiet, and so is the taproom.",
	"The stable roof leaks every time it rains.",
	"In fog we keep a lantern burning for the lost.",
	"We're full to the rafters in snow, but I've soup for all.",
	"Travellers say the road is bad in winter. I say it's bad in every season.",
	"Thieves are rare up here. The cold kills them first.",
	"Pouring, cooking, listening. Travellers tell me all manner of things.",
	"Wood or oats, ask Walter. I've nothing today.",
	"Get some warmth into you first. Come at {meal}, and I'll bring the pot.",
	"Stirring the pot for tonight's guests.",
	"Hello! Do you have a horse? The grey kicks.",
	"No trouble! Do you want to see the horses?",
	"I'm Fricek. I look after the horses.",
	"Walter and Notburga. And twelve horses, sometimes.",
	"Notburga says I'm like her own. I think she means I eat a lot.",
	"The grey horse kicked me again. Walter says stand where she can't reach.",
	"Dry! The horses can graze outside.",
	"The grey hates the rain on the stable roof, and so do I.",
	"The horses are nervous in fog.",
	"Snow. I carry hay in till my arms ache.",
	"I know the pass well. I wouldn't go far alone.",
	"Horse thieves are the worst. I'd shout for Walter.",
	"Brushing, feeding, mucking out. The horses are good company.",
	"Ask Walter. I haven't got any work.",
	"Notburga cooks mutton. Come at {meal}!",
	"Brushing the grey and avoiding her back legs.",
	"Peace be with you. Are you hurt, or only visiting the sick?",
	"God repay you. A kind word is welcome in a room full of the sick.",
	"Brother Hugh. I keep the infirmary and the herb garden behind it.",
	"The brothers of the abbey are my family. I came here young and I've not been outside the walls much since.",
	"I don't miss a wife or children. I do miss my mother, and I say a prayer for her each Compline.",
	"The tenants keep our geese and bring herbs for the kitchen. Someone has to weed the garden before the frost.",
	"Good drying weather for the herbs. The sick are sitting out in the cloister.",
	"Damp coughs in every bed, with this rain blowing into the cloister.",
	"I keep the infirmary fire lit in this murk and pray for anyone still out on the roads.",
	"Snow means frostbite, chilblains and old bones aching. The beds will fill before Compline.",
	"The wardens keep the main roads. The ones who come to me hurt have usually left them.",
	"A thief who comes to me wounded is treated first and handed over after. The guards do the rest.",
	"There's always someone in a bed. Fevers, bad wounds, and old people who only want warmth.",
	"Nothing for you today. The garden is weeded and the shelves are stocked.",
	"The refectory feeds the brothers. For a meal as a guest, ask at one of the village houses.",
	"Walking the beds, then the garden. The sick come first.",
	"Good day. If it's the knife you need, say so. If it's a draught, ask Brother Hugh.",
	"Nobody thanks the man with the knife, as a rule. I'm glad you did.",
	"Brother Osmund. A lay brother, so I do the cutting the other brothers may not.",
	"I've a sister in Talmberg and a brother who farms. The abbey took me in when I could do no better.",
	"My brother thinks I'm wasted here. My sister says I'm the only one who ever visits.",
	"Brother Hugh says the garden needs weeding. I say a bad wound is worse than weeds, but I'll do both.",
	"Fewer festering wounds when it's dry. I'll scrub the table twice anyway.",
	"Every cut that comes through the door has mud in it when it rains.",
	"People fall down stairs they can't see in this. I expect company.",
	"Cold hands slip on tools. I see a lot of slipped hands in the snow.",
	"A man came in last week from the woods with a bad cut. Keep to the roads, they're watched.",
	"Someone took a bandage roll. I'd like to say it was a thief with an honest wound.",
	"The sick come in worse than they leave, thank God. Mostly. The cuts I see are not pretty.",
	"The table is scrubbed and the knives are clean. There's no work for you.",
	"Not here. The refectory is for the brothers. Ask at a village house.",
	"Scrubbing the table and sharpening the lancets.",
	"Peace be with you. Come in out of the cold, and mind the candles.",
	"God repay you. Few people thank a priest who has only done his duty.",
	"Father Anselm. I say mass here and keep the altar.",
	"The Church is my family now. The parish is all the children I'll have.",
	"I had a brother once. I pray for him at every mass.",
	"The bells ring for mass three times a day. Come to one and kneel when the host is raised.",
	"Dry weather fills the pews. The farmers come to give thanks before the harvest.",
	"Rain drums on the roof all through the Gloria. The people sing louder to be heard.",
	"In this fog the bell is the only way the parish finds the church.",
	"Snow on the road, and half my flock stays home. God knows who is truly sick.",
	"Pray before you travel. The wardens watch the roads, but they can't be everywhere.",
	"A thief who confesses to God sleeps better than one the guards catch. Neither sleeps well.",
	"The parish is well. A few sick to visit, and a wedding or two if God is kind.",
	"Nothing for you today. The candles are trimmed and the floor is swept.",
	"I eat with the brothers in the refectory. For a meal as a guest, ask at a village house.",
	"At prayer, then the missal, then the candles. The altar needs me most hours of the day."
};

enum E_FAMILY_TRADE_ROW_DEF
{
	E_FAMILY_TRADE_ROW_MEMBER,
	E_FAMILY_TRADE_ROW_ACTION,
	E_FAMILY_TRADE_ROW_QUESTION[88],
	E_FAMILY_TRADE_ROW_ANSWER[144],
}

new const g_FamilyTradeRowDefs[188][E_FAMILY_TRADE_ROW_DEF] = {
	{6, 0, "What cloth do you weave?", "Wool, mostly, and some linen. Grey, brown, and whatever colour the dyers send me."},
	{6, 0, "Can I buy cloth from you?", "Not by the ell from the loom. My bolts go to the merchants. Ask at the market stalls if you want cloth."},
	{6, 0, "What colours can you get?", "Grey, brown and black are easy. Blue is woad, and the dyer sisters in the church village hold what there is."},
	{6, 1, "Can I lend a hand with the weaving?", "You're in luck. I was about to go looking for someone."},
	{9, 0, "What are you writing at the moment?", "The tax rolls for the count. Every sheaf and sack goes down in ink."},
	{9, 1, "Is there copying I could help with?", "I won't turn down a willing back. Here's the job."},
	{13, 0, "What do you bake?", "Rye loaves, white bread for feast days, and bread rings. Whatever the flour allows."},
	{13, 0, "Can I buy a loaf?", "Fresh bread goes to the village first. If you want one, ask Klara at the counter, she has the day's tally."},
	{13, 1, "Do you need a hand at the oven?", "There is a job, and it's yours if you want it."},
	{15, 0, "What does an apprentice baker do?", "Sift, knead, haul wood, and burn the second batch. I'm still learning the rest."},
	{15, 1, "Can I help with the baking?", "Aye, if you've the time. I'll tell you what I need."},
	{16, 0, "What do you build?", "Tables, benches, scaffolds, doors. Anything the village needs that stands on wood."},
	{16, 0, "Can you mend a table?", "Of course. The innkeeper wants one fixed, and I told him to pay in coin."},
	{16, 1, "Do you need a hand in the workshop?", "Always. Come closer and I'll tell you."},
	{17, 0, "What are you working on?", "A scaffold for the mason, and a table for the inn. Father checks every joint."},
	{17, 1, "Can I help with the carpentry?", "Possibly. I'll tell you what I'd want fetched."},
	{18, 0, "What do you make?", "Stools, mostly. Father lets me do the small work and the cooking."},
	{18, 1, "Is there a job I can help with?", "Another pair of hands never hurts. I've a job for you."},
	{19, 0, "Have you a room for the night?", "There are rooms upstairs. The county steward lets them, so ask at the reeve's clerk's house."},
	{19, 2, "Can I have a meal here?", "You can. Sit down and I'll see what the kitchen has."},
	{19, 0, "What ale do you pour?", "Ale from the brewer, mostly. This week it's a bit sour. We call it a new kind."},
	{19, 0, "Any news from the road?", "Always. Come and sit, and I'll tell you what the travellers said."},
	{20, 2, "Can I have a meal here?", "Take a seat. I'll see what's in the pot."},
	{20, 0, "What's cooking today?", "Pottage, bread, and cheese. If a guest is hungry, he eats."},
	{21, 0, "What do you pour here?", "Ale, mostly. Plain, and the brewer's sour one this week."},
	{21, 0, "Who stays upstairs?", "I can't say. What happens in our rooms stays in our rooms."},
	{22, 0, "What's on tap tonight?", "Ale, and a sour one the brewer sent. We call it new."},
	{22, 1, "Can I help with the kegs?", "Maybe. Let me think what's most pressing."},
	{23, 0, "What do you make?", "Mugs, bowls, jugs and pots. Anything the village eats or drinks from."},
	{23, 0, "Can I buy a pot from you?", "The innkeeper has my mugs. For a pot of your own, ask me again when the kiln is open."},
	{23, 1, "Can I lend a hand at the wheel?", "A bit of help would be welcome. This is what I need."},
	{24, 0, "What are you working on?", "Mugs for the inn. Twenty of them, all the same size. It's harder than it sounds."},
	{24, 1, "Do you need any help?", "There's always something wanting doing. Here, I've a job."},
	{25, 0, "What do you keep?", "Cows for milk, sheep for wool, and one ox that thinks he's the master."},
	{25, 0, "Can I buy cheese?", "Ours goes to market. The cheesemaker's table is the place to ask, not my byre."},
	{25, 1, "Do you need a hand with the herd?", "Good of you to ask. I've a job that wants doing."},
	{29, 0, "What does an orchard keeper do?", "Apples, pears, a few plums, and the bees. Cider in autumn if the worms leave enough."},
	{29, 0, "Can I buy honey or apples?", "When there's a surplus, the market stalls have it. I don't sell from the gate."},
	{29, 1, "Do you need help in the orchard?", "If you can spare an hour. Here, I'll explain."},
	{31, 0, "What do you build?", "Walls, arches, steps and chimneys. Anything that has to stand a hundred winters."},
	{31, 0, "Can you repair a wall?", "The castle wall wants it. Someone has to pay first, and the count pays in promises."},
	{31, 1, "Do you need help on the site?", "Now you mention it, yes. I've a job in mind."},
	{33, 0, "What does a journeyman do?", "I cut and carry for the master. One day I'll sign my own work."},
	{33, 1, "Can I help with the stonework?", "If you're willing, yes. Right, here's what I need."},
	{34, 0, "What does a forester do?", "Mark the trees, keep the poachers off, and count what's fallen. Boar and fire are the worst of it."},
	{34, 0, "Can I buy firewood?", "Ask at the market. The count owns the timber, and I only mark what may be cut."},
	{34, 1, "Need a hand in the wood?", "Yes, as it happens. I'd have asked you in a minute."},
	{35, 0, "What do you burn?", "Pine, mostly. Slow, covered with turf, and no air. That's how you make good charcoal."},
	{35, 1, "Can I carry something for you?", "Yes, as it happens. Something needs doing today."},
	{36, 0, "What do you sew?", "Coats, tunics, cloaks, hose. Anything a body wears, I'll cut it to fit."},
	{36, 0, "What colours do you have?", "Black, brown, grey, and Lida's blue. Yellow from onion skins, when she has them."},
	{36, 0, "Can you make me clothes?", "I make for the village and the clerk's family. Ask me when the black coat is done."},
	{36, 1, "Can I help with the tailoring?", "I could. It's honest work, though not rich pay."},
	{37, 0, "What colours do you dye?", "Blue from woad, yellow from onion skins, brown from bark, and black when I have the oak galls."},
	{37, 0, "Can you dye cloth for me?", "For the village, in turn. The pot's full of the crofter's green this week."},
	{37, 1, "Can I help with the dyeing?", "Aye, there is. It won't do itself."},
	{38, 0, "What do you trade in?", "Salt, cloth, wax, spices when I can get them. Anything that sells twice."},
	{38, 0, "What is the price of salt?", "A third more than last year, and I'd rather it weren't. The toll bar does that."},
	{38, 1, "Do you have errands going out?", "I'd be glad of it. Here's what needs doing."},
	{40, 0, "What do you sell?", "Whatever Father trusts me with. Mostly salt and wax at the moment."},
	{40, 1, "Can I help with a delivery?", "Hands I can use. Listen, here's what I need."},
	{43, 0, "What do you make?", "Barrels, kegs, buckets and tubs. Anything that holds wet."},
	{43, 0, "Can you mend a barrel?", "If the staves are sound, I can tighten the hoops and reset the head. It takes an afternoon."},
	{43, 1, "Do you need help in the cooperage?", "There might be something. Give me a moment."},
	{44, 0, "What do you brew?", "Ale, mostly. A thin one for the fields, a strong one for the inn. Mead when Bozena has honey."},
	{44, 0, "Can I buy ale from you?", "The inn pours mine. If you want a barrel, the cooper makes them. I don't sell by the cup."},
	{44, 1, "Do you need help at the brewhouse?", "You've caught me at a good moment. I've a task."},
	{45, 0, "Can I buy a jug of ale?", "The inn pours it. I only brew it. Ask the innkeeper."},
	{45, 1, "Do you need help with the brewing?", "You're in luck. I was about to go looking for someone."},
	{48, 0, "What do you make?", "Nails, hinges, horseshoes, knives, axe heads. Anything that wants hammering."},
	{48, 0, "What goods do you have for sale?", "Nails, hinges, blades. Most of it's ordered already, and the count's men come before everyone."},
	{48, 0, "Can you mend my tools?", "A cracked axe or a bent blade, I can mend either. The count's spears come first, so don't bring me anything today."},
	{48, 1, "Can I lend a hand at the forge?", "I won't turn down a willing back. Here's the job."},
	{50, 0, "What does an apprentice smith do?", "I blow the bellows, quench, carry, and make mistakes. The master fixes them."},
	{50, 1, "Can I help at the forge?", "There is a job, and it's yours if you want it."},
	{55, 0, "What cloth do you make?", "Plain woven wool and linen, dyed to order. Blue, when the woad behaves."},
	{55, 0, "What colours can you dye?", "Blue from woad, green by dipping blue in yellow, brown from bark. Red is dear and I rarely have it."},
	{55, 0, "Can I buy cloth or dye?", "We dye what's brought to us. Ask for a bolt at the market, we don't sell from the door."},
	{55, 1, "Can I help at the dye house?", "Aye, if you've the time. I'll tell you what I need."},
	{56, 0, "What do you weave?", "Blankets, plain cloth, and whatever colour the vat gives us. The crofter's baby wants green."},
	{56, 1, "Can I carry a skein for you?", "Always. Come closer and I'll tell you."},
	{57, 0, "What do you make?", "Cart wheels, wagon beds, yokes and handles. Kristof does the bows."},
	{57, 0, "Can you mend a wheel?", "If the hub is sound, a wheel can be saved. New felloes cost wood and I don't work on credit."},
	{57, 1, "Do you need a hand in the shop?", "Possibly. I'll tell you what I'd want fetched."},
	{59, 0, "What do you make?", "Bows. Hunting bows, mostly, from yew when I can get it, ash when I can't."},
	{59, 0, "Can I buy a bow?", "Not off the rack. The hunter ordered one, and the yew's short. Ask me when I have a stave."},
	{59, 1, "Can I help find stave wood?", "Another pair of hands never hurts. I've a job for you."},
	{60, 0, "What do you bake?", "Rye loaves, white bread for feasts, and pretzels when the Hare asks."},
	{60, 0, "Can I buy bread?", "The village oven is for everyone. Come at the bell, and Bohumil will count the loaves."},
	{60, 1, "Can I help at the oven?", "Maybe. Let me think what's most pressing."},
	{61, 0, "What do you bake?", "Rye loaves, white bread, pretzel rings for the alehouse. Whatever Mother says."},
	{61, 1, "Can I help with the baking?", "A bit of help would be welcome. This is what I need."},
	{62, 0, "What can you treat?", "Fevers with feverfew, bones with comfrey, coughs with honey. For the rest, I pray."},
	{62, 0, "Can you heal me?", "Not with a word. Keep warm, eat well and keep a wound clean. Past that, you need rest more than me."},
	{62, 1, "Do you need herbs?", "There's always something wanting doing. Here, I've a job."},
	{63, 0, "What do you make?", "Tallow candles for the houses, wax ones for the church. And rushlights for the poor."},
	{63, 0, "Can I buy a candle?", "Not at the door. The church takes most of them, and the rest go to the market stalls."},
	{63, 1, "Can I help with the candles?", "Good of you to ask. I've a job that wants doing."},
	{66, 0, "What do you do in the shop?", "Dip candles, trim wicks, and scrape tallow. It's slow work and it smells."},
	{66, 1, "Can I help in the shop?", "If you can spare an hour. Here, I'll explain."},
	{67, 0, "Have you a room for the night?", "No beds at the Hare. Rooms to let are on the county steward's roll, in the reeve's clerk's house in Talmberg."},
	{67, 0, "What ale do you pour?", "The brewer's, by the cup. The first one's a welcome, and the second one is a penny."},
	{67, 0, "Any news from the road?", "Always. Sit down, and I'll tell you what the carters said."},
	{67, 1, "Can I help at the alehouse?", "Now you mention it, yes. I've a job in mind."},
	{68, 2, "What's in the pot tonight?", "Sausage, bread, pottage. Whatever the day brought."},
	{68, 1, "Can I help in the alehouse?", "If you're willing, yes. Right, here's what I need."},
	{69, 0, "What do you catch?", "Eels in autumn, trout in spring, whatever bites the rest of the year."},
	{69, 0, "Can I buy fish?", "Fast days, the whole county wants fish. Come early, and I may have a catch to spare."},
	{69, 1, "Can I help with the fishing?", "Yes, as it happens. I'd have asked you in a minute."},
	{73, 0, "What do you do?", "Roofs. Reed or straw, bound in bundles and laid in courses. A good roof lasts thirty years."},
	{73, 0, "Can you mend a roof?", "If the rafters are sound, I can. The reed bed flooded this year, so good reed is scarce."},
	{73, 1, "Can I help with the thatching?", "Yes, as it happens. Something needs doing today."},
	{75, 0, "What do you sell?", "Sausage, ham, blood pudding, lard. Whatever the pigs allow."},
	{75, 0, "Can I buy meat?", "Ask at the market stalls. I sell to the traders and the alehouses first."},
	{75, 1, "Do you need a hand?", "I could. It's honest work, though not rich pay."},
	{77, 0, "What does a butcher's son do?", "Breaks pigs, grinds sausage, and carries salt. I'm still learning the knife."},
	{77, 1, "Can I help in the shop?", "Aye, there is. It won't do itself."},
	{79, 0, "What does a journeyman butcher do?", "Splits carcasses, grinds meat, scrubs the block. The master checks every cut."},
	{79, 1, "Can I help with the butchery?", "I'd be glad of it. Here's what needs doing."},
	{80, 0, "What do you make?", "Leather. Soles, straps, harness. It takes bark, salt, and time."},
	{80, 0, "Can you make me boots?", "I tan the leather. A cobbler makes the boots. Ask in Talmberg."},
	{80, 1, "Can I help at the tannery?", "Hands I can use. Listen, here's what I need."},
	{81, 0, "What does a tanner's son do?", "Strip bark, haul hides, and turn them in the tubs. The smell never leaves."},
	{81, 1, "Can I help gather bark?", "There might be something. Give me a moment."},
	{84, 0, "What do you keep?", "Bees. Honey for the table, wax for the chandler, and mead when the batch is right."},
	{84, 0, "Can I buy honey or mead?", "The chandler's cousin takes the wax. For honey, ask me when the jars are full."},
	{84, 1, "Can I help with the bees?", "You've caught me at a good moment. I've a task."},
	{88, 0, "What do you keep?", "Sheep, mostly. Wool for the weaver and mutton for the pot."},
	{88, 0, "Can I buy wool or cheese?", "The weaver in Talmberg wants all the fleeces. I keep a wedge of cheese for guests."},
	{88, 1, "Can I help with the flock?", "You're in luck. I was about to go looking for someone."},
	{92, 0, "What do you hunt?", "Boar, deer, fox. Anything the count doesn't mind me taking, and some he does."},
	{92, 0, "Can I buy venison or a pelt?", "Pelts go to the traders. Venison is for my table, but a guest eats well."},
	{92, 1, "Can I help with the hunt?", "I won't turn down a willing back. Here's the job."},
	{94, 0, "What do you mine?", "Iron, mostly. The new seam is good, though the smith says it's full of slag."},
	{94, 0, "Can I buy ore?", "The smith takes what comes up. Ask in Talmberg if you need iron."},
	{94, 1, "Can I help at the mine?", "There is a job, and it's yours if you want it."},
	{96, 0, "What do you do in the mine?", "Hold the lamp, haul baskets, and listen to the timbers. Father says listen well."},
	{96, 1, "Can I help carry?", "Aye, if you've the time. I'll tell you what I need."},
	{101, 0, "What do you grind?", "Wheat, rye, barley, oats. Whatever the farmers bring. I keep a share for the work."},
	{101, 0, "Can I buy flour?", "The baker takes most of it. If there's a sack left, ask Vilma at the door."},
	{101, 1, "Do you need a hand at the mill?", "Always. Come closer and I'll tell you."},
	{104, 0, "What does a miller's son do?", "Weigh, sack, and carry. One day I'll set the stones myself."},
	{104, 1, "Can I help carry flour?", "Possibly. I'll tell you what I'd want fetched."},
	{105, 0, "What does a mill hand do?", "Haul, grease, patch, and stop the sail with a rope when the wind turns."},
	{105, 1, "Can I help at the mill?", "Another pair of hands never hurts. I've a job for you."},
	{116, 0, "What can you treat?", "A cut, a broken bone, a bad tooth, a boil. Bleeding for the humours, if you believe in it."},
	{116, 0, "Can you heal my wounds?", "A cut wants cleaning and covering, a bone wants a splint. For the worst, the monks at the abbey do more than I can."},
	{116, 1, "Do you need help with the shop?", "Maybe. Let me think what's most pressing."},
	{118, 0, "What do you make?", "Mail shirts, helmets, plate for the duke's men. Anything that stops a blade."},
	{118, 0, "Can you mend my armour?", "Dented plate and broken rings I can mend. The duke's forty shirts come first, so it'll have to wait."},
	{118, 1, "Can I help at the forge?", "A bit of help would be welcome. This is what I need."},
	{120, 0, "What does a journeyman armourer do?", "Rivets, rings, polish. The master checks every one."},
	{120, 1, "Can I help with the rivets?", "There's always something wanting doing. Here, I've a job."},
	{121, 0, "What do you do?", "Polish, fit and oil plate. A dull cuirass is a poor sight."},
	{121, 1, "Can I help with the polishing?", "Good of you to ask. I've a job that wants doing."},
	{122, 0, "What do you write?", "The duke's letters, mostly. Decrees, dues, summons. Anything with a seal."},
	{122, 0, "Can you read or write something for me?", "A scribe writes for the duke. For a common letter, ask the courier or the clerk in Talmberg."},
	{122, 1, "Do you need help with the writing?", "If you can spare an hour. Here, I'll explain."},
	{123, 0, "What do you do for the scribe?", "I keep the fire, the ink pot and the master fed. A scribe can't write cold."},
	{123, 1, "Can I carry a letter for you?", "Now you mention it, yes. I've a job in mind."},
	{124, 0, "What do you keep?", "The duke's hounds, twenty-odd, and a good many hunting horns."},
	{124, 0, "Can I see the hounds?", "Not in the kennel. Hounds don't take to strangers before a hunt."},
	{124, 1, "Can I help the hunt?", "If you're willing, yes. Right, here's what I need."},
	{127, 0, "What do you do with the hounds?", "Feed, walk, brush and doctor them. Twenty hounds, and each one has a name."},
	{127, 1, "Can I help?", "Yes, as it happens. I'd have asked you in a minute."},
	{128, 0, "Have you a room for the night?", "There's a dormitory above the taproom. The county steward lets it, so ask at the reeve's clerk's house in Talmberg."},
	{128, 2, "Can I have a meal here?", "Take a seat by the fire. We'll see what's in the pot."},
	{128, 0, "What's on tap?", "Ale from the lowland, and hot spiced wine when we have it."},
	{128, 0, "Any news from the pass?", "Always. Sit down, and I'll tell you what the merchant said."},
	{129, 2, "Can I have a meal here?", "Of course. Sit by the fire, and I'll bring the pot."},
	{129, 0, "What's in the pot?", "Barley, mutton, whatever the travellers left. It's hot."},
	{130, 0, "What do you do?", "Feed, water and brush the guests' horses. The grey kicks, but she's mine."},
	{130, 1, "Can I help with the horses?", "Yes, as it happens. Something needs doing today."},
	{131, 0, "Can you treat my wounds?", "I can, for a fee. You pay first, lie on a free bed, and I come over and see to each wound in turn."},
	{131, 0, "What do you charge?", "Looking over {fee_examine}, binding {fee_bandage}, washing {fee_clean}, a splint {fee_splint}, a draught {fee_remedy}. All in pfennig."},
	{131, 0, "Can I rest in a bed here?", "Lie in a free bed and I'll look in on you now and then. I ask nothing for it, but I won't hurry."},
	{131, 0, "Do you grow your own herbs?", "In the garden behind the infirmary. Feverfew, comfrey, yarrow. The tenants bring more for the kitchen."},
	{131, 0, "What are the hours of prayer?", "Prime at six, Terce at nine, Sext at eleven, Nones at one, Vespers at six, Compline at eight. The bells ring each one."},
	{131, 0, "When is mass said?", "Three times a day in the monastery church, when Father Anselm goes to the altar. You're welcome to hear it."},
	{131, 0, "Can I make my confession to you?", "Confession is for the priest. Father Anselm hears it at the monastery church."},
	{132, 0, "What do you do here?", "I do the cutting. Brother Hugh does the binding and the herbs. A lay brother may cut, where a monk may not."},
	{132, 0, "What does cutting cost?", "A lance is {fee_lance} pfennig, a stitch {fee_stitch}, setting a bone {fee_set}. You pay first, then lie on a free bed."},
	{132, 0, "Can you cut out a splinter or a boil?", "That's what the lance is for. Lie on a free bed and pay first. I'll tell you when it's done."},
	{133, 0, "When is mass said?", "At two in the afternoon, seven and ten at night, Brussels time. Be inside before the Kyrie ends."},
	{133, 0, "What do I get for hearing mass?", "Kneel when the host is raised, stay to the end, and I'll bless you. If you miss part of it, there's no blessing."},
	{133, 0, "Can I be wed here?", "Yes. I read your banns at three masses first. Then come back and choose Marriage and banns on my panel."},
	{133, 0, "Can you baptise my child?", "If your child isn't baptised yet, press Y at the font in the nave and I'll baptise them."},
	{133, 0, "Can I say mass myself?", "Only a priest, prior or abbot may. Come to me at the altar, and my panel offers Say mass now."}
};

